{"parentUuid":"7f2d89da-05a0-4415-afa8-c362abf23cbf","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgY3r6irKALqKcEhKvfPF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011QFm2pdfwBHk2KdP7iSaDk","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"head -40 \"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.5.md\"","description":"Read the previous step note for its format"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":296,"cache_read_input_tokens":200651,"output_tokens":259,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":56},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":296,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":259,"cache_read_input_tokens":200651,"cache_creation_input_tokens":296,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":296},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_011QFm2pdfwBHk2KdP7iSaDk":{"command":"head -40 \"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.5.md\"","description":"Read the previous step note for its format"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"b6082855-ecbe-47ba-a852-423db757c128","apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CgY3r5WAwuzr3RYqrC8yX","requestRef":"cd3c030b-7d4d-4196-8647-9c51c5d43d0f","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"d96027bc-4576-41b6-b6bb-33bf3fbb3708","timestamp":"2026-10-30T07:45:40.874Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-archive","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"d96027bc-4576-41b6-b6bb-33bf3fbb3708","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"f40c2187-e8b4-47d5-9c53-ee1f5c6e6ab4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011QFm2pdfwBHk2KdP7iSaDk","type":"tool_result","content":"# Step 2A.5: DONE\n\nCommit `10435e1` on `phase-2a-archive`, pushed. The PR is held until the 2A gate. Decision 26 records the design and the numbers.\n\n## Crop check: 48 of 50 pass (96%, 95% Wilson interval 86.5–98.9%)\n\n`eval/results/phase2a/crop-check.md` has a verdict and a reason for each pick. The contact sheet is `crop-check-1.jpg` to `crop-check-5.jpg`, and the picks are in `crop-check.json`.\n\n- **Picks:** one per year, from 50 years evenly spread over the 173 years with candidates (1770–1964), drawn at random with a per-year fixed seed. They come from 45 LoC batches, with at most 2 from any one, and have 105 boxes in all.\n- **Pass means:** the crops, one per box in `bbox_list` order, show the article's OCR text from its first line, in reading order, with nothing missing.\n- **Failures:**\n  - **#21, 1862 Daily dispatch:** box 1 is the last line of the item above, a letter's address and signature (\"To Hon. W. H. Seward … Franklin Pierce\"). The article's OCR doesn't contain it, so AmericanStories gave the article a stray box.\n  - **#50, 1964 Arizona tribune:** there is no crop. The scan has no batch path, and loc.gov lists the title (Phoenix, 1958–197?) but has no item for 1964-11-06, so no IIIF service can be named.\n- **Saved by the reissued-batch fallback:** #2 (1796) and #16 (1845) are recorded in `ver01` batches that tile.loc.gov no longer serves (404), and loc.gov lists them under `ver02`. Without the fallback, 4 picks would have failed.\n- **Notes on passes**, all of them with the crop matching the OCR:\n  - Overlapping boxes repeat a line in 7 picks.\n  - Box edges clip a first or last letter as the OCR does in 5.\n  - 3 articles start mid-story, where AmericanStories split them.\n  - In 3, a box runs into the next item.\n  - Box 1 is the paper's own dateline in #15, an ornament in #2, and a sliver of the item above in #33.\n\n## Display-headline precision, judged by hand (`headline-drops*.json`)\n\n- **First rule** (masthead lines dropped anywhere): 142 of 200 correct, **71.0% (64.4–76.8%)**, out of 17,270 drops in candidate headlines. The 58 mis-drops:\n  - about 41 dates or times that belong to the article, such as \"Out Ghosts Next / Monday Evening\", \"TUESDAY, OCTOBER 31 / WILL BE HALLOWEEN\", listing days, and poems titled MARCH and EVENING\n  - 9 lone issue words or series numbers (\"World / Series\", \"CHRISTMAS / NUMBER.\")\n  - 3 times the paper's name or place inside a sentence (\"in Big Stone Gap.\")\n  - 5 figures from tables or ads\n- **The rule change:**\n  - Masthead lines are dropped only while they lead the headline. Rules and stray marks are dropped anywhere.\n  - A weekday or month needs a second date word, a number or the paper's name.\n  - An issue word needs a number.\n- **Final rule:** 10,287 drops. A fresh seeded sample of 200 had 195 correct, **97.5% (94.3–98.9%)**. On the 143 lines with letters it's 139 correct, **97.2% (93.0–98.9%)**.\n  - The 5 mis-drops: a column title \"IN DETROIT\", a story's opening year \"1773.\", an advertisement's own date, an essay series' \"NO. XXXVI.\" and \"SATURDAY [Night Sermons]\".\n  - The cost: masthead blocks whose title line OCR'd garbled now keep their date lines. That's 27 of the 142 correct drops in the first sample.\n- **Short names:** a title or place under 6 letters must stand alone as a word. Real cases:\n  - `PoStry` (Poetry) in The post, and `VIOLA` in The Iola register, were mis-drops before.\n  - The Sun's own masthead `THE SUN, SUNDAY. DECEMBER 9. 1891.` was missed before, because \"sun\" was taken out of SUNDAY.\n  - Star and Bee had no such line in their own papers, so their tests use real lines from other papers (`Stars.`, `BEER.`).\n  - Tests fail if every name is bounded (Dallas, Memphis) or none is (the five short-name cases).","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"cd84d050-8c9d-43af-8fea-9bcac37a4248","timestamp":"2026-10-30T07:45:41.986Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Step 2A.5: DONE\n\nCommit `10435e1` on `phase-2a-archive`, pushed. The PR is held until the 2A gate. Decision 26 records the design and the numbers.\n\n## Crop check: 48 of 50 pass (96%, 95% Wilson interval 86.5–98.9%)\n\n`eval/results/phase2a/crop-check.md` has a verdict and a reason for each pick. The contact sheet is `crop-check-1.jpg` to `crop-check-5.jpg`, and the picks are in `crop-check.json`.\n\n- **Picks:** one per year, from 50 years evenly spread over the 173 years with candidates (1770–1964), drawn at random with a per-year fixed seed. They come from 45 LoC batches, with at most 2 from any one, and have 105 boxes in all.\n- **Pass means:** the crops, one per box in `bbox_list` order, show the article's OCR text from its first line, in reading order, with nothing missing.\n- **Failures:**\n  - **#21, 1862 Daily dispatch:** box 1 is the last line of the item above, a letter's address and signature (\"To Hon. W. H. Seward … Franklin Pierce\"). The article's OCR doesn't contain it, so AmericanStories gave the article a stray box.\n  - **#50, 1964 Arizona tribune:** there is no crop. The scan has no batch path, and loc.gov lists the title (Phoenix, 1958–197?) but has no item for 1964-11-06, so no IIIF service can be named.\n- **Saved by the reissued-batch fallback:** #2 (1796) and #16 (1845) are recorded in `ver01` batches that tile.loc.gov no longer serves (404), and loc.gov lists them under `ver02`. Without the fallback, 4 picks would have failed.\n- **Notes on passes**, all of them with the crop matching the OCR:\n  - Overlapping boxes repeat a line in 7 picks.\n  - Box edges clip a first or last letter as the OCR does in 5.\n  - 3 articles start mid-story, where AmericanStories split them.\n  - In 3, a box runs into the next item.\n  - Box 1 is the paper's own dateline in #15, an ornament in #2, and a sliver of the item above in #33.\n\n## Display-headline precision, judged by hand (`headline-drops*.json`)\n\n- **First rule** (masthead lines dropped anywhere): 142 of 200 correct, **71.0% (64.4–76.8%)**, out of 17,270 drops in candidate headlines. The 58 mis-drops:\n  - about 41 dates or times that belong to the article, such as \"Out Ghosts Next / Monday Evening\", \"TUESDAY, OCTOBER 31 / WILL BE HALLOWEEN\", listing days, and poems titled MARCH and EVENING\n  - 9 lone issue words or series numbers (\"World / Series\", \"CHRISTMAS / NUMBER.\")\n  - 3 times the paper's name or place inside a sentence (\"in Big Stone Gap.\")\n  - 5 figures from tables or ads\n- **The rule change:**\n  - Masthead lines are dropped only while they lead the headline. Rules and stray marks are dropped anywhere.\n  - A weekday or month needs a second date word, a number or the paper's name.\n  - An issue word needs a number.\n- **Final rule:** 10,287 drops. A fresh seeded sample of 200 had 195 correct, **97.5% (94.3–98.9%)**. On the 143 lines with letters it's 139 correct, **97.2% (93.0–98.9%)**.\n  - The 5 mis-drops: a column title \"IN DETROIT\", a story's opening year \"1773.\", an advertisement's own date, an essay series' \"NO. XXXVI.\" and \"SATURDAY [Night Sermons]\".\n  - The cost: masthead blocks whose title line OCR'd garbled now keep their date lines. That's 27 of the 142 correct drops in the first sample.\n- **Short names:** a title or place under 6 letters must stand alone as a word. Real cases:\n  - `PoStry` (Poetry) in The post, and `VIOLA` in The Iola register, were mis-drops before.\n  - The Sun's own masthead `THE SUN, SUNDAY. DECEMBER 9. 1891.` was missed before, because \"sun\" was taken out of SUNDAY.\n  - Star and Bee had no such line in their own papers, so their tests use real lines from other papers (`Stars.`, `BEER.`).\n  - Tests fail if every name is bounded (Dallas, Memphis) or none is (the five short-name cases).","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"b6082855-ecbe-47ba-a852-423db757c128","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","root":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","branch":"phase-2a-archive","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","platform":"macos"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d96027bc-4576-41b6-b6bb-33bf3fbb3708","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-archive","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"cd84d050-8c9d-43af-8fea-9bcac37a4248","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"silent_turn_reminder","text":"The user hasn't heard from you in a while — say in a few words what you're doing, then continue."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"9485bc56-1784-4921-85a3-23b4a733c915","timestamp":"2026-10-30T07:45:41.991Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nThe user hasn't heard from you in a while — say in a few words what you're doing, then continue.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-archive","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","hash":"2e5a3bf98794d5e41a5eadde35d9556894ebf66716db359fac27d7a8dbf1de13","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ced1AbPDLphJSGGXYZx3mv","name":"mcp__ccd_turn__update_plan","input":{"advance":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011QFm2pdfwBHk2KdP7iSaDk","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"head -40 \"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.5.md\"","description":"Read the previous step note for its format"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","hash":"1f4376cd92debf728c8920bb4bbd175662d36c2553fc6af8d1cc488b7d25bcb6","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ced1AbPDLphJSGGXYZx3mv","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Plan updated: step 6 of 6 is in progress."}]},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011QFm2pdfwBHk2KdP7iSaDk","type":"tool_result","content":"# Step 2A.5: DONE\n\nCommit `10435e1` on `phase-2a-archive`, pushed. The PR is held until the 2A gate. Decision 26 records the design and the numbers.\n\n## Crop check: 48 of 50 pass (96%, 95% Wilson interval 86.5–98.9%)\n\n`eval/results/phase2a/crop-check.md` has a verdict and a reason for each pick. The contact sheet is `crop-check-1.jpg` to `crop-check-5.jpg`, and the picks are in `crop-check.json`.\n\n- **Picks:** one per year, from 50 years evenly spread over the 173 years with candidates (1770–1964), drawn at random with a per-year fixed seed. They come from 45 LoC batches, with at most 2 from any one, and have 105 boxes in all.\n- **Pass means:** the crops, one per box in `bbox_list` order, show the article's OCR text from its first line, in reading order, with nothing missing.\n- **Failures:**\n  - **#21, 1862 Daily dispatch:** box 1 is the last line of the item above, a letter's address and signature (\"To Hon. W. H. Seward … Franklin Pierce\"). The article's OCR doesn't contain it, so AmericanStories gave the article a stray box.\n  - **#50, 1964 Arizona tribune:** there is no crop. The scan has no batch path, and loc.gov lists the title (Phoenix, 1958–197?) but has no item for 1964-11-06, so no IIIF service can be named.\n- **Saved by the reissued-batch fallback:** #2 (1796) and #16 (1845) are recorded in `ver01` batches that tile.loc.gov no longer serves (404), and loc.gov lists them under `ver02`. Without the fallback, 4 picks would have failed.\n- **Notes on passes**, all of them with the crop matching the OCR:\n  - Overlapping boxes repeat a line in 7 picks.\n  - Box edges clip a first or last letter as the OCR does in 5.\n  - 3 articles start mid-story, where AmericanStories split them.\n  - In 3, a box runs into the next item.\n  - Box 1 is the paper's own dateline in #15, an ornament in #2, and a sliver of the item above in #33.\n\n## Display-headline precision, judged by hand (`headline-drops*.json`)\n\n- **First rule** (masthead lines dropped anywhere): 142 of 200 correct, **71.0% (64.4–76.8%)**, out of 17,270 drops in candidate headlines. The 58 mis-drops:\n  - about 41 dates or times that belong to the article, such as \"Out Ghosts Next / Monday Evening\", \"TUESDAY, OCTOBER 31 / WILL BE HALLOWEEN\", listing days, and poems titled MARCH and EVENING\n  - 9 lone issue words or series numbers (\"World / Series\", \"CHRISTMAS / NUMBER.\")\n  - 3 times the paper's name or place inside a sentence (\"in Big Stone Gap.\")\n  - 5 figures from tables or ads\n- **The rule change:**\n  - Masthead lines are dropped only while they lead the headline. Rules and stray marks are dropped anywhere.\n  - A weekday or month needs a second date word, a number or the paper's name.\n  - An issue word needs a number.\n- **Final rule:** 10,287 drops. A fresh seeded sample of 200 had 195 correct, **97.5% (94.3–98.9%)**. On the 143 lines with letters it's 139 correct, **97.2% (93.0–98.9%)**.\n  - The 5 mis-drops: a column title \"IN DETROIT\", a story's opening year \"1773.\", an advertisement's own date, an essay series' \"NO. XXXVI.\" and \"SATURDAY [Night Sermons]\".\n  - The cost: masthead blocks whose title line OCR'd garbled now keep their date lines. That's 27 of the 142 correct drops in the first sample.\n- **Short names:** a title or place under 6 letters must stand alone as a word. Real cases:\n  - `PoStry` (Poetry) in The post, and `VIOLA` in The Iola register, were mis-drops before.\n  - The Sun's own masthead `THE SUN, SUNDAY. DECEMBER 9. 1891.` was missed before, because \"sun\" was taken out of SUNDAY.\n  - Star and Bee had no such line in their own papers, so their tests use real lines from other papers (`Stars.`, `BEER.`).\n  - Tests fail if every name is bounded (Dallas, Memphis) or none is (the five short-name cases).","is_error":false}]}}
