{"parentUuid":"d20ce87f-327d-4d89-94af-ccf50c94765a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgY5NtHyFWvYKaNaeLpXA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3631,"cache_read_input_tokens":349433,"output_tokens":1715,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":80},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3631,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1715,"cache_read_input_tokens":349433,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3631,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3631},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":801,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"a7f5c87a-7094-42ed-8070-91c27aa7d26b","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgY5NsLfZ66kBLDhQdhMr","requestRef":"024315f6-8f33-4f84-9395-c588e2dcdd66","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"02ae68c1-a6b5-421f-a2be-a623ea41ecb4","timestamp":"2026-10-30T08:05:44.291Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-archive","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"02ae68c1-a6b5-421f-a2be-a623ea41ecb4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgY5NtHyFWvYKaNaeLpXA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LWadLwAjTXxw8vekA8EsnX","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd \"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts\" && cat >> Docs/DECISIONS.md <<'EOF'\n\n**28. The display headline drops a date only when it is the issue's own, and a number only beside the paper's title or volume.**\n- This follows the EM's review of the last five mis-drops in decision 26's sample: `IN DETROIT`, `1773.`, `April 4th.`, `NO. XXXVI.` and `4k.SATURDAY`.\n- A dateline goes only when it is the issue's own, from the record's edition date:\n  - Each weekday and month on the line must be the issue's.\n  - The issue's weekday then settles it, because OCR misreads digits far more often than words. The Sun's `SUNDAY. DECEMBER 9.` is its masthead of the 6th.\n  - Without a weekday, the day after a month and the year must be the issue's too, with OCR's I, l and O read as digits. So an advertisement's `April 4th.` in the issue of the 11th stays, as does a story's `1773.`.\n- `In` followed by the paper's own place is never a masthead (`IN DETROIT` titles a column).\n- A number line goes only when the leading lines hold the paper's title or its volume. This covers a bare number, and `No.`, `Number`, `Whole No.` or `Page` with a number, because `NO. XXXVI.` on its own numbers an essay.\n- Rerun on the same 200 judged lines (`scripts/headline_drops.py --same`):\n  - It makes none of the 5 mis-drops.\n  - It keeps 37 of the 195 correct drops. That's 22 issue numbers with neither the title nor the volume beside them (`NO. 40`, `NUMBER 81.`), 5 whole numbers, 3 rules with a stray digit, 4 datelines with no weekday whose digits OCR misread (`DELAWARE, OHIO, AUGUST 4, 1866.` for 1865), and 3 datelines whose weekday or month isn't the record's.\n  - On the 158 lines it still drops, its precision is 158 of 158 (95% interval 97.6–100%).\n- Over every candidate headline, it drops 7,509 lines instead of 10,287, and nothing the old rule kept. So the judged lines it still drops remain a fair sample of its drops.\n- The cost is about 1,900 masthead lines left in headlines (37/200 of the old drops). That's 0.2% of candidates, against about 260 headline lines no longer hidden.\n\n**29. A third of candidates have no batch path, but most of their reels do. AmericanStories' stray boxes stay.**\n- 335,907 candidates (32.0%) have a scan with no batch path, so the IIIF service for their page has to come from somewhere other than `raw_data_loc`. The count for each year is in `eval_a.json` (`by_year[].no_batch_path`). By decade the share runs from 15% in the 1820s to 37% in the 1850s, and stays between 24% and 36% from the 1840s on.\n- The fallback (decision 18) asks loc.gov for each page. In the crop check, it named the service for 12 of the 13 picks with no batch path. The 13th was the 1964 Arizona Tribune, which has no loc.gov item.\n- loc.gov's JSON API allows 20 requests a minute and blocks an address for an hour past that ([Working Within Limits](https://www.loc.gov/apis/json-and-yaml/working-within-limits/)). Its image services allow 150 a minute.\n  - A probe of 100 such pages at one request a second was blocked after about 20, on 2026-10-30 at 04:03.\n  - `crops.get` now spaces loc.gov JSON requests 3.5 s apart. At that rate, a lookup for each of the 283,546 pages would take 11.6 days.\n- These candidates sit on 19,115 reels. 92.4% of them are on a reel whose batch another candidate's batch path names, and the service is built from the batch, LCCN, reel, issue and sequence. That leaves 25,595 candidates on 1,292 reels needing a lookup: about 76 minutes at one lookup per reel. 220 reels hold more than one batch, which is the ver01/ver02 reissue of decision 26. A reel-to-batch map isn't built yet. It needs a crop check of its own.\n- **Known limitation, not fixed:** AmericanStories can give an article a box that belongs to the item above it. In the crop check, #21's first box is the last line of the letter above, its address and signature. The crop shows that line and the OCR doesn't.\nEOF\necho ok","description":"Record decisions 28 and 29"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3631,"cache_read_input_tokens":349433,"output_tokens":1715,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":80},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3631,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1715,"cache_read_input_tokens":349433,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3631,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3631},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01LWadLwAjTXxw8vekA8EsnX":{"command":"cd \"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts\" && cat >> Docs/DECISIONS.md <<'EOF'\n\n**28. The display headline drops a date only when it is the issue's own, and a number only beside the paper's title or volume.**\n- This follows the EM's review of the last five mis-drops in decision 26's sample: `IN DETROIT`, `1773.`, `April 4th.`, `NO. XXXVI.` and `4k.SATURDAY`.\n- A dateline goes only when it is the issue's own, from the record's edition date:\n  - Each weekday and month on the line must be the issue's.\n  - The issue's weekday then settles it, because OCR misreads digits far more often than words. The Sun's `SUNDAY. DECEMBER 9.` is its masthead of the 6th.\n  - Without a weekday, the day after a month and the year must be the issue's too, with OCR's I, l and O read as digits. So an advertisement's `April 4th.` in the issue of the 11th stays, as does a story's `1773.`.\n- `In` followed by the paper's own place is never a masthead (`IN DETROIT` titles a column).\n- A number line goes only when the leading lines hold the paper's title or its volume. This covers a bare number, and `No.`, `Number`, `Whole No.` or `Page` with a number, because `NO. XXXVI.` on its own numbers an essay.\n- Rerun on the same 200 judged lines (`scripts/headline_drops.py --same`):\n  - It makes none of the 5 mis-drops.\n  - It keeps 37 of the 195 correct drops. That's 22 issue numbers with neither the title nor the volume beside them (`NO. 40`, `NUMBER 81.`), 5 whole numbers, 3 rules with a stray digit, 4 datelines with no weekday whose digits OCR misread (`DELAWARE, OHIO, AUGUST 4, 1866.` for 1865), and 3 datelines whose weekday or month isn't the record's.\n  - On the 158 lines it still drops, its precision is 158 of 158 (95% interval 97.6–100%).\n- Over every candidate headline, it drops 7,509 lines instead of 10,287, and nothing the old rule kept. So the judged lines it still drops remain a fair sample of its drops.\n- The cost is about 1,900 masthead lines left in headlines (37/200 of the old drops). That's 0.2% of candidates, against about 260 headline lines no longer hidden.\n\n**29. A third of candidates have no batch path, but most of their reels do. AmericanStories' stray boxes stay.**\n- 335,907 candidates (32.0%) have a scan with no batch path, so the IIIF service for their page has to come from somewhere other than `raw_data_loc`. The count for each year is in `eval_a.json` (`by_year[].no_batch_path`). By decade the share runs from 15% in the 1820s to 37% in the 1850s, and stays between 24% and 36% from the 1840s on.\n- The fallback (decision 18) asks loc.gov for each page. In the crop check, it named the service for 12 of the 13 picks with no batch path. The 13th was the 1964 Arizona Tribune, which has no loc.gov item.\n- loc.gov's JSON API allows 20 requests a minute and blocks an address for an hour past that ([Working Within Limits](https://www.loc.gov/apis/json-and-yaml/working-within-limits/)). Its image services allow 150 a minute.\n  - A probe of 100 such pages at one request a second was blocked after about 20, on 2026-10-30 at 04:03.\n  - `crops.get` now spaces loc.gov JSON requests 3.5 s apart. At that rate, a lookup for each of the 283,546 pages would take 11.6 days.\n- These candidates sit on 19,115 reels. 92.4% of them are on a reel whose batch another candidate's batch path names, and the service is built from the batch, LCCN, reel, issue and sequence. That leaves 25,595 candidates on 1,292 reels needing a lookup: about 76 minutes at one lookup per reel. 220 reels hold more than one batch, which is the ver01/ver02 reissue of decision 26. A reel-to-batch map isn't built yet. It needs a crop check of its own.\n- **Known limitation, not fixed:** AmericanStories can give an article a box that belongs to the item above it. In the crop check, #21's first box is the last line of the letter above, its address and signature. The crop shows that line and the OCR doesn't.\nEOF\necho ok","description":"Record decisions 28 and 29"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"a7f5c87a-7094-42ed-8070-91c27aa7d26b","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgY5NsLfZ66kBLDhQdhMr","requestRef":"024315f6-8f33-4f84-9395-c588e2dcdd66","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"b55fd99c-2232-4597-b9fd-31e7eac50e27","timestamp":"2026-10-30T08:05:57.350Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-archive","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
