{"parentUuid":"54e5184e-2adb-42fb-8024-76517b572369","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgYZX8QmTdfhc7xvqaiiE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HtEUct5CRFsTMvoQzasXde","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/DECISIONS.md","offset":150,"limit":90},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1008,"cache_read_input_tokens":170398,"output_tokens":140,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1008,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":140,"cache_read_input_tokens":170398,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1008,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1008},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01HtEUct5CRFsTMvoQzasXde":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/DECISIONS.md","offset":150,"limit":90}},"serverClassifierRequest":"844e97f2-c0ff-4acd-a69f-7b9a6736f574","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgYZX7MX9JHDFPaN8iGnq","requestRef":"17c41175-9694-41a6-94fa-ebe19e5b1398","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"deb30d29-39c3-4d9a-972d-3abf7158bc92","timestamp":"2026-10-30T14:14:47.466Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-archive","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"deb30d29-39c3-4d9a-972d-3abf7158bc92","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"efbe7dae-4a3a-47ef-9f27-91c90fdaed9c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HtEUct5CRFsTMvoQzasXde","type":"tool_result","content":"150\t  - One article's first box, as AmericanStories gives it, is the last line of the item above.\n151\t\n152\t**27. Eval A: 3.5% of candidates are ghost reports, none of 600 articles from outside them is one, and lexicon v1 stays.**\n153\t- `make eval` (`scripts/eval_a.py`) reads the counts, candidates and samples in 41 s, then writes `eval/results/eval_a.json` and eight charts to `eval/results/eval_a/`. Every sample is seeded. A rerun keeps the judgments made by hand in `composition.json`, `recall.json` and `reprint-check.json`.\n154\t- 11.2% of candidates have no state. The median candidate is 358 words long.\n155\t- OCR quality is the share of words of 3+ letters that `/usr/share/dict/words` knows, after line-break hyphens are joined and common endings come off. It is measured on 1 candidate in 20, chosen by the CRC of its id; 51,718 of those have 20 words or more. The median is 92.7%.\n156\t- Reprints:\n157\t  - Each candidate keeps a hash-chosen eighth of the 7-word runs in its first 120 words.\n158\t  - Two candidates are joined when they share a pair among their six smallest runs, so two runs in common. A pair held by more than 1,000 candidates is stock phrasing and joins nothing.\n159\t  - Linking on one shared run chained 6,720 candidates together through quoted Bible verses, and only 15 of 20 sampled pairs were the same story. With two shared runs, 20 of 20 are.\n160\t  - 80,770 clusters hold 31.4% of candidates, which leaves 801,071 distinct stories. 71,191 of the clusters span more than one paper.\n161\t- Composition: 200 candidates drawn uniformly at random and sorted by hand into seven categories.\n162\t  - **Ghost report:** a ghost, haunting, apparition or spirit manifestation, told as something that happened or was claimed to. A ghost scare that turns out to be a trapped man counts. A witch doesn't, and neither does a séance advertised for later or an essay doubting ghosts.\n163\t  - **Fiction:** stories, serials, poems, jokes and allegories.\n164\t  - **Religious:** the Holy Ghost, sermons, hymns, Sunday-school lessons and the Ghost Dance.\n165\t  - **Figurative:** a figure of speech, such as a haunt for a place someone frequents, giving up the ghost, or the spectre of war.\n166\t  - **Name or title:** a person, ship, play, lecture, book or headline, including misread names such as General Gobin read as Goblin.\n167\t  - **Social event:** Halloween parties and pranks, ghost parties and phantom drills.\n168\t  - **Other:** everything else.\n169\t\n170\t  | Category | Count | Share (95% interval) | Candidates implied |\n171\t  |---|---|---|---|\n172\t  | figurative | 79 | 39.5% (33.0–46.4%) | 414,900 |\n173\t  | fiction | 47 | 23.5% (18.2–29.8%) | 246,900 |\n174\t  | name or title | 30 | 15.0% (10.7–20.6%) | 157,600 |\n175\t  | other | 23 | 11.5% (7.8–16.7%) | 120,800 |\n176\t  | religious | 10 | 5.0% (2.7–9.0%) | 52,500 |\n177\t  | ghost report | 7 | 3.5% (1.7–7.0%) | 36,800 (17,900–74,000) |\n178\t  | social event | 4 | 2.0% (0.8–5.0%) | 21,000 |\n179\t- 15 of the 200 are OCR accidents in the source text, such as \"flaunting\" printed as \"haunting\" (6) and a codling moth printed as a goblin moth. The lexicon matches what the OCR says, so only the reader, reading in context, can reject them.\n180\t- Sizes:\n181\t  - The reader's job is 1,050,459 candidates, or 801,071 if it reads one printing per cluster.\n182\t  - Atlas would hold about 22,300 ghost-report stories, counting each candidate as 1/k of a story when its cluster holds k printings. Three of the 7 reports were reprinted (7, 13 and 30 printings), so this is a rough estimate.\n183\t- Recall, from the seeded reservoirs of 2,000 articles per year, with each year weighted by its pool's size:\n184\t  - Near-miss pool: 0 ghost reports in 300, so at most 1.26% (95%), or at most 86,900 of its 6,872,539 articles. The sample's terms are spirit (210), knock (80), supernatural (4), spiritualism (4), rap (3) and seance (3).\n185\t  - All non-candidates: 0 in 300, so at most 1.26%, or at most 4.67 million of 369,263,454. This bound is loose, at 127 times the ghost reports found. Bounding the misses at the 36,800 found would take about 38,600 articles with none among them.\n186\t  - The closest calls were a séance announcement promising that spirits \"will materialize\" and a Kentucky girl who claims to be a witch.\n187\t- No misses means no shared vocabulary to add, so there is no lexicon v2. A re-read would take 118.6 min and 340.4 GB of downloads at four processes (decision 25).\n188\t- Both chart series colors pass the dataviz palette validator, on the light surface and on the dark one.\n189\t\n190\t**28. The display headline drops a date only when it is the issue's own, and a number only beside the paper's title or volume.**\n191\t- This follows the EM's review of the last five mis-drops in decision 26's sample: `IN DETROIT`, `1773.`, `April 4th.`, `NO. XXXVI.` and `4k.SATURDAY`.\n192\t- A dateline goes only when it is the issue's own, from the record's edition date:\n193\t  - Each weekday and month on the line must be the issue's.\n194\t  - The issue's weekday then settles it, because OCR misreads digits far more often than words. The Sun's `SUNDAY. DECEMBER 9.` is its masthead of the 6th.\n195\t  - Without a weekday, the day after a month and the year must be the issue's too, with OCR's I, l and O read as digits. So an advertisement's `April 4th.` in the issue of the 11th stays, as does a story's `1773.`.\n196\t- `In` followed by the paper's own place is never a masthead (`IN DETROIT` titles a column).\n197\t- A number line goes only when the leading lines hold the paper's title or its volume. This covers a bare number, and `No.`, `Number`, `Whole No.` or `Page` with a number, because `NO. XXXVI.` on its own numbers an essay.\n198\t- Rerun on the same 200 judged lines (`scripts/headline_drops.py --same`):\n199\t  - It makes none of the 5 mis-drops.\n200\t  - It keeps 37 of the 195 correct drops:\n201\t    - 22 numbers with neither the title nor the volume beside them (`NO. 40`, `NUMBER 81.`, a bare `2`)\n202\t    - 5 whole numbers\n203\t    - 3 lines of stray marks and digits\n204\t    - 4 datelines with no weekday, whose digits don't match the record's (`DELAWARE, OHIO, AUGUST 4, 1866.` in the issue of 1865)\n205\t    - 3 datelines whose weekday or month isn't the record's\n206\t  - On the 158 lines it still drops, its precision is 158 of 158 (95% interval 97.6–100%).\n207\t- Over every candidate headline, it drops 7,509 lines instead of 10,287, and nothing the old rule kept. So the judged lines it still drops remain a fair sample of its drops.\n208\t- Of the 2,778 lines it no longer drops, split as in the sample (37 correct drops to 5 mis-drops), about 2,450 are masthead lines now left in headlines (0.2% of candidates). About 330 are headline lines no longer hidden.\n209\t\n210\t**29. A third of candidates have no batch path, but most of their reels do. AmericanStories' stray boxes stay.**\n211\t- 335,907 candidates (32.0%) have a scan with no batch path, so the IIIF service for their page has to come from somewhere other than `raw_data_loc`. The count for each year is in `eval_a.json` (`by_year[].no_batch_path`). By decade, the share runs from 15% in the 1820s to 37% in the 1850s. It is between 24% and 37% in every decade from the 1840s on.\n212\t- The fallback (decision 18) asks loc.gov for each page. In the crop check, it named the service for 12 of the 13 picks with no batch path. The 13th was the 1964 Arizona Tribune, which has no loc.gov item.\n213\t- loc.gov's JSON API allows 20 requests a minute and blocks an address for an hour past that ([Working Within Limits](https://www.loc.gov/apis/json-and-yaml/working-within-limits/)). Its image services allow 150 a minute.\n214\t  - A probe of 100 such pages, at one request a second, was answered 429 within its first minute, on 2026-10-30 at 04:03. Its script is deleted.\n215\t  - `crops.get` now spaces loc.gov JSON requests 3.5 s apart. At that rate, a lookup for each of the 283,546 pages would take 11.5 days.\n216\t- These candidates sit on 19,115 reels. 92.4% of them are on a reel whose batch another candidate's batch path names, and the service is built from the batch, LCCN, reel, issue and sequence. That leaves 25,595 candidates on 1,292 reels needing a lookup: about 75 minutes at one lookup per reel.\n217\t  - 220 reels hold more than one batch. For 57 of them that's a batch and its reissue (decision 26). The other 163 are split between batches, so the reel alone doesn't settle which batch a page is in.\n218\t  - A reel-to-batch map isn't built yet. It needs a crop check of its own.\n219\t- **Known limitation, not fixed:** AmericanStories can give an article a box that belongs to the item above it. In the crop check, #21's first box is the last line of the letter above, its address and signature. The crop shows that line and the OCR doesn't.\n220\t"}]},"uuid":"a56e2843-44f8-4ec1-8e58-f4481c815749","timestamp":"2026-10-30T14:14:47.485Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/DECISIONS.md","content":"  - One article's first box, as AmericanStories gives it, is the last line of the item above.\n\n**27. Eval A: 3.5% of candidates are ghost reports, none of 600 articles from outside them is one, and lexicon v1 stays.**\n- `make eval` (`scripts/eval_a.py`) reads the counts, candidates and samples in 41 s, then writes `eval/results/eval_a.json` and eight charts to `eval/results/eval_a/`. Every sample is seeded. A rerun keeps the judgments made by hand in `composition.json`, `recall.json` and `reprint-check.json`.\n- 11.2% of candidates have no state. The median candidate is 358 words long.\n- OCR quality is the share of words of 3+ letters that `/usr/share/dict/words` knows, after line-break hyphens are joined and common endings come off. It is measured on 1 candidate in 20, chosen by the CRC of its id; 51,718 of those have 20 words or more. The median is 92.7%.\n- Reprints:\n  - Each candidate keeps a hash-chosen eighth of the 7-word runs in its first 120 words.\n  - Two candidates are joined when they share a pair among their six smallest runs, so two runs in common. A pair held by more than 1,000 candidates is stock phrasing and joins nothing.\n  - Linking on one shared run chained 6,720 candidates together through quoted Bible verses, and only 15 of 20 sampled pairs were the same story. With two shared runs, 20 of 20 are.\n  - 80,770 clusters hold 31.4% of candidates, which leaves 801,071 distinct stories. 71,191 of the clusters span more than one paper.\n- Composition: 200 candidates drawn uniformly at random and sorted by hand into seven categories.\n  - **Ghost report:** a ghost, haunting, apparition or spirit manifestation, told as something that happened or was claimed to. A ghost scare that turns out to be a trapped man counts. A witch doesn't, and neither does a séance advertised for later or an essay doubting ghosts.\n  - **Fiction:** stories, serials, poems, jokes and allegories.\n  - **Religious:** the Holy Ghost, sermons, hymns, Sunday-school lessons and the Ghost Dance.\n  - **Figurative:** a figure of speech, such as a haunt for a place someone frequents, giving up the ghost, or the spectre of war.\n  - **Name or title:** a person, ship, play, lecture, book or headline, including misread names such as General Gobin read as Goblin.\n  - **Social event:** Halloween parties and pranks, ghost parties and phantom drills.\n  - **Other:** everything else.\n\n  | Category | Count | Share (95% interval) | Candidates implied |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | figurative | 79 | 39.5% (33.0–46.4%) | 414,900 |\n  | fiction | 47 | 23.5% (18.2–29.8%) | 246,900 |\n  | name or title | 30 | 15.0% (10.7–20.6%) | 157,600 |\n  | other | 23 | 11.5% (7.8–16.7%) | 120,800 |\n  | religious | 10 | 5.0% (2.7–9.0%) | 52,500 |\n  | ghost report | 7 | 3.5% (1.7–7.0%) | 36,800 (17,900–74,000) |\n  | social event | 4 | 2.0% (0.8–5.0%) | 21,000 |\n- 15 of the 200 are OCR accidents in the source text, such as \"flaunting\" printed as \"haunting\" (6) and a codling moth printed as a goblin moth. The lexicon matches what the OCR says, so only the reader, reading in context, can reject them.\n- Sizes:\n  - The reader's job is 1,050,459 candidates, or 801,071 if it reads one printing per cluster.\n  - Atlas would hold about 22,300 ghost-report stories, counting each candidate as 1/k of a story when its cluster holds k printings. Three of the 7 reports were reprinted (7, 13 and 30 printings), so this is a rough estimate.\n- Recall, from the seeded reservoirs of 2,000 articles per year, with each year weighted by its pool's size:\n  - Near-miss pool: 0 ghost reports in 300, so at most 1.26% (95%), or at most 86,900 of its 6,872,539 articles. The sample's terms are spirit (210), knock (80), supernatural (4), spiritualism (4), rap (3) and seance (3).\n  - All non-candidates: 0 in 300, so at most 1.26%, or at most 4.67 million of 369,263,454. This bound is loose, at 127 times the ghost reports found. Bounding the misses at the 36,800 found would take about 38,600 articles with none among them.\n  - The closest calls were a séance announcement promising that spirits \"will materialize\" and a Kentucky girl who claims to be a witch.\n- No misses means no shared vocabulary to add, so there is no lexicon v2. A re-read would take 118.6 min and 340.4 GB of downloads at four processes (decision 25).\n- Both chart series colors pass the dataviz palette validator, on the light surface and on the dark one.\n\n**28. The display headline drops a date only when it is the issue's own, and a number only beside the paper's title or volume.**\n- This follows the EM's review of the last five mis-drops in decision 26's sample: `IN DETROIT`, `1773.`, `April 4th.`, `NO. XXXVI.` and `4k.SATURDAY`.\n- A dateline goes only when it is the issue's own, from the record's edition date:\n  - Each weekday and month on the line must be the issue's.\n  - The issue's weekday then settles it, because OCR misreads digits far more often than words. The Sun's `SUNDAY. DECEMBER 9.` is its masthead of the 6th.\n  - Without a weekday, the day after a month and the year must be the issue's too, with OCR's I, l and O read as digits. So an advertisement's `April 4th.` in the issue of the 11th stays, as does a story's `1773.`.\n- `In` followed by the paper's own place is never a masthead (`IN DETROIT` titles a column).\n- A number line goes only when the leading lines hold the paper's title or its volume. This covers a bare number, and `No.`, `Number`, `Whole No.` or `Page` with a number, because `NO. XXXVI.` on its own numbers an essay.\n- Rerun on the same 200 judged lines (`scripts/headline_drops.py --same`):\n  - It makes none of the 5 mis-drops.\n  - It keeps 37 of the 195 correct drops:\n    - 22 numbers with neither the title nor the volume beside them (`NO. 40`, `NUMBER 81.`, a bare `2`)\n    - 5 whole numbers\n    - 3 lines of stray marks and digits\n    - 4 datelines with no weekday, whose digits don't match the record's (`DELAWARE, OHIO, AUGUST 4, 1866.` in the issue of 1865)\n    - 3 datelines whose weekday or month isn't the record's\n  - On the 158 lines it still drops, its precision is 158 of 158 (95% interval 97.6–100%).\n- Over every candidate headline, it drops 7,509 lines instead of 10,287, and nothing the old rule kept. So the judged lines it still drops remain a fair sample of its drops.\n- Of the 2,778 lines it no longer drops, split as in the sample (37 correct drops to 5 mis-drops), about 2,450 are masthead lines now left in headlines (0.2% of candidates). About 330 are headline lines no longer hidden.\n\n**29. A third of candidates have no batch path, but most of their reels do. AmericanStories' stray boxes stay.**\n- 335,907 candidates (32.0%) have a scan with no batch path, so the IIIF service for their page has to come from somewhere other than `raw_data_loc`. The count for each year is in `eval_a.json` (`by_year[].no_batch_path`). By decade, the share runs from 15% in the 1820s to 37% in the 1850s. It is between 24% and 37% in every decade from the 1840s on.\n- The fallback (decision 18) asks loc.gov for each page. In the crop check, it named the service for 12 of the 13 picks with no batch path. The 13th was the 1964 Arizona Tribune, which has no loc.gov item.\n- loc.gov's JSON API allows 20 requests a minute and blocks an address for an hour past that ([Working Within Limits](https://www.loc.gov/apis/json-and-yaml/working-within-limits/)). Its image services allow 150 a minute.\n  - A probe of 100 such pages, at one request a second, was answered 429 within its first minute, on 2026-10-30 at 04:03. Its script is deleted.\n  - `crops.get` now spaces loc.gov JSON requests 3.5 s apart. At that rate, a lookup for each of the 283,546 pages would take 11.5 days.\n- These candidates sit on 19,115 reels. 92.4% of them are on a reel whose batch another candidate's batch path names, and the service is built from the batch, LCCN, reel, issue and sequence. That leaves 25,595 candidates on 1,292 reels needing a lookup: about 75 minutes at one lookup per reel.\n  - 220 reels hold more than one batch. For 57 of them that's a batch and its reissue (decision 26). The other 163 are split between batches, so the reel alone doesn't settle which batch a page is in.\n  - A reel-to-batch map isn't built yet. It needs a crop check of its own.\n- **Known limitation, not fixed:** AmericanStories can give an article a box that belongs to the item above it. In the crop check, #21's first box is the last line of the letter above, its address and signature. The crop shows that line and the OCR doesn't.\n","numLines":71,"startLine":150,"totalLines":220}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"deb30d29-39c3-4d9a-972d-3abf7158bc92","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-archive","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
