{"parentUuid":"25c9407c-f890-4956-87b5-de10416c80dc","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgYZf3LtDwGp11Fv9qSQ8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UpcmngvPgbguRm8gP7hJ3k","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Judge screen batch 1","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"You are judging old newspaper articles for an evaluation of a ghost-report search over the Library of Congress's digitized American newspapers (1770s to 1960s). Every article in your batch was drawn at random from the articles the search did NOT pick up: none contains its ghost words (ghost, apparition, spectre, phantom, haunt, spook, wraith, poltergeist, banshee, goblin, knockings, rappings, and their OCR misspellings). Each was kept for reading only because it holds a flag word such as spirit, midnight, knock, mysterious, witch, \"in white\", corpse, superstition or uncanny. For each article, decide one thing: is it a ghost report?\n\nGHOST REPORT, the only positive: a ghost, haunting, apparition or spirit manifestation, told as something that happened or was claimed to. A ghost scare that turns out to be a trapped man counts. These all count when told as real or as claimed, in news, a letter, court testimony or an anecdote told as true: spirit-rapping, table-tipping, materializations, a spirit speaking through a medium, spirit photographs, a house where noises or lights are taken for the dead, a sighting of a dead person, a place people say the dead walk.\n\nNOT a report:\n- fiction: stories, serials, poems, jokes and allegories, even ones about ghosts\n- a witch or witchcraft with no ghost; a séance or spiritualist lecture only advertised for later; an essay or sermon doubting ghosts or discussing superstition in general\n- spirit as mood, courage, liquor or the Holy Spirit; \"the spirit of\" anything; knocks on doors, in fights or in baseball; midnight, corpses and graveyards in ordinary news such as a murder, a funeral or a burial\n- religious texts: sermons, hymns, Bible lessons\n\nMark CLOSE when:\n- it might be a report on a fuller reading, for example a window hints at spirits or strange noises but is cut off\n- it tells of a hallucination, a delusion, an insane person's visions, or a dream or premonition of the dead (the project hasn't settled these yet)\n- you are unsure for any other reason\n\nThe text is OCR of old newspapers and is often garbled; read through the noise. Each article is a block:\n\n=== <id> | <date> | <paper> | <length> | flags: <flag words found>\nHEADLINE: ...\nOPENING: the first ~420 characters of the article\nWINDOW: ~300 characters around a flag word (up to 6 windows)\n\nYour batch is /private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/screen/batch-1.txt, with 161 articles (ids 1-001 to 1-161). Read every block yourself. The file is about 150,000 characters, so read it in parts with the Read tool's offset and limit, for example 300 lines at a time, until you reach the end.\n\nWrite your verdicts to /private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/screen/verdicts-1.json as a JSON list with one object per article, in file order:\n{\"id\": \"1-001\", \"verdict\": \"no\", \"note\": \"\"}\nverdict is \"no\", \"close\" or \"report\". The note is required for close and report: one sentence on what the article says happened and why it is or might be a report. For \"no\", leave it empty, or give a few words if it was near.\n\nUse only the batch file. Don't read other files, search the web, or write code that decides verdicts (keyword rules would defeat the purpose). You may run one short script at the end only to check that your output is valid JSON with exactly one verdict for every id.\n\nReply with the count of each verdict and every id you marked close or report, with its note. Nothing else."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1083,"cache_read_input_tokens":192700,"output_tokens":13241,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1828},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1083,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":13241,"cache_read_input_tokens":192700,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1083,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1083},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01UpcmngvPgbguRm8gP7hJ3k":{"description":"Judge screen batch 1","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"You are judging old newspaper articles for an evaluation of a ghost-report search over the Library of Congress's digitized American newspapers (1770s to 1960s). Every article in your batch was drawn at random from the articles the search did NOT pick up: none contains its ghost words (ghost, apparition, spectre, phantom, haunt, spook, wraith, poltergeist, banshee, goblin, knockings, rappings, and their OCR misspellings). Each was kept for reading only because it holds a flag word such as spirit, midnight, knock, mysterious, witch, \"in white\", corpse, superstition or uncanny. For each article, decide one thing: is it a ghost report?\n\nGHOST REPORT, the only positive: a ghost, haunting, apparition or spirit manifestation, told as something that happened or was claimed to. A ghost scare that turns out to be a trapped man counts. These all count when told as real or as claimed, in news, a letter, court testimony or an anecdote told as true: spirit-rapping, table-tipping, materializations, a spirit speaking through a medium, spirit photographs, a house where noises or lights are taken for the dead, a sighting of a dead person, a place people say the dead walk.\n\nNOT a report:\n- fiction: stories, serials, poems, jokes and allegories, even ones about ghosts\n- a witch or witchcraft with no ghost; a séance or spiritualist lecture only advertised for later; an essay or sermon doubting ghosts or discussing superstition in general\n- spirit as mood, courage, liquor or the Holy Spirit; \"the spirit of\" anything; knocks on doors, in fights or in baseball; midnight, corpses and graveyards in ordinary news such as a murder, a funeral or a burial\n- religious texts: sermons, hymns, Bible lessons\n\nMark CLOSE when:\n- it might be a report on a fuller reading, for example a window hints at spirits or strange noises but is cut off\n- it tells of a hallucination, a delusion, an insane person's visions, or a dream or premonition of the dead (the project hasn't settled these yet)\n- you are unsure for any other reason\n\nThe text is OCR of old newspapers and is often garbled; read through the noise. Each article is a block:\n\n=== <id> | <date> | <paper> | <length> | flags: <flag words found>\nHEADLINE: ...\nOPENING: the first ~420 characters of the article\nWINDOW: ~300 characters around a flag word (up to 6 windows)\n\nYour batch is /private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/screen/batch-1.txt, with 161 articles (ids 1-001 to 1-161). Read every block yourself. The file is about 150,000 characters, so read it in parts with the Read tool's offset and limit, for example 300 lines at a time, until you reach the end.\n\nWrite your verdicts to /private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/screen/verdicts-1.json as a JSON list with one object per article, in file order:\n{\"id\": \"1-001\", \"verdict\":