{"parentUuid":"a460bed0-307a-45b8-8223-9f5f9bd5aad0","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"efbe7dae-4a3a-47ef-9f27-91c90fdaed9c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NjYP2fQFhxx6rP8J5vFfqj","type":"tool_result","content":"**25. The full read ran four years at a time, each on one CPU-bound core. A re-read can set its concurrency from these numbers.**\n- All 180 year files, 340.4 GB at revision `77e27fa6`, took 118.6 min on 2026-10-30: an aggregate 47.8 MB/s. Every manifest's `bytes_read` equals the listed size, no year failed, and no partial file was left.\n- 370,313,913 articles gave 1,050,459 candidates. The outputs take 1.94 GB (candidates 1.34 GB, samples 0.59 GB), and the highest peak memory was 535 MB (1910).\n- Each year process is CPU-bound on one core: 12.8 MB/s at the median over the 87 years above 1 GB, with a range of 11.0–15.6. With four running, the network carried 55 MB/s at the median of minute samples (50–57 for the middle 80%), and 43% of the CPU stayed idle (35–45%). Alone, a year runs nearly twice as fast: 1850 took 46.8 s by itself and 85.7 s beside three others.\n- Concurrency stayed at four:\n  - Time isn't the constraint.\n  - One clean run gives one wall time and clean kill-test evidence.\n  - Eight CPU-bound processes would saturate the owner's laptop while they use it, for well short of 2×, because 6 of its 10 cores are efficiency cores.\n- Six years ran twice:\n  - The streams for 1872 and 1889 ended partway through a page (`tarfile.ReadError: unexpected end of data`).\n  - Kill test 1's SIGKILL cost 1881–1884 their first attempts (`eval/results/phase2a/kill-test-1.md`).\n  - Each retry read its whole file again, because a gzip stream can't seek.\n- 1850 reproduces. With its candidates file moved aside, a rerun wrote all three outputs byte-identical, with the same sha256 as the committed manifest.\n\n/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.5.md:5:## Crop check: 48 of 50 pass (96%, 95% Wilson interval 86.5–98.9%)\n/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.5.md:11:- **Failures:**\n/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.5.md:54:All were between 05:29 and 05:49 UTC: pytest runs of about 12 s each, five scans of candidate headlines at nice 10, the skeleton reload, the web check and two single-crop fetches. Over those minutes the read held 51–56 MB/s with all four year processes busy. The crop check and both precision samples ran after the read had finished.\n/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.4.md:19:- 180 of 180 years done, with 0 failures and 0 `.partial` files left. Every manifest's `bytes_read` equals the listed size.\n/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.4.md:20:- Wall time 118.6 min (04:43:16 to 06:41:54 UTC), 340.4 GB, 47.8 MB/s aggregate. The ~2 min of the kill are included.\n/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.4.md:21:- Totals: 370,313,913 articles and 1,050,459 candidates. Outputs are 1.94 GB: candidates 1,342.7 MB, samples 592.5 MB, counts 0.5 MB.\n/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.4.md:23:- Disk: `data/candidates` 1.3 GB, `data/samples` 565 MB, counts 956 KB, manifests 720 KB, Temporal DB 2 MB. 39 GiB free of 460 GiB.\n/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.4.md:28:  - Per process: 12.8 MB/s median over the 87 years above 1 GB (11.0–15.6). Alone, a year runs nearly 2× faster: 1850 took 46.8 s by itself and 85.7 s with three others.\ncrop-check-1.jpg\ncrop-check-2.jpg\ncrop-check-3.jpg\ncrop-check-4.jpg\ncrop-check-5.jpg\ncrop-check.json\ncrop-check.md\nheadline-drops-first-rule.json\nheadline-drops.json\nkill-test-1-history.png\nkill-test-1-pending.png\nkill-test-1-retry.png\nkill-test-1.md\nskeleton-per-box.png","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"3fe3e343-1a49-4357-a28b-b027c9a53452","timestamp":"2026-10-30T14:19:23.919Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"**25. The full read ran four years at a time, each on one CPU-bound core. A re-read can set its concurrency from these numbers.**\n- All 180 year files, 340.4 GB at revision `77e27fa6`, took 118.6 min on 2026-10-30: an aggregate 47.8 MB/s. Every manifest's `bytes_read` equals the listed size, no year failed, and no partial file was left.\n- 370,313,913 articles gave 1,050,459 candidates. The outputs take 1.94 GB (candidates 1.34 GB, samples 0.59 GB), and the highest peak memory was 535 MB (1910).\n- Each year process is CPU-bound on one core: 12.8 MB/s at the median over the 87 years above 1 GB, with a range of 11.0–15.6. With four running, the network carried 55 MB/s at the median of minute samples (50–57 for the middle 80%), and 43% of the CPU stayed idle (35–45%). Alone, a year runs nearly twice as fast: 1850 took 46.8 s by itself and 85.7 s beside three others.\n- Concurrency stayed at four:\n  - Time isn't the constraint.\n  - One clean run gives one wall time and clean kill-test evidence.\n  - Eight CPU-bound processes would saturate the owner's laptop while they use it, for well short of 2×, because 6 of its 10 cores are efficiency cores.\n- Six years ran twice:\n  - The streams for 1872 and 1889 ended partway through a page (`tarfile.ReadError: unexpected end of data`).\n  - Kill test 1's SIGKILL cost 1881–1884 their first attempts (`eval/results/phase2a/kill-test-1.md`).\n  - Each retry read its whole file again, because a gzip stream can't seek.\n- 1850 reproduces. With its candidates file moved aside, a rerun wrote all three outputs byte-identical, with the same sha256 as the committed manifest.\n\n/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.5.md:5:## Crop check: 48 of 50 pass (96%, 95% Wilson interval 86.5–98.9%)\n/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.5.md:11:- **Failures:**\n/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.5.md:54:All were between 05:29 and 05:49 UTC: pytest runs of about 12 s each, five scans of candidate headlines at nice 10, the skeleton reload, the web check and two single-crop fetches. Over those minutes the read held 51–56 MB/s with all four year processes busy. The crop check and both precision samples ran after the read had finished.\n/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.4.md:19:- 180 of 180 years done, with 0 failures and 0 `.partial` files left. Every manifest's `bytes_read` equals the listed size.\n/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.4.md:20:- Wall time 118.6 min (04:43:16 to 06:41:54 UTC), 340.4 GB, 47.8 MB/s aggregate. The ~2 min of the kill are included.\n/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.4.md:21:- Totals: 370,313,913 articles and 1,050,459 candidates. Outputs are 1.94 GB: candidates 1,342.7 MB, samples 592.5 MB, counts 0.5 MB.\n/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.4.md:23:- Disk: `data/candidates` 1.3 GB, `data/samples` 565 MB, counts 956 KB, manifests 720 KB, Temporal DB 2 MB. 39 GiB free of 460 GiB.\n/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192