touched her deeply. To think that she had been upon the point of yielding to Pennington Brush's repeated plead ings the very hour she had received the intelligence of Brewster's death and his belated remembrance of her self.\n10\tWINDOW: e. He hurried out of doors and sped swiftly up the path towards Mrs. Brewster's. He stepped lightly upon the porch, determined not to brighten her by knocking, if it had been but a trick of his mag nation, she asleep and everything quiet within. He peeped cautiously through the parlor window, and th\n11\t\n12\t=== c-003 | 1918-11-05 | Daily capital journal. (Salem, Oregon) 1903-1919 | 205 words | flags: spirits\n13\tHEADLINE: The Wife /  /  / BY JANE PHELPS /  / ALTHOUGH RUTH HAS GUESTS /  /  / BRIAN GOES TO SCHOOL\" 80\n14\tOPENING: But Ruth's gay spirits soon made him forget Mollie and his own passive deception. She told him as much of her business matters as she thought would interest him, then of her trip down on the train, repeating little incidents in so entertaining a manner that the time passed quickly. Passed, too, with out as much as n thought straying Mollie.ward. The tension so often present, was not felt between them. In fact it\n15\tWINDOW: But Ruth's gay spirits soon made him forget Mollie and his own passive deception. She told him as much of her business matters as she thought would interest him, then of her trip down on the train, repeating little incidents in so entertaining a manner that the time passed quickly. Passed, too, w\n16\t\n17\t=== c-004 | 1915-05-06 | The Republican journal. | 572 words | flags: spirit\n18\tHEADLINE: NO MONOTONY IN HER VERSES\n19\tOPENING: crossed the narrow sea NO MONOTONY IN HER VERSES \"All her hymns may be said to be variations of this same great theme, and yet there is no monotony in her verses, and, less than almost any other author, has she repeated herself. This, it is said, is largely due to her wonderful memory, not only for the writings of others, but for hymns that she herself had written, Years and years after they had been pen ned and p\n20\tWINDOW: misery! Fanny Crosby never INDUCED in any such morbid self pity, and though she had abundant reason for considering her lot hard one, her optimistic spirit turn ed it's darkness into light and its discomfort into pure joy. ''This wonderful and beautiful spirit of religious optimism, which shines\n21\tWINDOW: nsidering her lot hard one, her optimistic spirit turn ed it's darkness into light and its discomfort into pure joy. ''This wonderful and beautiful spirit of religious optimism, which shines forth in her poems and hymns, was doubtless what commended her to all sorts and conditions of men. There ar\n22\tWINDOW: t all these statesmen and musicians, poets, evangelists and reformers were among her friends. Only woman of rare intellect and more rare ly beautiful spirit could have attracted people of such diverse interests and character. ''Withal she maintained her beautiful simplicity of character. Her succe\n23\t\n24\t=== c-005 | 1905-02-15 | The Forest Republican. [volume] (Tionesta, Pa.) 1869-1952 | 603 words | flags: spirit\n25\tHEADLINE: \n26\tOPENING: -0il market closed at $l.39. oil and gas leases at this Office. New lettuce at the White Star Grocery. Did your valentine please you or hurt you? -Washington's birthday one week from to-day. Clearance sale prices make busy times at Hopkins store. 1t The fellow who predicted an open winter has long since been showed under. -WANTED.-Live black squirrels. II. THos. G. LYON, Butler, Pa. That February cl\n27\tWINDOW: toward pneumonia. Our people should have care during these sudden and violent weather changes. The Clarion State Normal is noted for the excellent spirit that prevails among the young people who attend there. No institution affords better opportunity for young people to make the most of themselve\n28\t\n29\t=== c-006 | 1916-04-21 | Bisbee daily review. | 621 words | flags: spirit\n30\tHEADLINE: Such A Contingency might Result I