{"parentUuid":"2f1a9c10-fdb7-4e5f-aec6-8163c14715c5","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"400ad373-d924-47cf-8e2d-d61155c6ff44","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\n<tools-used-before-summary>Agent, Bash, Edit, Read, SendMessage, Skill, TaskStop, ToolSearch, Write, mcp__Claude_Browser__browser_batch, mcp__Claude_Browser__computer, mcp__Claude_Browser__find, mcp__Claude_Browser__get_page_text, mcp__Claude_Browser__javascript_tool, mcp__Claude_Browser__navigate, mcp__ccd_pr__get_status, mcp__ccd_turn__update_plan, mcp__ccd_turn__wrap_up</tools-used-before-summary>\nSummary:\n1. **Primary Request and Intent**\n\n   **Setup and standing rules.** This is a child session of the EM session `local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e` (\"Paperghosts Hacktoberfest week 4 kickoff\").\n   - The project is Paper Ghosts, in the private repo `Hotragn/Paperghosts` at `/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts`.\n   - Hand-over notes go to `/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-X.md`, because only about 400 characters are relayed to the EM.\n   - Standing rules, still in force:\n     - \"never print, log or echo secrets from `.env`, and check keys by shape only.\"\n     - \"No global installs and no Homebrew.\"\n     - \"Stop and ask before anything public (repo visibility, deploys, DNS, posting) or any spending beyond free tiers and existing credits.\"\n     - \"Make small commits on phase branches and open pull requests into `main`; I merge them.\"\n     - \"Record numbered decisions in `Docs/DECISIONS.md`, and end each milestone with a STATUS block in `Docs/RESUME.md`.\"\n     - \"Entire capture is enabled through `.claude/settings.json`; don't touch it.\"\n     - \"Don't run brainstorming Q&A with the owner; put questions in your reply to me.\"\n     - \"Start every reply to me with `<step>: DONE` or `<step>: BLOCKED — reason`, then 150 words at most.\"\n     - Scope `pgrep` and `ps` to my uid, and never print the hostname. Filter push output with `grep -v -i -F \"$(hostname -s)\"`.\n     - Before every commit, scan the staged diff with scratch `scan_staged.py` (`.env` names only, never values) and count machine-name matches.\n     - loc.gov's JSON API allows 20 requests a minute and blocks for an hour past that. Don't send it requests carelessly.\n     - Commit attribution: end messages with `Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>`. End PR bodies with `🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)`.\n\n   **Step 2A.7: DONE.** PR #2 was opened and has since been merged by the EM as `5ac2e1b`.\n\n   **Step 2A.8: IN PROGRESS.** Lexicon v2 on the new branch `phase-2a-lexicon-v2` from `main`. The seven conditions are quoted verbatim in section 6.\n\n2. **Key Technical Concepts**\n\n   **Tooling and stats**\n   - uv with Python 3.12, ruff (`make lint`) and pytest (`make test`).\n   - Wilson 95% intervals. For 0 of n, the upper bound is z²/(n+z²).\n   - Seeded draws:\n     - `draw()`: years weighted by pool size, multinomial per-year counts. It now asserts on a short reservoir.\n     - `uniform()`.\n     - `keep_judgments` merges hand verdicts by key.\n\n   **The read (Temporal)**\n   - Dev server: `make temporal`, with `~/.homebrew/bin/temporal` (pre-existing).\n   - Worker: `make worker`.\n   - `make ingest` with no years runs all 180, 4 at a time, and each year runs in a fresh process.\n   - The cache key is the dataset revision, lexicon version and code version.\n   - `code_version()` hashes `lexicon.py`, `prefilter.py`, `stream.py` and `year.py`.\n   - Manifests and counts record `lexicon_version`; manifests also record `code_version`.\n\n   **Lexicon**\n   - v1 has 12 ghost terms.\n   - v2 adds the spiritualist rules `spiritualism`, `seance`, `spirit_phrase` and `ouija`, plus the co-occurrence rule `spirit+medium` (`TOGETHER`).\n   - The precheck folds text (`FOLD = str.maketrans(\"qbc0fi1tu\", \"ghoosllln\")`) and looks for stems.\n   - Each candidate's `terms` dict records which rules matched, so a rule can be dropped by filtering.\n\n   **Eval structure**\n   - v1 record: `eval/results/eval_a.json` and `eval/results/eval_a/`, reproduced by `make eval-v1` from `data/archive/lexicon-v1`.\n   - v2 EDA: `eval/results/eval_a_v2.json` and `eval/results/eval_a_v2/`.\n   - v2 against v1: `eval/results/eval_a_v2/lexicon_v2.json` and `added.json`.\n\n   **The 2A.7 judging protocol**\n   - Reading agents judge from the opening and up to 6 windows.\n   - I re-judge a seeded 10% of their negatives from the same text.\n   - I re-read every positive and close call in full.\n\n   **In-sample recall.** v1's screen of 38,900 articles is read again with v2. Recall = (R1 + G) / (R1 + G + M2), where:\n   - R1: reports in v1's candidates\n   - G: reports gained among the added candidates\n   - M2: reports still missed\n\n3. **Files and Code Sections**\n\n   **`paperghosts/ingest/lexicon.py`** (committed in `72ef622`)\n   - `VERSION = \"v2\"`, and the docstring notes v2.\n   - New `GHOST` entries, placed after v1's so that v1 wins at the same position:\n     ```python\n     \"spiritualism\": r\"[sf]piritual(?:is[mt]s?|istic)\",\n     \"seance\": r\"s[eé]ances?\",\n     \"spirit_phrase\": (\n         r\"[sf]pirits?[\\s-]+(?:world|land|control|communications?|manifestations?|messages?\"\n         r\"|photographs?|circles?|mediums?)\"\n         r\"|control(?:led)?\\s+(?:of|by)\\s+(?:the\\s+)?[sf]pirits?|disembodied\"\n         r\"|[sf]pirits?\\s+of\\s+the\\s+(?:dead|departed)\"\n     ),\n     \"ouija\": r\"ouija|planchette\",\n     ```\n   - The co-occurrence rule:\n     ```python\n     TOGETHER = {\"spirit+medium\": (r\"[sf]pirits?\", r\"mediums?\")}\n     ```\n   - `_matcher` was renamed to the public `matcher(terms)`. `_TOGETHER` holds the compiled `\\b(?:word)\\b` patterns.\n   - Stems added: `\"spiritualism\": (\"splrlln\",)`, `\"seance\": (\"seano\",\"séano\")`, `\"spirit_phrase\": (\"splrl\",\"dlsemho\")`, `\"ouija\": (\"onlja\",\"planohe\")`, `\"spirit+medium\": (\"splrl\",)`.\n   - `_find(matcher, stems, text, precheck, together=None)` adds a term when every one of its words is found:\n     ```python\n     for term, words in (together or {}).items():\n         forms = [{match.group().lower() for match in word.finditer(text)} for word in words]\n         if all(forms):\n             found[term] = set().union(*forms)\n     ```\n   - `ghost_terms` passes `_TOGETHER`.\n   - `NEAR_MISS` is unchanged. Its spiritualism and seance terms now fire only inside candidates.\n\n   **`tests/test_lexicon.py`** (committed)\n   - The stems test is now `GHOST_STEMS.keys() == GHOST.keys() | TOGETHER.keys()`.\n   - New ordinary-word cases: \"a medium of exchange\", \"the spirit of the age\", \"the plans failed to materialize\".\n   - `test_spiritualist_terms`, 9 cases, each also checked with the precheck off.\n   - `test_spirit_and_medium_need_only_share_the_article` uses the Curran text and expects `{\"spirit+medium\": [\"medium\", \"spirit\"]}`.\n\n   **`scripts/eval_a.py`** (uncommitted)\n   - `main(data=DATA)`; `__main__` calls `main(Path(sys.argv[1]) if sys.argv[1:] else DATA)`.\n   - The `OUT` constant and the `code_version` import were removed; `import sys` was added.\n   - Inside `main()`:\n     - `(version,) = {counts[y][\"lexicon_version\"]...}` and `(code,) = {manifest code_version...}` require one version each.\n     - `judged = version == \"v1\"`, `name = \"eval_a\" if judged else f\"eval_a_{version}\"`, `out = RESULTS / name`.\n     - `read_terms` lists the lexicon's terms in order (`GHOST` then `TOGETHER`), keeping those present in the counts.\n     - The draws run only when judged: `uniform` for composition, `draw` for near-miss, and `rand, held, screen = random_picks(others)`.\n     - The reprint check samples 0 when not judged.\n     - `keep_judgments`, `report[\"reprints\"][\"check\"]`, `composition`, `recall`, `screen` and `largest_year` are added after the report dict only when judged, which keeps v1's key order.\n     - The report records `lexicon_version: version` and `code_version: code`.\n     - It writes `RESULTS / f\"{name}.json\"`, and `headline_numbers` guards a missing screen section.\n   - `year_pass(job)` unpacks `data, year, wanted, near, rand, screen, terms = job`, masks with `mask = lexicon.matcher({term: lexicon.GHOST[term] for term in terms})`, and reads `data / \"candidates\"` and `data / \"samples\"`. Jobs are sorted by `-counts[job[1]][\"articles\"]`.\n   - The new `random_picks(others)`:\n     ```python\n     def random_picks(others):\n         reservoirs = {y: range(min(SAMPLE_SIZE, n)) for y, n in others.items()}\n         rand, screen = defaultdict(set), defaultdict(set)\n         for year, index in draw(reservoirs, others, RECALL, \"eval A recall random\"):\n             rand[year].add(index)\n         held = {y: [i for i in reservoirs[y] if i not in rand[y]] for y in reservoirs}\n         for year, index in draw(held, others, SCREEN, \"eval A screen\"):\n             screen[year].add(index)\n         return rand, held, screen\n     ```\n   - From 2A.7, already committed: `SCREEN`, `flagged()`, `for_reading()`, `summarise_screen()` (including `recall` and `recall_at_least`), the dictionary sha256 and word count, and the `draw()` assert.\n\n   **`scripts/charts.py`** (uncommitted)\n   - Uses `report.get(\"composition\", {}).get(\"categories\")`, `report.get(\"recall\", {}).get(\"screen\", {})` and `recall = report.get(\"recall\", {}) | {...}`, so a v2 report draws only the EDA charts.\n   - `pct()` and the screen subtitle were committed in 2A.7.\n\n   **`scripts/lexicon_v2.py`** (new, uncommitted)\n   - Constants: `V1 = DATA/\"archive\"/\"lexicon-v1\"`, `OUT = RESULTS/\"eval_a_v2\"`, `ADDED = 200`.\n   - Functions:\n     - `compare(v1, v2, new)` returns `missing`, `added` and `old_terms`.\n     - `summarise_rules(sample, added)` gives per rule `{\"added\", \"n\", \"no_report\"}`, plus `rate()` fields when the rule has judged items.\n     - `recall_in_sample(v1, gained, missed)` returns `point` and `at_least` (low, point, high).\n     - Year workers: `year_compare(job)`, `year_items(job)` (opening plus up to 6 windows around the rule's forms, and the judgment fields) and `year_rescreen(job)` (applies v2 `ghost_terms` to v1's screened random articles).\n     - `counts_of(data)`.\n   - `main()` does the following:\n     - compares v1 and v2 per year\n     - counts `holding` and `alone` per rule\n     - draws the seed \"eval A v2 added\" sample of 200 from the sorted added tuples\n     - re-screens v1's 38,900 through `random_picks(v1_others)`\n     - writes `keep_judgments(OUT/\"added.json\", fields (\"first\",\"rejudged\",\"ghost_report\",\"note\"))`\n   - Report sections:\n     - `checks`: years, code_versions, lexicon_versions, v1 and v2 candidates, v1_candidates_lost, added, added_holding_an_old_term\n     - `rules`: new, candidates, only, added_holding, added_holding_only_it\n     - `added`: seed, sample, judged, first_reading, negatives_rejudged and negatives_agreed, then rate, by_rule and reports once fully judged\n     - `recall_in_sample`: v1_screened, v2_candidates_among_them, v1_reports, caught, `missed = rate(len(left), drawn - len(caught), non_candidates)`, and the `recall` arithmetic once judged, from v1 `eval_a.json` composition ghost_report\n   - It writes `OUT/\"lexicon_v2.json\"` and prints a summary.\n\n   **`tests/test_lexicon_v2.py`** (new, uncommitted): 3 tests, for `compare`, `summarise_rules` flags and the `recall_in_sample` arithmetic.\n\n   **`Makefile`** (uncommitted)\n   ```\n   eval:\n   \tuv run python scripts/eval_a.py\n   \tuv run python scripts/lexicon_v2.py\n\n   eval-v1:\n   \tuv run python scripts/eval_a.py data/archive/lexicon-v1\n   ```\n   `eval-v1` was added to `.PHONY`.\n\n   **Data**\n   - `data/archive/lexicon-v1/{candidates,samples}` were moved there (180 files each, 1.3G and 565M). `{counts,manifests}` were copied (180 files each).\n   - `data/archive/` is ignored by `data/*`.\n   - The v2 read is writing `data/candidates`, `data/samples`, `data/counts` and `data/manifests`.\n\n   **Docs from 2A.7 (committed, now on `main`)**\n   - Decision 30, with a pointer added at the end of decision 27.\n   - BUILD-PLAN: the 2B reprint set; the 2C `POLICY.md` item (delusions, hallucinations, spirit control, felt presence); 2C eval B.\n   - RESUME: the Phase 2A STATUS block.\n\n   **Scratch** (`/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/`)\n   - `scan_staged.py`, `shot.mjs` and `charts/`.\n   - `screen/`: batches, verdicts, `ids.json`, `merge.py`, `finalize.py`, `fulltext.py` and `v2.py`.\n   - `added/brief.txt`: the reader brief template with placeholders BATCH_PATH, N_ITEMS, FIRST_ID, LAST_ID and OUT_PATH.\n   - Logs: `ingest-v2.log`, `temporal-v2.log`, `worker-v2.log` and `eval-v1-repro.log`.\n\n4. **Errors and fixes**\n   - **Lexicon test placement:** I first inserted the new tests between the near-miss parametrize decorator and its function, which caused a collection error. I moved the block above the near-miss decorator.\n   - **Lint:**\n     - ruff FURB167 flagged the `re.I` alias; changed to `re.IGNORECASE`.\n     - ruff RUF100 flagged an unused `noqa`; I replaced the lambda with a nested `def totals(key)`.\n   - **Reproduction timestamp:** the v1 reproduction changed only `generated_at` in `eval/results/eval_a.json`, which I restored with `git checkout`.\n   - **From 2A.7:**\n     - The chart subtitle overflowed; I shortened it.\n     - Small shares read \"0.0%\"; I added `pct()`, test first.\n     - ruff reformatting broke a substring replacement; I redid the edit directly.\n     - zsh doesn't word-split `$A`; `fulltext.py` now splits its arguments.\n     - The `ULTRAMARINE_BASE_URL` scan hit was a false positive (\"instruments.\" in a 1913 article).\n\n5. **Problem Solving**\n   - **v1 reproducibility under v2 code:**\n     - The term list comes from the data; all 12 v1 terms occur, so v1's order is kept.\n     - Masking uses only the read's own ghost terms.\n     - The lexicon and code versions are read from counts and manifests.\n     - The judged stages run only for v1.\n     - Verified byte-identical apart from the timestamp. The re-run took 65 s, alongside the read.\n   - **v2 is additive:** v1's terms come first in the alternation, and the post-read subset check will confirm v1 ⊆ v2.\n   - **Read progress:** 69 of 180 years by 14:56:42Z, 14.3 GB, one code version `2d6ff0faaf81`. ETA about 16:50Z.\n\n6. **All user messages** (cross-session EM messages)\n   - **The 2A.7 request.**\n     - The EM's review: eval A passes; the re-run was byte-identical apart from `generated_at`, in 43 s at 4.2 GB.\n     - The step's items: the 38,600 draw with `draw()` asserting, screening with FLAGS, judging by decision 27, the subagent 10% check, the mask test, the report, the lexicon v2 proposal if any reports turn up, the dictionary sha256 and word count.\n     - Plan items: 2C `POLICY.md`, 2B reprint set (per candidate; chaining), 2C eval B (sized by the Tinker credit check).\n     - The EM's spot check, seed \"EM spot-check 2A\": every candidate's term is in its text, none of the 10 non-candidates is a report, and 1 of the 20 candidates is a report. \"Cite this in the STATUS block.\"\n     - \"After 2A.7, write the Phase 2A STATUS block, open the 2A PR into main, and report its number. I'll merge it once CI is green.\"\n   - **The 2A.8 message**, verbatim:\n\n     \"PR #2 is merged as 5ac2e1b. Lexicon v2 is approved, as step 2A.8, on a new branch `phase-2a-lexicon-v2` from main. 2A.7 passes my review: - The 4 reports hold up, and your two borderline calls stand. They match eval A's 1904 spectre, and `POLICY.md` will settle them. - 4 of 38,900 gives 0.0040–0.026%, about 38,000 missed, and recall of about 49% with a floor of 27%. I checked the arithmetic. **Why v2:** decision 27 counts spirit manifestations, and v1 misses about half of all reports. The re-read costs machine time and bandwidth, not money. **Conditions:** 1. **Keep v1.** Before the re-read, move v1's candidates and samples to `data/archive/lexicon-v1/`; move them, don't delete them. Eval A's v1 composition, recall and screen stay as the record of v1, reproducible from that archive. No hand judgment is redone or lost. 2. **Record the rule.** Each candidate records the rule that matched it, for example `spirit+medium`. That way a rule can be dropped later by filtering, without another re-read. 3. **Re-read all 180 years at 4 processes.** While it runs: - don't switch branches, and don't edit anything the worker imports in this checkout, because each year's process loads the code from disk - if you want to start on other work meanwhile, use a separate worktree Afterwards, check that: - all 180 manifests share one code_version - v1 is a subset of v2: every v1 candidate id is still a candidate, and every new one matches only a new rule - running 1850 twice gives identical outputs - disk use, the archive plus v2, is within the 10 GB budget Report the counts per rule. 4. **Measure the new rules on fresh data.** Judge a seeded 200 from the v2-only candidates, with the 2A.7 protocol. Report: - the added candidates' report rate, with its interval - the reports gained - the same per rule, where the counts allow Flag any rule with no report in its share of the sample. Dropping it is my call. 5. **Report v2's recall as in-sample,** because its terms were chosen from these 4 misses. The independent figure comes from 2C eval B's reader screen. 6. **Regenerate eval A's EDA charts on v2.** 7. **Close out.** Update the STATUS block with the v2 numbers and the in-sample caveat, open a PR into main, and report its number. I'll merge it after review, and then 2B starts. The reader's 15% more candidates gets checked against Tinker credits before the full pass, as planned. Nothing is spent now.\"\n\n7. **Pending Tasks (2A.8)**\n   - Wait for the re-read to finish (background task `bmpjzqy3u`). Do not switch branches or edit `paperghosts/` while it runs.\n   - **After the read:**\n     - Check all 180 manifests share one code_version.\n     - Run the subset check and get the counts per rule (`lexicon_v2.py`).\n     - Re-run 1850 with its candidates moved aside, and compare its output sha256 values with the saved manifest.\n     - Report disk use for the archive plus v2, against 10 GB.\n   - **Evals:** run `make eval`, which does the v2 EDA (6 charts in `eval_a_v2/`) and then `lexicon_v2.py`. Check the charts in both themes. Re-run `make eval-v1` to confirm the v1 record is still byte-identical after the `random_picks` refactor.\n   - **Judge the seeded 200 added candidates** (`added.json`) with the 2A.7 protocol:\n     - reading agents, using `brief.txt`\n     - my seeded 10% negative re-judge\n     - my full re-read of every positive and close call\n   - Then fill in the judgments, re-run, and report the rate, the reports gained, per-rule results and the no-report flags.\n   - **In-sample recall:** report it labelled as in-sample.\n   - **Write-up:**\n     - decision 31\n     - update the RESUME STATUS block with the v2 numbers and the in-sample caveat\n     - write the hand-over note `step-2A.8.md`\n   - **Ship:**\n     - commit in small parts: eval code, data and results, docs\n     - scan each staged diff first\n     - push with `-u`\n     - open a PR into `main`\n     - call `get_status` and bind the PR\n     - report its number: \"2A.8: DONE\", 150 words at most\n\n8. **Current Work**\n\n   Immediately before the summary request, I had:\n   - confirmed the read's progress with \"69 of 180 years on v2; 14.3 GB read; ['2d6ff0faaf81']\", latest finished 14:56:28Z\n   - called `wrap_up` with the recap: \"2A.8 in progress: lexicon v2 is committed (72ef622), v1's data is archived, and the re-read is at 69 of 180 years, all on code 2d6ff0faaf81. v1's eval record reproduces from the archive, and the v2 eval scripts are tested. Checks and judging start when the read ends, about 16:50Z.\"\n\n   The closing in-progress message to the EM was still to be written. The Temporal server, worker and ingest are running in the background. The ingest log is `scratchpad/ingest-v2.log`, which starts with \"started 2026-10-30T14:49:24Z\". Uncommitted on `phase-2a-lexicon-v2`: `scripts/eval_a.py`, `scripts/charts.py`, `scripts/lexicon_v2.py`, `tests/test_lexicon_v2.py` and `Makefile`, plus the read's `data/counts` and `data/manifests` changes.\n\n9. **Optional Next Step**\n\n   Send the brief in-progress closing message, for example \"2A.8: RUNNING — the v2 re-read is underway (69 of 180 years, one code version). The eval code is ready; checks and judging follow the read.\" Then wait for the ingest completion notification. When it arrives, carry on with 2A.8 per the EM's conditions: \"Afterwards, check that: - all 180 manifests share one code_version - v1 is a subset of v2 ... - running 1850 twice gives identical outputs - disk use, the archive plus v2, is within the 10 GB budget. Report the counts per rule.\" Then: \"Judge a seeded 200 from the v2-only candidates, with the 2A.7 protocol.\"\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/hotragn/.claude/projects/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened.\n\nThe messages after this summary are the most recent messages from before compaction, kept verbatim. The summary was written without seeing them, so something it says has not happened yet may already have happened in them."},"isVisibleInTranscriptOnly":true,"isCompactSummary":true,"uuid":"78023a87-361d-4e76-906a-0dd0a2928ddb","timestamp":"2026-10-30T15:52:40.710Z","turnPosition":{"promptIndex":0,"turnIndex":28},"turnOrigin":"task_notification","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"78023a87-361d-4e76-906a-0dd0a2928ddb","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/added/brief.txt","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/added/brief.txt","content":"You are judging old newspaper articles for an evaluation of a ghost-report search over the Library of Congress's digitized American newspapers (1770s to 1960s). Every article in your batch was drawn at random from the articles that the search's new spiritualist terms made candidates, and that its older ghost words did not. Each holds at least one of: spiritualism or spiritualist(s); séance; a spirit phrase such as spirit world, spirit message, control of spirits, disembodied, or spirits of the dead; ouija or planchette; or the words spirit and medium somewhere in one article. For each article, decide one thing: is it a ghost report?\n\nGHOST REPORT, the only positive: a ghost, haunting, apparition or spirit manifestation, told as something that happened or was claimed to. These all count when told as real or as claimed, in news, a letter, court testimony or an anecdote told as true: spirit-rapping, table-tipping, materializations, a spirit speaking through a medium or a ouija board, spirit photographs, a séance described as having happened with its phenomena, a house where noises or lights are taken for the dead, a sighting of a dead person. A particular séance or manifestation exposed as a fraud still counts, because it was claimed, just as a ghost scare that turns out to be a trapped man counts.\n\nNOT a report:\n- fiction: stories, serials, poems, jokes and allegories, even ones about spirits\n- a séance, spiritualist meeting or lecture only announced or advertised for later\n- spiritualism discussed in general, for or against: essays, sermons, editorials, a lecture's arguments, mediums' tricks explained in general, a list of a church's meetings\n- an advertisement for a medium's or clairvoyant's services\n- a witch or witchcraft with no ghost; devils and demons, which are religious\n- spirit as mood, courage, liquor or the Holy Spirit; \"the spirit of\" anything; a medium of exchange, an artist's medium, a medium size\n\nMark CLOSE when:\n- it might be a report on a fuller reading, for example a window mentions a séance's phenomena but is cut off\n- it tells of a hallucination, a delusion, an insane person's visions, a claim of spirit control (for example in a court defence), a felt presence of the dead, or a dream or premonition of the dead (the project hasn't settled these yet)\n- you are unsure for any other reason\n\nThe text is OCR of old newspapers and is often garbled; read through the noise. Each article is a block:\n\n=== <id> | <date> | <paper> | <length> | terms: <the new terms that made it a candidate>\nHEADLINE: ...\nOPENING: the first ~420 characters of the article\nWINDOW: ~300 characters around a word of those terms (up to 6 windows)\n\nYour batch is BATCH_PATH, with N_ITEMS articles (ids FIRST_ID to LAST_ID). Read every block yourself. Read the file with the Read tool, in parts with offset and limit if it is long, until you reach the end.\n\nWrite your verdicts to OUT_PATH as a JSON list with one object per article, in file order:\n{\"id\": \"FIRST_ID\", \"verdict\": \"no\", \"note\": \"\"}\nverdict is \"no\", \"close\" or \"report\". The note is required for close and report: one sentence on what the article says happened and why it is or might be a report. For \"no\", leave it empty, or give a few words if it was near.\n\nUse only the batch file. Don't read other files, search the web, or write code that decides verdicts (keyword rules would defeat the purpose). You may run one short script at the end only to check that your output is valid JSON with exactly one verdict for every id.\n\nReply with the count of each verdict and every id you marked close or report, with its note. Nothing else.\n","numLines":34,"startLine":1,"totalLines":34}},"displayPath":"../../../../../../../private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/added/brief.txt"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"85f88274-91f9-402c-9861-73865e456bd4","timestamp":"2026-10-30T16:50:04.261Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/added/brief.txt\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\tYou are judging old newspaper articles for an evaluation of a ghost-report search over the Library of Congress's digitized American newspapers (1770s to 1960s). Every article in your batch was drawn at random from the articles that the search's new spiritualist terms made candidates, and that its older ghost words did not. Each holds at least one of: spiritualism or spiritualist(s); séance; a spirit phrase such as spirit world, spirit message, control of spirits, disembodied, or spirits of the dead; ouija or planchette; or the words spirit and medium somewhere in one article. For each article, decide one thing: is it a ghost report?\n2\t\n3\tGHOST REPORT, the only positive: a ghost, haunting, apparition or spirit manifestation, told as something that happened or was claimed to. These all count when told as real or as claimed, in news, a letter, court testimony or an anecdote told as true: spirit-rapping, table-tipping, materializations, a spirit speaking through a medium or a ouija board, spirit photographs, a séance described as having happened with its phenomena, a house where noises or lights are taken for the dead, a sighting of a dead person. A particular séance or manifestation exposed as a fraud still counts, because it was claimed, just as a ghost scare that turns out to be a trapped man counts.\n4\t\n5\tNOT a report:\n6\t- fiction: stories, serials, poems, jokes and allegories, even ones about spirits\n7\t- a séance, spiritualist meeting or lecture only announced or advertised for later\n8\t- spiritualism discussed in general, for or against: essays, sermons, editorials, a lecture's arguments, mediums' tricks explained in general, a list of a church's meetings\n9\t- an advertisement for a medium's or clairvoyant's services\n10\t- a witch or witchcraft with no ghost; devils and demons, which are religious\n11\t- spirit as mood, courage, liquor or the Holy Spirit; \"the spirit of\" anything; a medium of exchange, an artist's medium, a medium size\n12\t\n13\tMark CLOSE when:\n14\t- it might be a report on a fuller reading, for example a window mentions a séance's phenomena but is cut off\n15\t- it tells of a hallucination, a delusion, an insane person's visions, a claim of spirit control (for example in a court defence), a felt presence of the dead, or a dream or premonition of the dead (the project hasn't settled these yet)\n16\t- you are unsure for any other reason\n17\t\n18\tThe text is OCR of old newspapers and is often garbled; read through the noise. Each article is a block:\n19\t\n20\t=== <id> | <date> | <paper> | <length> | terms: <the new terms that made it a candidate>\n21\tHEADLINE: ...\n22\tOPENING: the first ~420 characters of the article\n23\tWINDOW: ~300 characters around a word of those terms (up to 6 windows)\n24\t\n25\tYour batch is BATCH_PATH, with N_ITEMS articles (ids FIRST_ID to LAST_ID). Read every block yourself. Read the file with the Read tool, in parts with offset and limit if it is long, until you reach the end.\n26\t\n27\tWrite your verdicts to OUT_PATH as a JSON list with one object per article, in file order:\n28\t{\"id\": \"FIRST_ID\", \"verdict\": \"no\", \"note\": \"\"}\n29\tverdict is \"no\", \"close\" or \"report\". The note is required for close and report: one sentence on what the article says happened and why it is or might be a report. For \"no\", leave it empty, or give a few words if it was near.\n30\t\n31\tUse only the batch file. Don't read other files, search the web, or write code that decides verdicts (keyword rules would defeat the purpose). You may run one short script at the end only to check that your output is valid JSON with exactly one verdict for every id.\n32\t\n33\tReply with the count of each verdict and every id you marked close or report, with its note. Nothing else.\n34\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"85f88274-91f9-402c-9861-73865e456bd4","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/scripts/lexicon_v2.py","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/scripts/lexicon_v2.py","content":"\"\"\"The second half of `make eval`: lexicon v2's read in data/ against v1's, archived in\ndata/archive/lexicon-v1 (decision 31). It writes eval/results/eval_a_v2/lexicon_v2.json, and the\nhand-judged sample of the candidates v2 adds to eval/results/eval_a_v2/added.json.\n\n1. Checks: one code version made the read, every v1 candidate is still one, and every added\n   candidate holds only v2's new terms.\n2. Each rule's candidates, and the added ones it holds.\n3. 200 added candidates at random, judged as in decision 30: their report rate and the reports\n   v2 gains, overall and by rule.\n4. Recall, in-sample: v1's screen (decision 30) read again with v2. v2's terms were chosen from\n   that screen's misses, so 2C's eval B gives the independent figure.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport json\nimport random\nimport re\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\nfrom concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor\nfrom datetime import UTC, datetime\n\nimport zstandard\nfrom eval_a import DATA, RESULTS, keep_judgments, random_picks, rate, window\n\nfrom paperghosts.ingest import lexicon\n\nV1 = DATA / \"archive\" / \"lexicon-v1\"\nOUT = RESULTS / \"eval_a_v2\"\nADDED = 200\n\n\ndef compare(v1, v2, new):\n    \"\"\"v1's candidates that v2 lost, v2's added ones, and any added one holding an old term.\"\"\"\n    kept = {row[\"article_id\"] for row in v2}\n    added = [row for row in v2 if row[\"article_id\"] not in v1]\n    return {\n        \"missing\": sorted(v1 - kept),\n        \"added\": added,\n        \"old_terms\": [row[\"article_id\"] for row in added if set(row[\"terms\"]) - new],\n    }\n\n\ndef summarise_rules(sample, added):\n    \"\"\"Each rule's share of the judged sample, its report rate, and the reports it gains among\n    the added candidates holding it; a rule with no report in its share is flagged.\"\"\"\n    out = {}\n    for rule, held in added.items():\n        judged = [\n            s for s in sample if rule in s[\"terms\"] and s[\"ghost_report\"] is not None\n        ]\n        found = sum(bool(s[\"ghost_report\"]) for s in judged)\n        out[rule] = {\"added\": held, \"n\": len(judged), \"no_report\": not found}\n        if judged:\n            out[rule] |= rate(found, len(judged), held)\n    return out\n\n\ndef recall_in_sample(v1, gained, missed):\n    \"\"\"v2's recall: the reports in v1's candidates and in those v2 adds, over those and the\n    reports still missed. The floor takes the most missed, at each end of the found.\"\"\"\n    found = {\n        name: v1[f\"implied{end}\"] + gained[f\"implied{end}\"]\n        for name, end in ((\"low\", \"_low\"), (\"point\", \"\"), (\"high\", \"_high\"))\n    }\n    return {\n        \"point\": found[\"point\"] / (found[\"point\"] + missed[\"implied\"]),\n        \"at_least\": {\n            name: f / (f + missed[\"implied_high\"]) for name, f in found.items()\n        },\n    }\n\n\ndef year_compare(job):\n    year, new = job\n    with zstandard.open(V1 / \"candidates\" / f\"{year}.jsonl.zst\", \"rt\") as rows:\n        v1 = {json.loads(row)[\"article_id\"] for row in rows}\n    with zstandard.open(DATA / \"candidates\" / f\"{year}.jsonl.zst\", \"rt\") as rows:\n        v2 = [\n            {\"article_id\": row[\"article_id\"], \"terms\": sorted(row[\"terms\"])}\n            for row in map(json.loads, rows)\n        ]\n    found = compare(v1, v2, new)\n    found[\"added\"] = [(year, row[\"article_id\"], row[\"terms\"]) for row in found[\"added\"]]\n    return year, len(v1), len(v2), found\n\n\ndef year_items(job):\n    \"\"\"The picked added candidates of one year, as they are judged: the opening, and the text\n    around each word of their terms.\"\"\"\n    year, wanted = job\n    items = []\n    with zstandard.open(DATA / \"candidates\" / f\"{year}.jsonl.zst\", \"rt\") as rows:\n        for row in map(json.loads, rows):\n            if row[\"article_id\"] not in wanted:\n                continue\n            text = (row[\"text\"] or \"\").replace(\"-\\n\", \"\")\n            forms = {form for found in row[\"terms\"].values() for form in found}\n            spots = sorted(\n                {\n                    m.start()\n                    for f in forms\n                    for m in re.finditer(re.escape(f), text, re.IGNORECASE)\n                }\n            )\n            items.append(\n                {\n                    \"article_id\": row[\"article_id\"],\n                    \"year\": year,\n                    \"date\": row[\"date\"],\n                    \"paper\": row[\"paper\"],\n                    \"terms\": sorted(row[\"terms\"]),\n                    \"forms\": sorted(forms),\n                    \"words\": len(text.split()),\n                    \"headline\": \" / \".join((row[\"headline\"] or \"\").split(\"\\n\"))[:200],\n                    \"opening\": \" \".join(text[:420].split()),\n                    \"contexts\": [window(text, p, 300) for p in spots[:6]],\n                    \"first\": None,\n                    \"rejudged\": None,\n                    \"ghost_report\": None,\n                    \"note\": None,\n                }\n            )\n    return items\n\n\ndef year_rescreen(job):\n    \"\"\"Which of v1's screened articles of one year lexicon v2 makes candidates.\"\"\"\n    year, wanted = job\n    caught, seen = {}, 0\n    with zstandard.open(V1 / \"samples\" / f\"{year}.jsonl.zst\", \"rt\") as rows:\n        for row in map(json.loads, rows):\n            if row[\"sample\"] != \"random\":\n                continue\n            seen += 1\n            if seen - 1 in wanted:\n                terms = lexicon.ghost_terms(\n                    f\"{row['headline'] or ''}\\n{row['text'] or ''}\"\n                )\n                if terms:\n                    caught[row[\"article_id\"]] = sorted(terms)\n    return caught\n\n\ndef counts_of(data):\n    return {\n        c[\"year\"]: c\n        for c in (json.loads(p.read_text()) for p in (data / \"counts\").glob(\"*.json\"))\n    }\n\n\ndef main():\n    v1_counts, v2_counts = counts_of(V1), counts_of(DATA)\n    years = sorted(v2_counts)\n    old = {term for c in v1_counts.values() for term in c[\"by_term\"]}\n    new = [t for t in (*lexicon.GHOST, *lexicon.TOGETHER) if t not in old]\n    manifests = [json.loads(p.read_text()) for p in (DATA / \"manifests\").glob(\"*.json\")]\n\n    added, missing, old_terms, sizes = [], [], [], Counter()\n    with ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=6) as pool:\n        jobs = [\n            (y, set(new))\n            for y in sorted(years, key=lambda y: -v2_counts[y][\"articles\"])\n        ]\n        for _, n1, n2, found in pool.map(year_compare, jobs):\n            sizes.update(v1=n1, v2=n2)\n            added += found[\"added\"]\n            missing += found[\"missing\"]\n            old_terms += found[\"old_terms\"]\n    added.sort()\n    holding = Counter(term for *_, terms in added for term in terms)\n    alone = Counter(terms[0] for *_, terms in added if len(terms) == 1)\n\n    picks = random.Random(\"eval A v2 added\").sample(added, min(ADDED, len(added)))\n    wanted = defaultdict(set)\n    for year, article_id, _ in picks:\n        wanted[year].add(article_id)\n    v1_others = {\n        y: c[\"articles\"] - c[\"candidates\"] for y, c in sorted(v1_counts.items())\n    }\n    rand, _, screen = random_picks(v1_others)\n    screened = {y: rand[y] | screen[y] for y in years if rand[y] | screen[y]}\n    with ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=6) as pool:\n        items = [i for found in pool.map(year_items, wanted.items()) for i in found]\n        caught = {}\n        for found in pool.map(year_rescreen, screened.items()):\n            caught |= found\n\n    OUT.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    items = keep_judgments(\n        OUT / \"added.json\",\n        sorted(items, key=lambda i: (i[\"year\"], i[\"article_id\"])),\n        lambda i: i[\"article_id\"],\n        (\"first\", \"rejudged\", \"ghost_report\", \"note\"),\n    )\n    judged = [i for i in items if i[\"ghost_report\"] is not None]\n    checked = [i for i in judged if i[\"first\"] == \"no\" and i[\"rejudged\"]]\n\n    def totals(key):\n        return {\n            t: sum(c[key].get(t, 0) for c in v2_counts.values())\n            for t in (*lexicon.GHOST, *lexicon.TOGETHER)\n        }\n\n    report = {\n        \"generated_at\": datetime.now(UTC).isoformat(timespec=\"seconds\"),\n        \"checks\": {\n            \"years\": len(manifests),\n            \"code_versions\": sorted({m[\"code_version\"] for m in manifests}),\n            \"lexicon_versions\": sorted({m[\"lexicon_version\"] for m in manifests}),\n            \"v1_candidates\": sizes[\"v1\"],\n            \"v2_candidates\": sizes[\"v2\"],\n            \"v1_candidates_lost\": len(missing),\n            \"added\": len(added),\n            \"added_holding_an_old_term\": len(old_terms),\n        },\n        \"rules\": {\n            \"new\": new,\n            \"candidates\": totals(\"by_term\"),\n            \"only\": totals(\"only_by_term\"),\n            \"added_holding\": {t: holding[t] for t in new},\n            # what dropping the rule alone would take away\n            \"added_holding_only_it\": {t: alone[t] for t in new},\n        },\n        \"added\": {\n            \"seed\": \"eval A v2 added\",\n            \"sample\": len(items),\n            \"judged\": len(judged),\n            \"first_reading\": dict(Counter(i[\"first\"] for i in judged)),\n            \"negatives_rejudged\": len(checked),\n            \"negatives_agreed\": sum(not i[\"ghost_report\"] for i in checked),\n        },\n    }\n    articles = sum(v2_counts[y][\"articles\"] for y in years)\n    non_candidates = articles - sizes[\"v2\"]\n    v1_reports = {\n        r[\"article_id\"]\n        for path in (\"screen.json\", \"recall.json\")\n        for r in json.loads((RESULTS / \"eval_a\" / path).read_text())\n        if r.get(\"pool\", \"random\") == \"random\" and r[\"ghost_report\"]\n    }\n    drawn = sum(len(v) for v in screened.values())\n    left = v1_reports - caught.keys()\n    report[\"recall_in_sample\"] = {\n        \"v1_screened\": drawn,\n        \"v2_candidates_among_them\": len(caught),\n        \"v1_reports\": len(v1_reports),\n        \"caught\": {a: caught[a] for a in sorted(v1_reports & caught.keys())},\n        \"missed\": rate(len(left), drawn - len(caught), non_candidates),\n    }\n    if judged and len(judged) == len(items):\n        found = sum(bool(i[\"ghost_report\"]) for i in judged)\n        report[\"added\"] |= rate(found, len(judged), len(added))\n        report[\"added\"][\"by_rule\"] = summarise_rules(\n            items, {t: holding[t] for t in new}\n        )\n        report[\"added\"][\"reports\"] = [\n            {k: i[k] for k in (\"article_id\", \"year\", \"date\", \"paper\", \"terms\", \"note\")}\n            for i in judged\n            if i[\"ghost_report\"]\n        ]\n        v1_eval = json.loads((RESULTS / \"eval_a.json\").read_text())\n        report[\"recall_in_sample\"][\"recall\"] = recall_in_sample(\n            v1_eval[\"composition\"][\"categories\"][\"ghost_report\"],\n            report[\"added\"],\n            report[\"recall_in_sample\"][\"missed\"],\n        )\n    (OUT / \"lexicon_v2.json\").write_text(json.dumps(report, indent=1) + \"\\n\")\n    c = report[\"checks\"]\n    print(\n        f\"{c['years']} years, code {c['code_versions']}; v1 {c['v1_candidates']:,} -> v2 \"\n        f\"{c['v2_candidates']:,} candidates; {c['v1_candidates_lost']} lost, {c['added']:,} \"\n        f\"added, {c['added_holding_an_old_term']} holding an old term\"\n    )\n    print(\"added holding each rule:\", report[\"rules\"][\"added_holding\"])\n    print(f\"wrote {(OUT / 'lexicon_v2.json').relative_to(DATA.parent)}\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n","numLines":279,"startLine":1,"totalLines":279}},"displayPath":"scripts/lexicon_v2.py"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"3195dd82-72d1-4ef8-830d-885651176122","timestamp":"2026-10-30T16:50:04.261Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/scripts/lexicon_v2.py\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t\"\"\"The second half of `make eval`: lexicon v2's read in data/ against v1's, archived in\n2\tdata/archive/lexicon-v1 (decision 31). It writes eval/results/eval_a_v2/lexicon_v2.json, and the\n3\thand-judged sample of the candidates v2 adds to eval/results/eval_a_v2/added.json.\n4\t\n5\t1. Checks: one code version made the read, every v1 candidate is still one, and every added\n6\t   candidate holds only v2's new terms.\n7\t2. Each rule's candidates, and the added ones it holds.\n8\t3. 200 added candidates at random, judged as in decision 30: their report rate and the reports\n9\t   v2 gains, overall and by rule.\n10\t4. Recall, in-sample: v1's screen (decision 30) read again with v2. v2's terms were chosen from\n11\t   that screen's misses, so 2C's eval B gives the independent figure.\n12\t\"\"\"\n13\t\n14\timport json\n15\timport random\n16\timport re\n17\tfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\n18\tfrom concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor\n19\tfrom datetime import UTC, datetime\n20\t\n21\timport zstandard\n22\tfrom eval_a import DATA, RESULTS, keep_judgments, random_picks, rate, window\n23\t\n24\tfrom paperghosts.ingest import lexicon\n25\t\n26\tV1 = DATA / \"archive\" / \"lexicon-v1\"\n27\tOUT = RESULTS / \"eval_a_v2\"\n28\tADDED = 200\n29\t\n30\t\n31\tdef compare(v1, v2, new):\n32\t    \"\"\"v1's candidates that v2 lost, v2's added ones, and any added one holding an old term.\"\"\"\n33\t    kept = {row[\"article_id\"] for row in v2}\n34\t    added = [row for row in v2 if row[\"article_id\"] not in v1]\n35\t    return {\n36\t        \"missing\": sorted(v1 - kept),\n37\t        \"added\": added,\n38\t        \"old_terms\": [row[\"article_id\"] for row in added if set(row[\"terms\"]) - new],\n39\t    }\n40\t\n41\t\n42\tdef summarise_rules(sample, added):\n43\t    \"\"\"Each rule's share of the judged sample, its report rate, and the reports it gains among\n44\t    the added candidates holding it; a rule with no report in its share is flagged.\"\"\"\n45\t    out = {}\n46\t    for rule, held in added.items():\n47\t        judged = [\n48\t            s for s in sample if rule in s[\"terms\"] and s[\"ghost_report\"] is not None\n49\t        ]\n50\t        found = sum(bool(s[\"ghost_report\"]) for s in judged)\n51\t        out[rule] = {\"added\": held, \"n\": len(judged), \"no_report\": not found}\n52\t        if judged:\n53\t            out[rule] |= rate(found, len(judged), held)\n54\t    return out\n55\t\n56\t\n57\tdef recall_in_sample(v1, gained, missed):\n58\t    \"\"\"v2's recall: the reports in v1's candidates and in those v2 adds, over those and the\n59\t    reports still missed. The floor takes the most missed, at each end of the found.\"\"\"\n60\t    found = {\n61\t        name: v1[f\"implied{end}\"] + gained[f\"implied{end}\"]\n62\t        for name, end in ((\"low\", \"_low\"), (\"point\", \"\"), (\"high\", \"_high\"))\n63\t    }\n64\t    return {\n65\t        \"point\": found[\"point\"] / (found[\"point\"] + missed[\"implied\"]),\n66\t        \"at_least\": {\n67\t            name: f / (f + missed[\"implied_high\"]) for name, f in found.items()\n68\t        },\n69\t    }\n70\t\n71\t\n72\tdef year_compare(job):\n73\t    year, new = job\n74\t    with zstandard.open(V1 / \"candidates\" / f\"{year}.jsonl.zst\", \"rt\") as rows:\n75\t        v1 = {json.loads(row)[\"article_id\"] for row in rows}\n76\t    with zstandard.open(DATA / \"candidates\" / f\"{year}.jsonl.zst\", \"rt\") as rows:\n77\t        v2 = [\n78\t            {\"article_id\": row[\"article_id\"], \"terms\": sorted(row[\"terms\"])}\n79\t            for row in map(json.loads, rows)\n80\t        ]\n81\t    found = compare(v1, v2, new)\n82\t    found[\"added\"] = [(year, row[\"article_id\"], row[\"terms\"]) for row in found[\"added\"]]\n83\t    return year, len(v1), len(v2), found\n84\t\n85\t\n86\tdef year_items(job):\n87\t    \"\"\"The picked added candidates of one year, as they are judged: the opening, and the text\n88\t    around each word of their terms.\"\"\"\n89\t    year, wanted = job\n90\t    items = []\n91\t    with zstandard.open(DATA / \"candidates\" / f\"{year}.jsonl.zst\", \"rt\") as rows:\n92\t        for row in map(json.loads, rows):\n93\t            if row[\"article_id\"] not in wanted:\n94\t                continue\n95\t            text = (row[\"text\"] or \"\").replace(\"-\\n\", \"\")\n96\t            forms = {form for found in row[\"terms\"].values() for form in found}\n97\t            spots = sorted(\n98\t                {\n99\t                    m.start()\n100\t                    for f in forms\n101\t                    for m in re.finditer(re.escape(f), text, re.IGNORECASE)\n102\t                }\n103\t            )\n104\t            items.append(\n105\t                {\n106\t                    \"article_id\": row[\"article_id\"],\n107\t                    \"year\": year,\n108\t                    \"date\": row[\"date\"],\n109\t                    \"paper\": row[\"paper\"],\n110\t                    \"terms\": sorted(row[\"terms\"]),\n111\t                    \"forms\": sorted(forms),\n112\t                    \"words\": len(text.split()),\n113\t                    \"headline\": \" / \".join((row[\"headline\"] or \"\").split(\"\\n\"))[:200],\n114\t                    \"opening\": \" \".join(text[:420].split()),\n115\t                    \"contexts\": [window(text, p, 300) for p in spots[:6]],\n116\t                    \"first\": None,\n117\t                    \"rejudged\": None,\n118\t                    \"ghost_report\": None,\n119\t                    \"note\": None,\n120\t                }\n121\t            )\n122\t    return items\n123\t\n124\t\n125\tdef year_rescreen(job):\n126\t    \"\"\"Which of v1's screened articles of one year lexicon v2 makes candidates.\"\"\"\n127\t    year, wanted = job\n128\t    caught, seen = {}, 0\n129\t    with zstandard.open(V1 / \"samples\" / f\"{year}.jsonl.zst\", \"rt\") as rows:\n130\t        for row in map(json.loads, rows):\n131\t            if row[\"sample\"] != \"random\":\n132\t                continue\n133\t            seen += 1\n134\t            if seen - 1 in wanted:\n135\t                terms = lexicon.ghost_terms(\n136\t                    f\"{row['headline'] or ''}\\n{row['text'] or ''}\"\n137\t                )\n138\t                if terms:\n139\t                    caught[row[\"article_id\"]] = sorted(terms)\n140\t    return caught\n141\t\n142\t\n143\tdef counts_of(data):\n144\t    return {\n145\t        c[\"year\"]: c\n146\t        for c in (json.loads(p.read_text()) for p in (data / \"counts\").glob(\"*.json\"))\n147\t    }\n148\t\n149\t\n150\tdef main():\n151\t    v1_counts, v2_counts = counts_of(V1), counts_of(DATA)\n152\t    years = sorted(v2_counts)\n153\t    old = {term for c in v1_counts.values() for term in c[\"by_term\"]}\n154\t    new = [t for t in (*lexicon.GHOST, *lexicon.TOGETHER) if t not in old]\n155\t    manifests = [json.loads(p.read_text()) for p in (DATA / \"manifests\").glob(\"*.json\")]\n156\t\n157\t    added, missing, old_terms, sizes = [], [], [], Counter()\n158\t    with ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=6) as pool:\n159\t        jobs = [\n160\t            (y, set(new))\n161\t            for y in sorted(years, key=lambda y: -v2_counts[y][\"articles\"])\n162\t        ]\n163\t        for _, n1, n2, found in pool.map(year_compare, jobs):\n164\t            sizes.update(v1=n1, v2=n2)\n165\t            added += found[\"added\"]\n166\t            missing += found[\"missing\"]\n167\t            old_terms += found[\"old_terms\"]\n168\t    added.sort()\n169\t    holding = Counter(term for *_, terms in added for term in terms)\n170\t    alone = Counter(terms[0] for *_, terms in added if len(terms) == 1)\n171\t\n172\t    picks = random.Random(\"eval A v2 added\").sample(added, min(ADDED, len(added)))\n173\t    wanted = defaultdict(set)\n174\t    for year, article_id, _ in picks:\n175\t        wanted[year].add(article_id)\n176\t    v1_others = {\n177\t        y: c[\"articles\"] - c[\"candidates\"] for y, c in sorted(v1_counts.items())\n178\t    }\n179\t    rand, _, screen = random_picks(v1_others)\n180\t    screened = {y: rand[y] | screen[y] for y in years if rand[y] | screen[y]}\n181\t    with ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=6) as pool:\n182\t        items = [i for found in pool.map(year_items, wanted.items()) for i in found]\n183\t        caught = {}\n184\t        for found in pool.map(year_rescreen, screened.items()):\n185\t            caught |= found\n186\t\n187\t    OUT.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n188\t    items = keep_judgments(\n189\t        OUT / \"added.json\",\n190\t        sorted(items, key=lambda i: (i[\"year\"], i[\"article_id\"])),\n191\t        lambda i: i[\"article_id\"],\n192\t        (\"first\", \"rejudged\", \"ghost_report\", \"note\"),\n193\t    )\n194\t    judged = [i for i in items if i[\"ghost_report\"] is not None]\n195\t    checked = [i for i in judged if i[\"first\"] == \"no\" and i[\"rejudged\"]]\n196\t\n197\t    def totals(key):\n198\t        return {\n199\t            t: sum(c[key].get(t, 0) for c in v2_counts.values())\n200\t            for t in (*lexicon.GHOST, *lexicon.TOGETHER)\n201\t        }\n202\t\n203\t    report = {\n204\t        \"generated_at\": datetime.now(UTC).isoformat(timespec=\"seconds\"),\n205\t        \"checks\": {\n206\t            \"years\": len(manifests),\n207\t            \"code_versions\": sorted({m[\"code_version\"] for m in manifests}),\n208\t            \"lexicon_versions\": sorted({m[\"lexicon_version\"] for m in manifests}),\n209\t            \"v1_candidates\": sizes[\"v1\"],\n210\t            \"v2_candidates\": sizes[\"v2\"],\n211\t            \"v1_candidates_lost\": len(missing),\n212\t            \"added\": len(added),\n213\t            \"added_holding_an_old_term\": len(old_terms),\n214\t        },\n215\t        \"rules\": {\n216\t            \"new\": new,\n217\t            \"candidates\": totals(\"by_term\"),\n218\t            \"only\": totals(\"only_by_term\"),\n219\t            \"added_holding\": {t: holding[t] for t in new},\n220\t            # what dropping the rule alone would take away\n221\t            \"added_holding_only_it\": {t: alone[t] for t in new},\n222\t        },\n223\t        \"added\": {\n224\t            \"seed\": \"eval A v2 added\",\n225\t            \"sample\": len(items),\n226\t            \"judged\": len(judged),\n227\t            \"first_reading\": dict(Counter(i[\"first\"] for i in judged)),\n228\t            \"negatives_rejudged\": len(checked),\n229\t            \"negatives_agreed\": sum(not i[\"ghost_report\"] for i in checked),\n230\t        },\n231\t    }\n232\t    articles = sum(v2_counts[y][\"articles\"] for y in years)\n233\t    non_candidates = articles - sizes[\"v2\"]\n234\t    v1_reports = {\n235\t        r[\"article_id\"]\n236\t        for path in (\"screen.json\", \"recall.json\")\n237\t        for r in json.loads((RESULTS / \"eval_a\" / path).read_text())\n238\t        if r.get(\"pool\", \"random\") == \"random\" and r[\"ghost_report\"]\n239\t    }\n240\t    drawn = sum(len(v) for v in screened.values())\n241\t    left = v1_reports - caught.keys()\n242\t    report[\"recall_in_sample\"] = {\n243\t        \"v1_screened\": drawn,\n244\t        \"v2_candidates_among_them\": len(caught),\n245\t        \"v1_reports\": len(v1_reports),\n246\t        \"caught\": {a: caught[a] for a in sorted(v1_reports & caught.keys())},\n247\t        \"missed\": rate(len(left), drawn - len(caught), non_candidates),\n248\t    }\n249\t    if judged and len(judged) == len(items):\n250\t        found = sum(bool(i[\"ghost_report\"]) for i in judged)\n251\t        report[\"added\"] |= rate(found, len(judged), len(added))\n252\t        report[\"added\"][\"by_rule\"] = summarise_rules(\n253\t            items, {t: holding[t] for t in new}\n254\t        )\n255\t        report[\"added\"][\"reports\"] = [\n256\t            {k: i[k] for k in (\"article_id\", \"year\", \"date\", \"paper\", \"terms\", \"note\")}\n257\t            for i in judged\n258\t            if i[\"ghost_report\"]\n259\t        ]\n260\t        v1_eval = json.loads((RESULTS / \"eval_a.json\").read_text())\n261\t        report[\"recall_in_sample\"][\"recall\"] = recall_in_sample(\n262\t            v1_eval[\"composition\"][\"categories\"][\"ghost_report\"],\n263\t            report[\"added\"],\n264\t            report[\"recall_in_sample\"][\"missed\"],\n265\t        )\n266\t    (OUT / \"lexicon_v2.json\").write_text(json.dumps(report, indent=1) + \"\\n\")\n267\t    c = report[\"checks\"]\n268\t    print(\n269\t        f\"{c['years']} years, code {c['code_versions']}; v1 {c['v1_candidates']:,} -> v2 \"\n270\t        f\"{c['v2_candidates']:,} candidates; {c['v1_candidates_lost']} lost, {c['added']:,} \"\n271\t        f\"added, {c['added_holding_an_old_term']} holding an old term\"\n272\t    )\n273\t    print(\"added holding each rule:\", report[\"rules\"][\"added_holding\"])\n274\t    print(f\"wrote {(OUT / 'lexicon_v2.json').relative_to(DATA.parent)}\")\n275\t\n276\t\n277\tif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n278\t    main()\n279\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"3195dd82-72d1-4ef8-830d-885651176122","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_lexicon_v2.py","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_lexicon_v2.py","content":"\"\"\"Lexicon v2 against v1: v2 only adds candidates, each rule's share of the judged sample is\nreported, and recall counts both lexicons' reports.\"\"\"\n\nfrom lexicon_v2 import compare, recall_in_sample, summarise_rules\n\n\ndef test_v2_keeps_every_v1_candidate_and_adds_only_new_terms():\n    v1 = {\"a\", \"b\"}\n    v2 = [\n        {\"article_id\": \"a\", \"terms\": [\"ghost\"]},\n        {\"article_id\": \"c\", \"terms\": [\"seance\"]},\n        {\"article_id\": \"d\", \"terms\": [\"ghost\", \"seance\"]},\n    ]\n\n    found = compare(v1, v2, new={\"seance\"})\n\n    assert found[\"missing\"] == [\"b\"]\n    assert [row[\"article_id\"] for row in found[\"added\"]] == [\"c\", \"d\"]\n    # d holds an old term, so v1 should have made it a candidate\n    assert found[\"old_terms\"] == [\"d\"]\n\n\ndef test_a_rule_with_no_report_in_its_share_is_flagged():\n    sample = [\n        {\"terms\": [\"seance\"], \"ghost_report\": True},\n        {\"terms\": [\"seance\", \"ouija\"], \"ghost_report\": False},\n        {\"terms\": [\"ouija\"], \"ghost_report\": False},\n        {\"terms\": [\"spirit+medium\"], \"ghost_report\": False},\n    ]\n    added = {\"seance\": 100, \"ouija\": 50, \"spirit+medium\": 400}\n\n    rules = summarise_rules(sample, added)\n\n    assert (rules[\"seance\"][\"count\"], rules[\"seance\"][\"n\"]) == (1, 2)\n    assert rules[\"seance\"][\"implied\"] == 50 and not rules[\"seance\"][\"no_report\"]\n    assert rules[\"ouija\"][\"no_report\"] and rules[\"spirit+medium\"][\"no_report\"]\n\n\ndef test_recall_counts_the_reports_of_both_lexicons():\n    v1 = {\"implied_low\": 10, \"implied\": 20, \"implied_high\": 40}\n    gained = {\"implied_low\": 5, \"implied\": 10, \"implied_high\": 20}\n    missed = {\"implied\": 10, \"implied_high\": 30}\n\n    recall = recall_in_sample(v1, gained, missed)\n\n    assert recall[\"point\"] == 30 / 40\n    assert recall[\"at_least\"] == {\"low\": 15 / 45, \"point\": 30 / 60, \"high\": 60 / 90}\n","numLines":48,"startLine":1,"totalLines":48}},"displayPath":"tests/test_lexicon_v2.py"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"4f0e3c5d-768d-41ca-a495-cf22fa7f1e43","timestamp":"2026-10-30T16:50:04.261Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_lexicon_v2.py\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t\"\"\"Lexicon v2 against v1: v2 only adds candidates, each rule's share of the judged sample is\n2\treported, and recall counts both lexicons' reports.\"\"\"\n3\t\n4\tfrom lexicon_v2 import compare, recall_in_sample, summarise_rules\n5\t\n6\t\n7\tdef test_v2_keeps_every_v1_candidate_and_adds_only_new_terms():\n8\t    v1 = {\"a\", \"b\"}\n9\t    v2 = [\n10\t        {\"article_id\": \"a\", \"terms\": [\"ghost\"]},\n11\t        {\"article_id\": \"c\", \"terms\": [\"seance\"]},\n12\t        {\"article_id\": \"d\", \"terms\": [\"ghost\", \"seance\"]},\n13\t    ]\n14\t\n15\t    found = compare(v1, v2, new={\"seance\"})\n16\t\n17\t    assert found[\"missing\"] == [\"b\"]\n18\t    assert [row[\"article_id\"] for row in found[\"added\"]] == [\"c\", \"d\"]\n19\t    # d holds an old term, so v1 should have made it a candidate\n20\t    assert found[\"old_terms\"] == [\"d\"]\n21\t\n22\t\n23\tdef test_a_rule_with_no_report_in_its_share_is_flagged():\n24\t    sample = [\n25\t        {\"terms\": [\"seance\"], \"ghost_report\": True},\n26\t        {\"terms\": [\"seance\", \"ouija\"], \"ghost_report\": False},\n27\t        {\"terms\": [\"ouija\"], \"ghost_report\": False},\n28\t        {\"terms\": [\"spirit+medium\"], \"ghost_report\": False},\n29\t    ]\n30\t    added = {\"seance\": 100, \"ouija\": 50, \"spirit+medium\": 400}\n31\t\n32\t    rules = summarise_rules(sample, added)\n33\t\n34\t    assert (rules[\"seance\"][\"count\"], rules[\"seance\"][\"n\"]) == (1, 2)\n35\t    assert rules[\"seance\"][\"implied\"] == 50 and not rules[\"seance\"][\"no_report\"]\n36\t    assert rules[\"ouija\"][\"no_report\"] and rules[\"spirit+medium\"][\"no_report\"]\n37\t\n38\t\n39\tdef test_recall_counts_the_reports_of_both_lexicons():\n40\t    v1 = {\"implied_low\": 10, \"implied\": 20, \"implied_high\": 40}\n41\t    gained = {\"implied_low\": 5, \"implied\": 10, \"implied_high\": 20}\n42\t    missed = {\"implied\": 10, \"implied_high\": 30}\n43\t\n44\t    recall = recall_in_sample(v1, gained, missed)\n45\t\n46\t    assert recall[\"point\"] == 30 / 40\n47\t    assert recall[\"at_least\"] == {\"low\": 15 / 45, \"point\": 30 / 60, \"high\": 60 / 90}\n48\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"4f0e3c5d-768d-41ca-a495-cf22fa7f1e43","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"compact_file_reference","filename":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/scripts/eval_a.py","displayPath":"scripts/eval_a.py"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"327776e6-720d-4ff8-89c3-e88f64fd3d5f","timestamp":"2026-10-30T16:50:04.261Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/scripts/eval_a.py was read before the last conversation was summarized, but the contents are too large to include. Use Read tool if you need to access it.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"327776e6-720d-4ff8-89c3-e88f64fd3d5f","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.7.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.7.md","content":"# Step 2A.7: DONE\n\nOn `phase-2a-archive`, pushed:\n- `1426fd8`: the screen, its results and decision 30\n- `36e20e3`: the STATUS block and the new 2B and 2C plan items\n\n**PR #2** into main: https://github.com/Hotragn/Paperghosts/pull/2. When it opened, its 3 CI checks were pending and GitHub showed it as mergeable. Decision 30 has the detail. `make eval` takes 42.5 s and peaks at 4.0 GB. 160 tests pass, and `make lint` exits 0.\n\n## The draw\n\n- 38,600 more articles from the random reservoirs, seed \"eval A screen\", each year in proportion to its non-candidates, across 164 years.\n- None of eval A's 300 random articles is drawn again, so together they are 38,900 distinct articles.\n- The largest draw is 1914's: 950 of the 1,985 articles left in its reservoir. 1910 drew 940 of 1,997, against the 958 its share predicts. The per-year counts are random draws, so they scatter around their shares.\n- `draw()` now asserts when a reservoir is smaller than its year's share, and the message names the year. I wrote the test first and watched it fail. The existing draws are unchanged, because the RNG calls are the same.\n\n## The screen\n\n- An article is read if its headline or text holds one of eval A's flag words **or a near-miss term**, once line-break hyphens are joined.\n  - The near-miss terms only add to the flags. So every near-miss article in the draw gets read, and the near-miss estimate doesn't rest on flag words.\n- 1,284 of the 38,600 are read (3.3%), and 713 of those are near-miss articles.\n- `eval/results/eval_a/screen.json` holds each one: the opening, up to 6 windows (eval A's recall items have 4), and these fields:\n  - `first`: the reader agent's verdict\n  - `rejudged`\n  - `ghost_report`: the final verdict\n  - `note`\n\n## The reading\n\n- Eight general-purpose agents each read 160 or 161 hits, in a seeded split. They had decision 27's definition, and a close-call rule that also covers hallucinations, delusions and dreams of the dead.\n- Their first reading was 1,274 no, 8 close calls and 2 reports.\n- I re-judged a seeded 10% of their negatives (127, seed \"eval A screen check\") from the same text, without their notes. I agreed on all 127 (95% interval 97.1–100%).\n- I re-read all 10 close calls and reports in full.\n\n## The 4 reports\n\nAll 4 are near-miss articles, and in each the only near-miss term is spirit.\n\n1. **1856-10-29, Grand River times** (from the Trenton Gazette), \"Singular Marriage\": spiritualists in Bordentown marry a dead young man's corpse to his fiancée, \"in compliance with the directions of the spirit of the bridegroom\". It counts as a claimed communication from the dead, like eval A's 1878 Vanderbilt medium. The reader agent called it a report.\n2. **1919-11-15, New-York Tribune**: Mrs. Curran says the spirit Patience chose her \"as a medium for her messages to this world\", and gives verses at a sitting where Ethel Barrymore and Dr. Hyslop are present. The reader agent called it a report.\n3. **Borderline. 1898-01-20, Abilene weekly reflector**, \"Controlled by Spirits\": a Chicago wife-murderer's defence will be that he was \"under the control of spirits\", with spiritualist mediums to testify. I counted it like eval A's 1904 courtroom spectre. The reader agent called it a close call.\n4. **Borderline. 1918-04-21, New-York Tribune**: Captain Knyvett, an Australian scout, writes \"I have felt a touch on the shoulder as when one would speak to me when there has been no one by\", and that the spirits of No Man's Land's unburied dead \"hover near\". It is a first-person claim of the dead's presence. The reader agent called it a close call.\n\nNot counted, all of them close calls first:\n- 1692 witch-trial testimony reprinted in 1841: the \"shapes\" are of living witches\n- 1907, Zion City: an \"evil spirit\", the devil, is tortured out of a woman\n- 1906: a psychical researcher's own spirit goes to Mars\n- 1900: the belief that a dead papoose's spirit stays with its mother, which is folklore in general\n- 1912: a cabman's passenger who turns out to be Aaron Burr, which reads as syndicated fiction ending at Hamilton's monument\n- 1910: a dialect sketch about a tavern, where \"hoodoo\" is OCR noise\n\n## Estimates (`eval_a.json`, `recall.screen`)\n\n| | Reports | Rate (95% Wilson) | Implied articles |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Non-candidates (the 38,600 and eval A's 300) | 4 of 38,900 | 0.010% (0.0040–0.026%) | ~38,000 (14,800–97,600) of 369.3M |\n| Near-miss pool (713 drawn and eval A's 300, deduplicated by id) | 4 of 1,013 | 0.39% (0.15–1.0%) | ~27,100 (10,600–69,500) of 6.87M |\n\n- **Recall:** the candidates hold about 36,800 reports, so lexicon v1 finds **49%** of them.\n  - It finds **at least 27%** with the most reports that non-candidates can hold.\n  - Across the candidates' own interval (17,900–74,000), that floor runs from 16% to 43%.\n- With only the 2 clear reports: about 19,000 missed (5,200–69,200), so recall is about 66%, and at least 35%.\n- `recall.svg` now shows the screen's figures, and small shares keep two significant figures (0.0040%–0.026%), so the interval doesn't read as 0.0%. I rendered it and checked it in both themes.\n\n## The screen's caveat (mask test)\n\n- With their lexicon terms masked, **5 of eval A's 7 reports** still hold a flag word or near-miss term (71%, 36–92%). They hold mysterious and spirit; in white and witchcraft; spiritualism and spirits; in white; and mysterious.\n- The two that don't:\n  - the 1904 courtroom spectre, whose only supernatural word is \"spectre\"\n  - the 1916 Peking legation, which says ghost, haunted and spooks, and also calls its ghost \"the shade of an officer\". \"Shade\" isn't a flag word.\n- So the screen may miss about 2 in 7 of the reports outside the candidates. If it does, the true count is about 1.4 times the estimates above.\n- `composition.json` gains a `flags_without_terms` field for each of its 200 items, which is the input to this test. It adds 328 lines and removes none.\n\n## Lexicon v2: a proposal for your call, not run\n\nEach term was measured on the 38,900 judged articles, with no re-read (scratch `v2.py`):\n\n| Term | Articles in 38,900 | Candidates added | Reports caught |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| spiritualism, spiritualist(s), spiritualistic | 8 | ~76,000 | 2 (1856, 1898) |\n| spirit(s) and medium(s) in one article | 8 | ~76,000 | 2 (1919, 1898) |\n| spirit world, land, control, communication, message, manifestation, circle, photograph or medium; control of or by spirits; disembodied; spirits of the dead or departed | 5 | ~47,000 | 2 (1856, 1898) |\n| ouija, planchette | 1 | ~9,500 | 1 (1919) |\n| séance, moved from the near-miss lexicon | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n\n- **Together:** 17 articles, so about **161,000 more candidates (+15%)**, and they catch **3 of the 4**. About 18% of what they add are reports (6–41%), against 3.5% for v1's candidates.\n- Their other matches are spiritualism mentioned in passing, announced spiritualist meetings, a stage exposé of mediums, and \"medium\" in other senses.\n- Left out: materialize, which matched 7 articles and no report. No term catches the 1918 scout's \"spirits\".\n- **Cost:** a full re-read, 118.6 min and 340.4 GB at four processes (decision 25), because the near-miss pool is stored only as samples. The reader's job would grow from 1.05 million candidates to about 1.21 million.\n- The estimates rest on 3 reports in 17 articles, so the intervals are wide.\n\n## Interpretation calls, yours to overrule\n\n- I counted the 1898 spirit-control defence and the 1918 felt presence as reports. Without them, the figures are the \"2 clear reports\" line above. I added both kinds of claim to the 2C `POLICY.md` item.\n- The screen reads near-miss terms as well as flag words.\n- The non-candidate estimate pools the 38,600 screened with eval A's 300, which were read in full. The near-miss estimate pools the 713 with eval A's 300.\n\n## Plan items added (`BUILD-PLAN.md`)\n\n- **2B reprint evaluation set:** sample per candidate as well as per cluster. Eval A's check drew clusters uniformly, so 11 of its 20 pairs came from 2-printing clusters, and none of the 157 clusters of 50 or more printings was checked. I verified both numbers. The largest clusters also get a hand check for chaining.\n- **2C `POLICY.md`:** one rule for delusions and hallucinations, with your 1904 and 1909 examples (I verified both verdicts), and also for spirit control and a felt presence of the dead.\n- **2C eval B:** the reader screens the random reservoirs, for a bound that doesn't rest on flag words. The Tinker credit check sets its size.\n\n## Also\n\n- `eval_a.json` has `ocr_quality.dictionary`: sha256 `be41ad97963bf8dabedd5871d5d691596175269d540956b0f9965a885c2bbab9`, 235,976 words (`wc -l`).\n- These files are byte-identical to before:\n  - `recall.json`\n  - `reprint-check.json`\n  - the other seven charts\n- The Phase 2A STATUS block in `RESUME.md` cites your spot check, seed \"EM spot-check 2A\". \"Next step\" now points at 2B, with v2 waiting on you.\n- Staged-diff scans:\n  - Commit 1 named `ULTRAMARINE_BASE_URL`. The match was the word \"instruments.\" in the 1913 White Plains court report in `screen.json`, which shares 12 characters with that URL's domain, so it's a false positive. I checked it without printing the value.\n  - Commit 2 was clean.\n  - Machine name: 0 in both.\n- No loc.gov requests in this step.\n","numLines":116,"startLine":1,"totalLines":116}},"displayPath":"../../../../../Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.7.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"9fdd2570-9d90-40b8-bd21-a776149d7f7d","timestamp":"2026-10-30T16:50:04.261Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.7.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# Step 2A.7: DONE\n2\t\n3\tOn `phase-2a-archive`, pushed:\n4\t- `1426fd8`: the screen, its results and decision 30\n5\t- `36e20e3`: the STATUS block and the new 2B and 2C plan items\n6\t\n7\t**PR #2** into main: https://github.com/Hotragn/Paperghosts/pull/2. When it opened, its 3 CI checks were pending and GitHub showed it as mergeable. Decision 30 has the detail. `make eval` takes 42.5 s and peaks at 4.0 GB. 160 tests pass, and `make lint` exits 0.\n8\t\n9\t## The draw\n10\t\n11\t- 38,600 more articles from the random reservoirs, seed \"eval A screen\", each year in proportion to its non-candidates, across 164 years.\n12\t- None of eval A's 300 random articles is drawn again, so together they are 38,900 distinct articles.\n13\t- The largest draw is 1914's: 950 of the 1,985 articles left in its reservoir. 1910 drew 940 of 1,997, against the 958 its share predicts. The per-year counts are random draws, so they scatter around their shares.\n14\t- `draw()` now asserts when a reservoir is smaller than its year's share, and the message names the year. I wrote the test first and watched it fail. The existing draws are unchanged, because the RNG calls are the same.\n15\t\n16\t## The screen\n17\t\n18\t- An article is read if its headline or text holds one of eval A's flag words **or a near-miss term**, once line-break hyphens are joined.\n19\t  - The near-miss terms only add to the flags. So every near-miss article in the draw gets read, and the near-miss estimate doesn't rest on flag words.\n20\t- 1,284 of the 38,600