{"parentUuid":"29731560-e325-4396-90e3-f65d3980e696","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"400ad373-d924-47cf-8e2d-d61155c6ff44","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GYUap2QKSomLRkbiE1JjtE","type":"tool_result","content":"5:## Before Step 1 (2026-10-29)\n34:## Planning (2026-10-29)\n57:## Phase 1 (2026-10-29)\n83:- 9 of the 20 matching 1850 pages have no `raw_data_loc`, and their size is marked approximate. On the Lewistown page the record says 5867 × 8352 while the JP2 is 6337 × 8901: the \"approximate\" size is just the extent of the boxes, which are already in the JP2's pixels. The crop matched its text from the first line to the last.\n86:## Phase 2A (2026-10-29)\n88:**19. Lexicon v1 counts the 1850s spirit noises as ghost terms, and reads through common OCR damage.**\n89:- Only 3 of the 20 Stratford knockings articles of 1850 use a classic ghost word, and the walking skeleton's article uses none. So \"knockings\", \"rappings\" and \"rappers\" are ghost terms. A single \"knocking\", \"spirits\" and \"strange noises\" put an article in the near-miss pool instead.\n91:- Recall comes first. On faro_1850, v1 keeps 2,954 of 733,880 articles (`scripts/prefilter_year.py 1850`), and 18 of the 20 known knockings articles; the other 2 are in the near-miss pool.\n93:**20. The knocking term stays: on its own it keeps 293 candidates of 1850, and 11 of the 18 known knockings articles.**\n99:- Folding the OCR classes to one letter lets 34 short stems stand for every pattern, and an article holding none of them skips the regex. Over faro_1850 the pre-check changes no article's terms (`scripts/verify_precheck.py 1850`), and the year takes 46 s instead of 98 s.\n100:- The near-miss pool and all non-candidates are each kept as a seeded reservoir of 2,000 articles per year, in `data/samples/{year}.jsonl.zst`: 4.8 MB for 1850.\n106:- 1804, 1850 and 1910 took 2.7 s, 50.6 s and 389.4 s, wrote 39 MB in all, and peaked at 536 MB of memory (1910). A second run of 1850 wrote byte-identical counts. The code version hashes exact bytes, so even a reformat re-runs a year: format before an ingest.\n122:- Each year process is CPU-bound on one core: 12.8 MB/s at the median over the 87 years above 1 GB, with a range of 11.0–15.6. With four running, the network carried 55 MB/s at the median of minute samples (50–57 for the middle 80%), and 43% of the CPU stayed idle (35–45%). Alone, a year runs nearly twice as fast: 1850 took 46.8 s by itself and 85.7 s beside three others.\n131:- 1850 reproduces. With its candidates file moved aside, a rerun wrote all three outputs byte-identical, with the same sha256 as the committed manifest.\n134:- There is one IIIF crop per box in `bbox_list`, in its order, which is the reading order. This replaces decision 18's single crop over every box, which takes in the columns between them on a multi-column article. In 64 of 595 multi-box 1850 articles a later box starts higher on the page, so sorting boxes by height would break the order.\n211:- 335,907 candidates (32.0%) have a scan with no batch path, so the IIIF service for their page has to come from somewhere other than `raw_data_loc`. The count for each year is in `eval_a.json` (`by_year[].no_batch_path`). By decade, the share runs from 15% in the 1820s to 37% in the 1850s. It is between 24% and 37% in every decade from the 1840s on.\n989144629 94.8 3393 {'candidates': 'f4f1477f1ce70851fa8cf5aeda7830e91be1fd72f0341a62f2031166b830b0cb', 'samples': '43bd2d7943769f0f13754c3102c1f517604351737365e6ddc14aa47908bf9b8f', 'counts': '3647043a857694ee28f1fd2d91b610267c322f8cb3632bde7fdd436ae8c8c221'}","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"90ea36c7-dd37-49a2-b279-c4aa8899b9c4","timestamp":"2026-10-30T16:51:06.944Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"5:## Before Step 1 (2026-10-29)\n34:## Planning (2026-10-29)\n57:## Phase 1 (2026-10-29)\n83:- 9 of the 20 matching 1850 pages have no `raw_data_loc`, and their size is marked approximate. On the Lewistown page the record says 5867 × 8352 while the JP2 is 6337 × 8901: the \"approximate\" size is just the extent of the boxes, which are already in the JP2's pixels. The crop matched its text from the first line to the last.\n86:## Phase 2A (2026-10-29)\n88:**19. Lexicon v1 counts the 1850s spirit noises as ghost terms, and reads through common OCR damage.**\n89:- Only 3 of the 20 Stratford knockings articles of 1850 use a classic ghost word, and the walking skeleton's article uses none. So \"knockings\", \"rappings\" and \"rappers\" are ghost terms. A single \"knocking\", \"spirits\" and \"strange noises\" put an article in the near-miss pool instead.\n91:- Recall comes first. On faro_1850, v1 keeps 2,954 of 733,880 articles (`scripts/prefilter_year.py 1850`), and 18 of the 20 known knockings articles; the other 2 are in the near-miss pool.\n93:**20. The knocking term stays: on its own it keeps 293 candidates of 1850, and 11 of the 18 known knockings articles.**\n99:- Folding the OCR classes to one letter lets 34 short stems stand for every pattern, and an article holding none of them skips the regex. Over faro_1850 the pre-check changes no article's terms (`scripts/verify_precheck.py 1850`), and the year takes 46 s instead of 98 s.\n100:- The near-miss pool and all non-candidates are each kept as a seeded reservoir of 2,000 articles per year, in `data/samples/{year}.jsonl.zst`: 4.8 MB for 1850.\n106:- 1804, 1850 and 1910 took 2.7 s, 50.6 s and 389.4 s, wrote 39 MB in all, and peaked at 536 MB of memory (1910). A second run of 1850 wrote byte-identical counts. The code version hashes exact bytes, so even a reformat re-runs a year: format before an ingest.\n122:- Each year process is CPU-bound on one core: 12.8 MB/s at the median over the 87 years above 1 GB, with a range of 11.0–15.6. With four running, the network carried 55 MB/s at the median of minute samples (50–57 for the middle 80%), and 43% of the CPU stayed idle (35–45%). Alone, a year runs nearly twice as fast: 1850 took 46.8 s by itself and 85.7 s beside three others.\n131:- 1850 reproduces. With its candidates file moved aside, a rerun wrote all three outputs byte-identical, with the same sha256 as the committed manifest.\n134:- There is one IIIF crop per box in `bbox_list`, in its order, which is the reading order. This replaces decision 18's single crop over every box, which takes in the columns between them on a multi-column article. In 64 of 595 multi-box 1850 articles a later box starts higher on the page, so sorting boxes by height would break the order.\n211:- 335,907 candidates (32.0%) have a scan with no batch path, so the IIIF service for their page has to come from somewhere other than `raw_data_loc`. The count for each year is in `eval_a.json` (`by_year[].no_batch_path`). By decade, the share runs from 15% in the 1820s to 37% in the 1850s. It is between 24% and 37% in every decade from the 1840s on.\n989144629 94.8 3393 {'candidates': 'f4f1477f1ce70851fa8cf5aeda7830e91be1fd72f0341a62f2031166b830b0cb', 'samples': '43bd2d7943769f0f13754c3102c1f517604351737365e6ddc14aa47908bf9b8f', 'counts': '3647043a857694ee28f1fd2d91b610267c322f8cb3632bde7fdd436ae8c8c221'}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"52dc18b0-afe0-402f-83a4-7c2c6b715976","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","root":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","branch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":363,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":2},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"}]