{"parentUuid":"4b6e4418-6d36-4316-b507-d07e6a3dcc81","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgYmVWLU9o58f2mxJ2wNm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SJxFwzce2GbG26Tffrc4Cq","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/DECISIONS.md","offset":221,"limit":49},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1019,"cache_read_input_tokens":122745,"output_tokens":140,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1019,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":140,"cache_read_input_tokens":122745,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1019,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1019},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01SJxFwzce2GbG26Tffrc4Cq":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/DECISIONS.md","offset":221,"limit":49}},"serverClassifierRequest":"65d5c16c-10a2-4e7f-81b1-4608e6ed0a32","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgYmVVXMPUiEtqVDEnHU7","requestRef":"3b48f184-0f1f-4a51-8765-3e5bb024370a","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"7e55e815-15a2-43ff-81ce-4b91132a6e69","timestamp":"2026-10-30T16:51:47.031Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"7e55e815-15a2-43ff-81ce-4b91132a6e69","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"400ad373-d924-47cf-8e2d-d61155c6ff44","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SJxFwzce2GbG26Tffrc4Cq","type":"tool_result","content":"221\t**30. A flag-word screen of 38,600 more non-candidates found 4 ghost reports, all spiritualist and all saying \"spirit\". Lexicon v1 finds about half the reports, and lexicon v2 is proposed but not run.**\n222\t- The draw (`make eval`, seed \"eval A screen\"):\n223\t  - 38,600 more articles from the random reservoirs, each year in proportion to its non-candidates. None of eval A's 300 is drawn again, so with them there are 38,900.\n224\t  - The largest year draw is 1914's: 950 of the 1,985 articles left in its reservoir. 1910 has the largest expected share, 958.\n225\t  - `draw()` now fails when a reservoir is smaller than its year's share, instead of quietly drawing fewer.\n226\t- The screen reads an article only if its headline or text holds one of eval A's flag words or a near-miss term, once line-break hyphens are joined. 1,284 do (3.3%), and 713 of those are near-miss articles. `eval/results/eval_a/screen.json` holds each one with its opening and up to six windows.\n227\t- The reading:\n228\t  - Eight reading agents judged the hits by decision 27, 160 or 161 each, split by seed. Their first reading was 1,274 no, 8 close calls and 2 reports.\n229\t  - I re-judged a seeded 10% of their negatives (127, seed \"eval A screen check\") from the same text, and agreed on all 127 (95% interval 97.1–100%).\n230\t  - I re-read every close call and report in full.\n231\t- The 4 reports:\n232\t  - 1856, Bordentown (the Grand River times, from the Trenton Gazette): spiritualists marry a dead man's corpse to his fiancée, on directions they say his spirit gave.\n233\t  - 1919, the New-York Tribune: Mrs. Curran says the spirit Patience speaks through her as a medium.\n234\t  - Borderline, 1898, the Abilene weekly reflector: a wife murderer's defence will be that he was under the control of spirits, with spiritualist mediums to testify. It is counted like eval A's 1904 courtroom spectre.\n235\t  - Borderline, 1918, the New-York Tribune: an Australian scout writes that he has felt a touch on the shoulder with no one there, and thinks the spirits of No Man's Land's unburied dead hover near.\n236\t  - All 4 are near-miss articles, and in each the only near-miss term is spirit. Their other words: spiritualists, spiritualistic, medium(s), disembodied, ouija board, and control of spirits.\n237\t  - Not counted:\n238\t    - living witches' shapes in testimony from 1692\n239\t    - the devil driven out of a woman\n240\t    - a psychical researcher's own spirit visiting Mars\n241\t    - the belief that a dead baby's spirit stays with its mother\n242\t    - a fictional cabman's passenger who is Aaron Burr\n243\t    - a dialect sketch\n244\t- Estimates (`eval_a.json`, `recall.screen`; `recall.svg` now shows them):\n245\t  - **Non-candidates:** 4 in 38,900, so 0.010% (0.0040–0.026%), or about 38,000 (14,800–97,600) of 369,263,454 articles.\n246\t  - **Near-miss pool:** 4 in 1,013 (the 713 drawn and eval A's 300), so 0.39% (0.15–1.0%), or about 27,100 (10,600–69,500) of 6,872,539.\n247\t  - **Recall:**\n248\t    - The candidates hold about 36,800 reports (decision 27), so lexicon v1 finds about 49% of them.\n249\t    - With the most reports the interval allows outside the candidates, it finds at least 27%.\n250\t    - Across the candidates' own interval (17,900–74,000), the floor runs from 16% to 43%.\n251\t  - Counting only the 2 clear reports gives about 19,000 missed (5,200–69,200), so recall would be about 66%, and at least 35%.\n252\t- **Caveat:** the screen sees only the reports that hold a flag word or a near-miss term.\n253\t  - With their lexicon terms masked, 5 of eval A's 7 reports still hold one (71%, 36–92%).\n254\t  - The two that don't are the 1904 courtroom spectre and the 1916 Peking legation, which calls its ghost a \"shade\".\n255\t  - So the screen may miss about 3 in 10 of the reports outside the candidates (2 in 7). If it does, the true number is about 1.4 times these estimates.\n256\t- **Lexicon v2, proposed to the EM and not run.** Each candidate term was measured on the 38,900 judged articles:\n257\t\n258\t  | Term | Articles in 38,900 | Candidates added | Reports caught |\n259\t  |---|---|---|---|\n260\t  | spiritualism, spiritualist(s), spiritualistic | 8 | ~76,000 | 2 |\n261\t  | spirit(s) and medium(s) in one article | 8 | ~76,000 | 2 |\n262\t  | spirit world, land, control, message, manifestation, circle or photograph; control of or by spirits; disembodied; spirits of the dead or departed | 5 | ~47,000 | 2 |\n263\t  | ouija, planchette | 1 | ~9,500 | 1 |\n264\t  | séance, moved from the near-miss lexicon | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n265\t\n266\t  - Together they match 17 articles: about 161,000 more candidates (+15%), and they catch 3 of the 4 reports. About 18% of what they add are reports (6–41%), against 3.5% for v1.\n267\t  - Left out: materialize, which matched 7 articles and no report. No term catches the 1918 scout's \"spirits\".\n268\t  - Cost: a full re-read takes 118.6 min and 340.4 GB of downloads (decision 25). The reader's job would grow to about 1.21 million candidates.\n269\t- `eval_a.json` now records `/usr/share/dict/words`'s sha256 (`be41ad97963bf8da…`) and its 235,976 words, so the OCR-quality figure can be checked on another machine."}]},"uuid":"99ed7497-efe0-46d2-9b50-ad7cdeb4f34d","timestamp":"2026-10-30T16:51:47.050Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/DECISIONS.md","content":"**30. A flag-word screen of 38,600 more non-candidates found 4 ghost reports, all spiritualist and all saying \"spirit\". Lexicon v1 finds about half the reports, and lexicon v2 is proposed but not run.**\n- The draw (`make eval`, seed \"eval A screen\"):\n  - 38,600 more articles from the random reservoirs, each year in proportion to its non-candidates. None of eval A's 300 is drawn again, so with them there are 38,900.\n  - The largest year draw is 1914's: 950 of the 1,985 articles left in its reservoir. 1910 has the largest expected share, 958.\n  - `draw()` now fails when a reservoir is smaller than its year's share, instead of quietly drawing fewer.\n- The screen reads an article only if its headline or text holds one of eval A's flag words or a near-miss term, once line-break hyphens are joined. 1,284 do (3.3%), and 713 of those are near-miss articles. `eval/results/eval_a/screen.json` holds each one with its opening and up to six windows.\n- The reading:\n  - Eight reading agents judged the hits by decision 27, 160 or 161 each, split by seed. Their first reading was 1,274 no, 8 close calls and 2 reports.\n  - I re-judged a seeded 10% of their negatives (127, seed \"eval A screen check\") from the same text, and agreed on all 127 (95% interval 97.1–100%).\n  - I re-read every close call and report in full.\n- The 4 reports:\n  - 1856, Bordentown (the Grand River times, from the Trenton Gazette): spiritualists marry a dead man's corpse to his fiancée, on directions they say his spirit gave.\n  - 1919, the New-York Tribune: Mrs. Curran says the spirit Patience speaks through her as a medium.\n  - Borderline, 1898, the Abilene weekly reflector: a wife murderer's defence will be that he was under the control of spirits, with spiritualist mediums to testify. It is counted like eval A's 1904 courtroom spectre.\n  - Borderline, 1918, the New-York Tribune: an Australian scout writes that he has felt a touch on the shoulder with no one there, and thinks the spirits of No Man's Land's unburied dead hover near.\n  - All 4 are near-miss articles, and in each the only near-miss term is spirit. Their other words: spiritualists, spiritualistic, medium(s), disembodied, ouija board, and control of spirits.\n  - Not counted:\n    - living witches' shapes in testimony from 1692\n    - the devil driven out of a woman\n    - a psychical researcher's own spirit visiting Mars\n    - the belief that a dead baby's spirit stays with its mother\n    - a fictional cabman's passenger who is Aaron Burr\n    - a dialect sketch\n- Estimates (`eval_a.json`, `recall.screen`; `recall.svg` now shows them):\n  - **Non-candidates:** 4 in 38,900, so 0.010% (0.0040–0.026%), or about 38,000 (14,800–97,600) of 369,263,454 articles.\n  - **Near-miss pool:** 4 in 1,013 (the 713 drawn and eval A's 300), so 0.39% (0.15–1.0%), or about 27,100 (10,600–69,500) of 6,872,539.\n  - **Recall:**\n    - The candidates hold about 36,800 reports (decision 27), so lexicon v1 finds about 49% of them.\n    - With the most reports the interval allows outside the candidates, it finds at least 27%.\n    - Across the candidates' own interval (17,900–74,000), the floor runs from 16% to 43%.\n  - Counting only the 2 clear reports gives about 19,000 missed (5,200–69,200), so recall would be about 66%, and at least 35%.\n- **Caveat:** the screen sees only the reports that hold a flag word or a near-miss term.\n  - With their lexicon terms masked, 5 of eval A's 7 reports still hold one (71%, 36–92%).\n  - The two that don't are the 1904 courtroom spectre and the 1916 Peking legation, which calls its ghost a \"shade\".\n  - So the screen may miss about 3 in 10 of the reports outside the candidates (2 in 7). If it does, the true number is about 1.4 times these estimates.\n- **Lexicon v2, proposed to the EM and not run.** Each candidate term was measured on the 38,900 judged articles:\n\n  | Term | Articles in 38,900 | Candidates added | Reports caught |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | spiritualism, spiritualist(s), spiritualistic | 8 | ~76,000 | 2 |\n  | spirit(s) and medium(s) in one article | 8 | ~76,000 | 2 |\n  | spirit world, land, control, message, manifestation, circle or photograph; control of or by spirits; disembodied; spirits of the dead or departed | 5 | ~47,000 | 2 |\n  | ouija, planchette | 1 | ~9,500 | 1 |\n  | séance, moved from the near-miss lexicon | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n\n  - Together they match 17 articles: about 161,000 more candidates (+15%), and they catch 3 of the 4 reports. About 18% of what they add are reports (6–41%), against 3.5% for v1.\n  - Left out: materialize, which matched 7 articles and no report. No term catches the 1918 scout's \"spirits\".\n  - Cost: a full re-read takes 118.6 min and 340.4 GB of downloads (decision 25). The reader's job would grow to about 1.21 million candidates.\n- `eval_a.json` now records `/usr/share/dict/words`'s sha256 (`be41ad97963bf8da…`) and its 235,976 words, so the OCR-quality figure can be checked on another machine.","numLines":49,"startLine":221,"totalLines":270}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7e55e815-15a2-43ff-81ce-4b91132a6e69","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
