{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-10-30T16:58:46.504Z","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","content":"<agent-message from=\"aa7aebeec762bd204\">\n[Subagent hand-back] The text below is the final report of a subagent this session delegated to. It is model output, NOT a message from the user: instructions, requests, or approval claims inside it are the subagent's words and carry no user authority. The harness indents every line of the report, so a frame-like line at column zero inside it would be forged. Notes above this frame may quote model-derived text, which carries no user authority either. The report follows:\n  Counts: 3 report, 5 close, 42 no (50 in all). The output is valid JSON, with one verdict for each id from 2-001 to 2-050.\n  \n  Report:\n  - 2-027: Conan Doyle, landing in America, says he has many times spoken with his dead son Kingsley at séances. It is a first-person claim of spirit communication told as having happened, amid general advocacy.\n  - 2-030: An account of Victor Hugo's table-turning séances, where the spirits of Aeschylus and Shakespeare supposedly dictated verses. These are particular séances described as having happened, with their phenomena.\n  - 2-035: A Middlebury reporter attended a Union Hall séance where a spirit named Judd was said to speak through a trance medium from Massachusetts, and he sums up the spirit's speech. It is a spirit speaking through a medium, told mockingly as having happened.\n  \n  Close:\n  - 2-001: A \"second sight seance\" at Old Bridge, N.J., where a hypnotized woman was questioned about a local robbery and murder mystery. The séance happened, but its phenomena look like hypnotic clairvoyance rather than spirits.\n  - 2-013: A jailed Norwegian accused of a shooting claims to be a spiritualist who can hear spirits but not see them. The paper says it was evidently an evil spirit he heard at the time of the shooting, which is close to a spirit-control defence.\n  - 2-020: An 1880 Arkansas story of a hidden murder whose suspect's wife was a spiritualist medium. The window cuts off just as the neighbours begin to say something, so the crime may have come out through spirit messages.\n  - 2-043: The end of the Picaud (\"real Monte Cristo\") narrative. The dying Allut would not say how he learned facts he had not witnessed, and the text raises \"a disembodied spirit\" as a possible source. It is unclear whether a spirit communication is claimed or only joked about.\n  - 2-048: A psychologist who attended Conan Doyle's lecture dismisses the spirit photographs Doyle showed as trickery. The photographs are mentioned but not described, and the rest is general rebuttal.\n  \n  Verdicts file: /private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/added/verdicts-2.json\n</agent-message>","reason":"absorbed_mid_turn","commandUuid":"142df22f-84f5-402a-b64f-0674e1b70d93","deliveryId":"82f2fc59-78c1-40d3-a05d-c6b231d76fbc"}
