ecornizc ccrtain quality Of truth, and who may be pro- \n\n##### 4-021 | 1869-10-23 | The Petroleum Centre daily record. [volume] (Petroleum Center, Pa.) 1868-1873 | terms ['spirit_phrase']\nHEADLINE: \nA family residing near Adamsville, in Mnskngum county, spiItualists, have had, they profess, communication from the spirit world, to the effect that the family, numbering some tour or five persons, will receive IA legacy Of $50 600 each. This vast legacy is located in jersey City, the estate of man named Leonard deceased. for which no persons have established their claims as righttul heirs The Adamsvll,, family assert that they are relatives of this man Leonard. Tneir faith in this spiritual revelation was so strong that recently one member Of the family went to Jersey City found The estate, and they propose now tC prove that they are the rightiul heirs O said Leonard. \n\n##### 4-003 | 1872-01-30 | Gold Hill daily news. [volume] (Gold Hill, N.T. [Nev.]) 1863-1882 | terms ['spirit+medium']\nHEADLINE: \nTIE ORIGIN OF SPIMTUALIsM.-A Vermont exchange says that among the latest to throw away Christianity and embrace Spirltualism was Elder -, who lived not n thousand miles from Waterford, (Vt). The elder had n daughter who struggled hard against the influence, but finally gave up and became q writing medium. Ono day, while wrestling hard with the spirit, she prayed that If Spirltuallsm was Gods work it might be manifested If not, that tho name Of its parents might be given. Imagine her surprise, on recovering from her trance, to find written out In brief, \"Ths Devil\" \n\n##### 4-006 | 1873-09-01 | Delaware Republican. (Wilmington, Del.) 1843-1874 | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: \nceased brother of quaker lady then presented his credentials, which affected her very much A young lady who died with the consumption, came forth with two wreatbs, one of which she placed on the head Of a lady in front of the doc tor, and the other she retained. The consumptive, however, was not recognized. Many other spirits were roaming around, among which was Owen Lovejoy's, but we have not space to enunerale. \"Well be gathered Home\" was sang with good effect. The circle was then broken, when John Jackson made lengthy speech in favor oflhe doctrine Of spiritualism, and made some very good points. Spiritualism, he asserted en-. ters in to all things animate and inanimate-and that there was no nonsense,no tomfooling about spiritual life, and deprecated, In very strong terms the sacredo,al power which had ruled the world for so many huc dreds of years. The comic song of \"CourtiJg two Ladies'\" by Mr.. R0wbOtham elected thunders of applause. Mr.. IL. X. jackson and his two social and agreea' bic daughters sang very sweelly \"Tominy don't Co. The audience were highly amused by listen ing TO and witnessing the \"Quaker Cour tship, by III. X. jackson and daughter. The balance Of the day was spent in dancing and pleasant conversation. The excursion was 4 success in the literal meaning Of the term every one present enjoyed themselves TO their hearts content; not murmur Of dissatisfaction was eXt tfessed by solitary individual present, and the Committee deserve, and no doubt will receive, the praise their efforts 10 please entile them 10. On no excursion that we have ever been, were the entertainments so varied, enjoy able and rational. Tho day was a lovely one nature lad on her gayest dress of green, and every one appreciated the pains she had taken to look her best. We arrived home jnst at twilight not at all sorry That We accepte'. the in vitalion to so on the excursion Of the Spiritualists and Liberaist. \n\n##### 4-018 | 1893-03-30 | Crawford avalanche. (Grayling, Mich.) 1879-19?? | terms ['spirit_phrase', 'spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: None Other Genuine.\nThe grave announcement is made by a spiritualistic periodical that it has secured tho exclusive\" collaboration of William Shakespeare in the spirit world, and that any alleged communities tons from t