{"parentUuid":"dda99f0f-511c-4160-af52-361689f1374f","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4acd64b9-626e-4904-824b-5a9167cf4217","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EEwZcH8RfrMY8PtehscHEn","type":"tool_result","content":"##### 4-028 | 1890-02-21 | The daily citizen. [volume] (Asheville, N.C.) 1889-1890 | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: A FEW NEWS ITEMS.\nA theatrical performance given in Ne, , York for the benefit of Mrs. James { Blaine, jr.. nettcd $4,000. The formal opening Of the Carnegie hrnry took place at Pittshurg on he 1871 President Harrison was present. Bishop O'Connor. of Omaha. is lyin III Mt the Mercy hospital, Pittsburg. an all hope of his recovery has been abar doned. | A woman in Shelbyville, named Molli Corwin. has just been married to he ninth husband. Six Of the old ones ar living. A winter resort for invalids has bee opened at Biskra, in the southern part d, Algeria, on the borders Of the dessert q Sahara. party Of spiritualists, male and fc male. were recently arrested in Chicago charged with obtaining money undr false prctense. A bill has been reported favorably i, the senate to appropriate annually iO six years 81, 100000 for a deep watc harbor at Galveston. Eight million dollars have already bee i'aid out in pensions this month, SO fa md It is expected that ten millions MOI will go the same way. Johnstown. Pa. continues to provid, sensations. Three men. on the 1911 walking on railroad track. were ru 'vcr by fast train of the Chicago lin tied express and killed. James Stuart. aged 80. has just bcc, ,dmitted to the infrinary at Hamilton Ohio. He claims to be lincal desccn, mt of the house of Stuart, and n kin, man of fQueen Victoria. During a prayer meeting at a priva, house in Lebanon. Ind. some one fired 'hot through the window. striking Mr. Randall, wife of the owner Of the houS, n the eye. and wounding her fatally. The Queen has announced that 81 will pntronizc the Carl Rosa opera Sc, SON ut Drury Lane. and has ordered IT, royal box to be reserved. This will l., her first visit to the opera since the deaf If Prince Albert. The Cronin suspect Smith, who has :, Gently been captured and who gives HI name as J.. B, Kelly. is identified as IT,, man who drove the famous white hnr>. Ind who took Or. Crouin to his death q the Carlson cottage. The female seminary ut San Marco, Texas, thirty miles south of Austin. wa, burned OIL the night of the 18th. Thr :irls were seriously burned. The other escaped by jumping from the window md some were badly hurt in doing SO. An execution recently took place i Pekin in which fourteen criminals su fercd death. They were dressed in r, aud their faces were painted red. Mi we're bcheaded ami FIVE were strangled and the bodies Of all were left on IT, ground for the birds Of prey. Marcus Marx. member of n who,csal clothing store. in Chicago while nsccndin m nm elevator attempted tojump off At Il, sixth floor, hut was caught betwe 4 side oft hc shaft and he elevator, was terr bly, crushed. and then fell to the floor b, meath. distance oi scvcnty.hve feet. In Erie. Pa, on the night Of the 18th. Young lady. Miss Emma Fischer. wen to the door in response to a summon, when a masked man threw a quantity vitriol in her face. Dennis Carty. tn vounC In iy's accepted lover, rushed on in pursuit. and ovcrtaking the miscr, ont' Was sho. thro\"En t\"e oody ana lei ana 'he party escaped The National Womau's Suffrage ASS. ciation is now in session in Washington A resolution introduced demands nn only recognition OF all rights in I he Stat, but also in the church: and among otbc things the right TO fiji the offices II eide \n\n##### 4-023 | 1897-01-02 | The morning times. | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: \nton, was called to this city yesterday to attend female resident Of the First ward, who was in a cataleptic state. While il this condition she prophesied that u portion of this city would he, destroyed in a few days. She said that on next Tuesday Mount vesuvius would be in eruption, causing earthquakes and great tidal wave, which would sweep across the Atlantic, destroy ing large portion Of the coast. A large portion OF this city, she said. would be wiped out, and the same thing would occur in New York city; Italy would be destroyed, the Isthmus Of Panama and part of Mexico would be swept away, and the Atlantic and Pacific will be united She stated that Washington school build ing in this city would be among the struclures that would escape destruction, and advised everyone to go there. When she recovered from the trance, she said that satan had tried to prevent her from making these things known, but she had told them out of love for the people. The lady had been suffering from nervous ness, and Dr.. Mindberg, who is known as u spiritualist, was summoned for advice 1t was while under mis- hypnotic power that size made the prophecies. II is said the lady's condition was brought about by reading sensational prophetic story pub lished in 4 New York paper. Alexandria Council, Junior Order of American Mechanics, elected the following officers for the ensuing year: Junior past councillor, B. F, Warder; councillor, L. E Butts; vice councillor, W. q. Simpson, recording secretary, James R. Mansfield; sistant recording secretary, c. H Pickin; financial secretary, Frank Elliott; treas urer, j. r. y. Curtin; conductor, j. Robert TraveTs; warden, H. II. Simpson; inside sentinel, Frank PeveriI; outside sentinel, George Penn: trustee for eighteen months. O. F. baggott; representative To F 3. A, j. r. y. Curtin. These officers will be installed next Thursday by Deputy State Councillor Charles H. Pickin. Yesterday was one of the quietest New Years Days ever experienced in this city. The public offices and banks were closed and Sunday hours We're observed at the postoffice. a few business houses closed during the afternoon. But few persons kept open house. The city engineer has commenced the work OF Improvement on North Columbus street. This street is one of the best graded in the city and is connecting link with Mount Vernon avenue, III the county. 1t win be macadamiZed and made beau tiful driveway. When this work is COM pleted it is proposed TO improve other unpaEed streets in the city in similar manner. At an early hour yesterday morning Lieut.. Smith, with Officers Bettis, Wilkinson, Lyies and Davis, raided disUrderly house conducted by Lewis Solomon, coiored. on the east side of Alfred street, between Duke and Prince streets. The inmates, six men and three women, were charged with indecent conduct in the police court and were fined So each. This same house was raidcd a few months ago and has been much COM planned OF by .le residents OF the neigh borl0od. Tie remains OF Mr.. George Sutton, who diedin Anacosta on Tuesday, were brought to this city yesterday and inferred. Y Peverill, residing on Wilkes street. was painfully scalded on Thursday night. The | child threw a glass of water in an open stove. Rev Frank P Berkley of this city has accepted a call to the paStorate Of the Baptist Church at Flint Hill, Rappahanmock county, and will take charge OF his new field OF labor ON the esd inst. Miss Bette Keys and Mrs. Mason OF | the Southern Bell Telephone Exchange We're | handsomely remembered by the subscribers yesterday. Mr.. and Mrs. Warren Taylor of Ricii mond are the guests OF Mr.. winiani II. AIarbury on Prince street. | Mr.. aud Mrs. George >. French have is | sued cards for q reception on the sth in. slant, at their residence, on Alfred street. on this occasion Miss Mamie French Will make her debut. Miss Sarah Daingerfield is the guest Uf Miss Lennon. in Baltimore. Mr.. Charles Collins is critically i'll, az his home, on Prince street. Mr.. William N McVeigb, of this city, is | quite sick in Richmond, where he is visit | ing Mr.. G. Powell Hill. Miss Anna Leadbeater has returned from a visit to Philadelphia. I Mrs. o. F. Carter has gone to Orange | n. j., to visit her mother. | Mr.. stephen Cowling, who has been under treatment in this city for some time, has | returned to his home, at Anandale. | William Motley, colored, was taken into custody yesterday afternoon by Officers Sherwood. Beach, .and Proctor, and is de | tanned at the police station for exa nina tion as to his mental condition. \n\n##### 4-025 | 1924-08-30 | The broad ax. [volume] (Salt Lake City, Utah) 1895-19?? | terms ['spirit_phrase', 'spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: \nballot is the. instrument placed i'll the hands Of American citizens with which to protect that rights. their lives, their property, real and personal. and with out the ballot we are helpless. There would Be more protection III our country and lynching would be sent direct to hades where il belonged if orr peopie could only vote down home and have all say in the making Of legislators. congressman and the like, then if we had some say about who shall be governor. Get the ballot i'll your hand and you have good weapon. Ii will do more good than a thousand automatics. take pleasure in saying this. But l have been knocking around Chicago a long time and when you read this letter | will be shaking some Of the Chicago dust off Of my feet and headed another way, and you can't stop me and there is no need OF you trying. There is no need Of you calling on dear spirit to stop me. Speaking Of Dear Spirit\" l find that they continue to manufacture damphules and it is being done right here in Chicago. A few days ago, c. L. Webb, that wonderful shorthand writer from Tuskegee, came and got me to take a ride with him, and you put it down that | accepted the inyitation. He toted me around by a tea room and we were invited to come back Sunday evening, and it was accepted. When it was made known, then Mrs. Evie L. Stewart, said she would like to 60, and this was followed by Mrs. Elizabeth Scott, and 10 and behold Mr. Webbs sister, who is the wife Of the tooth dentist doctor, DR.. Garnett, said she would like to 60, hence the party was formed, and we went at it, We did not all 60 together, for Mr.. Webb, his sister and two other ladies went out in their automobile car car riage and got Mrs. Stewart, and ! went for Mrs. Scott, and her husband was on hand and-he decided to 60 too. Believe me they had some fine sing ing, and when this was over, man with voice charming and sweet, made lecture on Spiritualism. He delivered a message from the people who lived in Mars. Neptune, and some other worlds. He said that they were in daily communication with them. They had visits from them. He told about their civilization, about their habits, their dress, and how near they were to God. ! just had to sit up and take notice. tell you honey, when you hear this kind Of stuff, it just makes you feel like saying-well / don't know what to call it, for think it is almost name less. 1t sounds like lot OF damphuleishness. but ! just listened. He told Of the wonderful things accomplished through his religion. He said that men were trying to do what they were doing daily, getting in touch with the people in Mars, and them other worlds 1t makes me want to say why in the hell\" don't they bring them down here and let Us see them. stood all this. and like in any 9TH er church, they took up a collection, although the speaker said that they did not ask anything from anybody. / put in the collection, and then an other collection was raised for charity. and ! put in that | said | was going to be dead game once. Next came the healing business. While the choir sang some selection, some women dressed in white, looked like they were robed for bed, walked in carrying a pitcher in one hand, towels and something else in the other. and took their places behind chairs, and the sick were asked to take seats in the chairs, and everybody were requested to hold up their right hand SO as to assist the healing spirit in doing his work. OF course T held ID my right hand with the rest. They went through some kind Of stuff, and then the persons would get Up and 60 about their business rejoicing. When this was all over, then the house was made dark, and We were told that we would get messages from the other world-not from he'll, for they said that there was no such place A. hell, but from the spirit land. All was still as a mouse, and in the room where was seated the chief woman, came a voice sound. She spoke UP and said that there was message for a woman had gone and","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"9d8bd0de-672f-4adc-b646-4e71cc515435","timestamp":"2026-10-30T16:59:46.790Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"##### 4-028 | 1890-02-21 | The daily citizen. [volume] (Asheville, N.C.) 1889-1890 | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: A FEW NEWS ITEMS.\nA theatrical performance given in Ne, , York for the benefit of Mrs. James { Blaine, jr.. nettcd $4,000. The formal opening Of the Carnegie hrnry took place at Pittshurg on he 1871 President Harrison was present. Bishop O'Connor. of Omaha. is lyin III Mt the Mercy hospital, Pittsburg. an all hope of his recovery has been abar doned. | A woman in Shelbyville, named Molli Corwin. has just been married to he ninth husband. Six Of the old ones ar living. A winter resort for invalids has bee opened at Biskra, in the southern part d, Algeria, on the borders Of the dessert q Sahara. party Of spiritualists, male and fc male. were recently arrested in Chicago charged with obtaining money undr false prctense. A bill has been reported favorably i, the senate to appropriate annually iO six years 81, 100000 for a deep watc harbor at Galveston. Eight million dollars have already bee i'aid out in pensions this month, SO fa md It is expected that ten millions MOI will go the same way. Johnstown. Pa. continues to provid, sensations. Three men. on the 1911 walking on railroad track. were ru 'vcr by fast train of the Chicago lin tied express and killed. James Stuart. aged 80. has just bcc, ,dmitted to the infrinary at Hamilton Ohio. He claims to be lincal desccn, mt of the house of Stuart, and n kin, m