{"parentUuid":"83516631-a6b7-4748-b104-e4c91a27d586","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4acd64b9-626e-4904-824b-5a9167cf4217","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0134Fzb8SRnaA2SGyp3pUmUB","type":"tool_result","content":"##### 3-012 | 1875-03-29 | The Rutland daily globe. [volume] (Rutland, Vt.) 1873-1877 | terms ['seance', 'spirit+medium', 'spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: TIE EDDYS AGAIN. /  / Recent MaterializatiOn..\nEditor Rutisnd Globe Having recent ly, several times, had my attention direct ed toward the subject of spiritualism, and more especially TO the naturalization which arc attracting SO favorable notice all over the world through the Eddy mediums. am prompted, in all kindcess, at least, to make mention of some things which have seen at Spirit Dale. ! have, in the course of three years, made as many trips TO Chittenden, to witness the phenomenon of spirits return, dressed in various CARDS, before us at every visit, al ways changing. Last Thursday evening, myself, in COM pany with two others Of my family friends were present at a seance. Fourteen other persons were included in the circle, many from distance, who witness ed the actual return of spirit frieuds. Several of them were recognized. Fourteen spirits appear ed in the front of the cabinet, In lighted room, varying in height, size, compilations, and dress. Every spirit stood neatly dress ed, from their heads to their feet, several oInamented as gay as you may find at the Opera house in Rutland. More than oDe-third spoke 10 Us distinctly. Three or four danced gracefully without producing the least noise that | could hear. The shortest spirit that came out in sight was Mary Powell, child, about four and a half feet in height The tallest spirit ans wered TO the name of Henry Powell, aud stood six feet four inches in height. The first spirit that stepped ont from the cabinet was Andrew Garter he wore suit of dark clothes, except his vest, which was pure white straight fine form, about five feet nine inches, and to appearance the size of person who would weigh 40 pounds. lie stepped out from the cabinet in full view, and remained half Il minute and then returned. As soon as he disappeared whispered 10 my son, \"That is n spirit\" He said Il want to know ] thought it was man ?\" The third spirit was an old lady Mrs. Eaton, who came out rather bowed over, and talked in fine but distinct voice for some time. She faithfully reproyed one person for coming there, and calling him by name, telling him if he had came there to arrest or cxposeth \"humbug,\" he better keep away, for the spirits did not seek to instruct those who came to make trouble, etc, etc. Fourth, John Deyine 9th, VIM. Brown and several Indians made their appearance; 9th, Witchochee; 7lh, Jocco; 9Th, San tum 9th, Amanda f; 10th, Marmon 1lth, Red Jacket. The scene closed by da- \n\n##### 3-033 | 1893-03-25 | Grand Rapids herald. [volume] (Grand Rapids, Mich.) 1892-1959 | terms ['seance']\nHEADLINE: \nWafzIxg - Tn HsR.Lp a eonan friend protevfs against allowing arr Per 80 - szpa~a The srirstisna seances = M wAte She says that the HIS. stue eoatrihutoF = unq1siifess q crit CSC ~ truth. q which b xnovs nofh Frg Il = presumed that the coutrihu sor attended Mfs Wsixs ssances earn the trnth Re ssx - many ssl sisnees = FRAUD ~ eas 10th saFsriss~i s0si =sartltsi tsosher cnmfuanaeatben sprestinE IA. morning run64nr he ssfreetnees q b. aliegstious II = 4eile evident Frnsa hcth communications fhst Ms Watt : fraud q Ia Archer -sripN \n\n##### 3-004 | 1895-05-24 | The Lebanon express. (Lebanon, Linn County, Or.) 1887-1898 | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: Waterloo VS.. spirituaIism\nEDITOR ExPRlsss; We arrived IN Waterloo Sunday the 18th inst and followed the crowd WIN appeared to have and Object point In view, arrived at Yew park AT eleven O'Clock. Here we were truly surprised to find aband Of SO called spiritualists who were assembled to worship in their peculiar manner The coming woman, we took 1t to be, addressed the multitude for some time bu. what the subject or object was, we did not learn. Later on q lady read poenl $50 called, OF her own composition, We heard few stanaas and fled, but curiosity caused us to return for the afternoon meeting that occurred at 1880, Then o gentleman who claimed, or we understood so at least, to be under the influence Of Old Sitting Bull sawed the air to a mix ture of Latin jargon and murdered English for q few minutes, after which the conglomerate mass was dissected ny q female spiritfor our edliication. Said female was adorned with a \"lotemn that looked not unlike a child's bib after said child had been dining on siewed huckleberrles. Then came more incarnations, and lady claimed to see children and old men in the audience, and this we believe true, as the children at least were painfully \n\n##### 3-029 | 1898-01-12 | The record-union. [volume] (Sacramento, Calif.) 1891-1903 | terms ['seance']\nHEADLINE: SLATER,s sEANCES. /  / He Gave His Ballot Tests to an APP /  /  / nreciative Audience.\nJohn Slater, the inimitable,\" gave another of his wonderful test seances last evening to an appreciative audi ence. Among other manifestations he gave the ballot test, or reading of sealed letters. He will repeat his seance tonight at Pythian Castle, Ninth and streets, at s o'clock. Mr.. Slater certainly hit off his answers last night to the satisfaction of his audience and most of his questioners, others being more or less embarrassed by his answers and illusions to tender subjects and episodes which elected much rilarity among the audience. In one instance a mother was told that her son, from whom she had not heard for eleven years, was not dead, but could be heard from by writing to a certain address in Melbourne, Australia. 1t is safe to say that if a letter to that address brings her a reply from her wandering boy, she will have full faith in future in the correctness Of Slater S tests. \n\n##### 3-034 | 1919-06-18 | Ellsworth American. [volume] (Ellsworth, Me.) 1855-current | terms ['ouija']\nHEADLINE: Motto for Dead Authors.\nWe are told that once, when Pa fence Worth was spelling out the end iess pages of the Sorry Tale\" she came TO sudden stop, then wrote, This be nuff,\" and knocked off for the night. A blessed phrase, and. Of a certainty, her finest inspiration. Would hat all dead authors would adopt 1t as heir motto; and with ouija boards, md table legs, and automatic pencils, write as their farewell message to the world those three short, comedy words, This be nuff.\"-Agnes Repplier. In Atlantic. \n\n##### 3-023 | 1920-07-19 | Alexandria gazette. [volume] (Alexandria, D.C.) 1834-1974 | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: PREDICT THE ELECTION OF cox\nNew York. July l9.--lt has come! Owners Of astral bodies, Auras and the l!ke--;n other words spirits have gene into politics. According to Melvin A. Fogg, of the First Spiritualist Church, of Trenron y. j.. Abraham Lincoln has sent a message back to his mortal fellow countrymen that Governor James NJ. Cox will surely be elected in November. Not only that. but this message was received before Governor Cox had been nomirated--a week before the convention opened in San Francisco.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"104f6df3-04ff-4875-9a62-9c977bd8281b","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:00:59.138Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"##### 3-012 | 1875-03-29 | The Rutland daily globe. [volume] (Rutland, Vt.) 1873-1877 | terms ['seance', 'spirit+medium', 'spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: TIE EDDYS AGAIN. /  / Recent MaterializatiOn..\nEditor Rutisnd Globe Having recent ly, several times, had my attention direct ed toward the subject of spiritualism, and more especially TO the naturalization which arc attracting SO favorable notice all over the world through the Eddy mediums. am prompted, in all kindcess, at least, to make mention of some things which have seen at Spirit Dale. ! have, in the course of three years, made as many trips TO Chittenden, to witness the phenomenon of spirits return, dressed in various CARDS, before us at every visit, al ways changing. Last Thursday evening, myself, in COM pany with two others Of my family friends were present at a seance. Fourteen other persons were included in the circle, many from distance, who witness ed the actual return of spirit frieuds. Several of them were recognized. Fourteen spirits appear ed in the front of the cabinet, In lighted room, varying in height, size, compilations, and dress. Every spirit stood neatly dress ed, from their heads to their feet, several oInamented as gay as you may find at the Opera house in Rutland. More than oDe-third spoke 10 Us distinctly. Three or four danced gracefully without producing the least noise that | could hear. The shortest spirit that came out in sight was Mary Powell, child, about four and a half feet in height The tallest spirit ans wered TO the name of Henry Powell, aud stood six feet four inches in height. The first spirit that stepped ont from the cabinet was Andrew Garter he wore suit of dark clothes, except his vest, which was pure white straight fine form, about five feet nine inches, and to appearance the size of person who would weigh 40 pounds. lie stepped out from the cabinet in full view, and remained half Il minute and then returned. As soon as he disappeared whispered 10 my son, \"That is n spirit\" He said Il want to know ] thought it was man ?\" The third spirit was an old lady Mrs. Eaton, who came out rather bowed over, and talked in fine but distinct voice for some time. She faithfully reproyed one person for coming there, and calling him by name, telling him if he had came there to arrest or cxposeth \"humbug,\" he better keep away, for the spirits did not seek to instruct those who came to make trouble, etc, etc. Fourth, John Deyine 9th, VIM. Brown and several Indians made their appearance; 9th, Witchochee; 7lh, Jocco; 9Th, San tum 9th, Amanda f; 10th, Marmon 1lth, Red Jacket. The scene closed by da- \n\n#####