-bab0-ad0681f1b133","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"3de15aaf-6049-4e5c-9d2b-4fb31455b095","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:01:17.138Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"3de15aaf-6049-4e5c-9d2b-4fb31455b095","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4acd64b9-626e-4904-824b-5a9167cf4217","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FZoYtSmd9zV4FpTGW1DmRL","type":"tool_result","content":"##### 3-003 | 1883-02-15 | The Worthington advance. [volume] (Worthington, Minn.) 1874-1908 | terms ['spirit+medium', 'spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: \nWORTHINGTON, n THE CHURCH: IN AMERICA. ''The Catholic Church has battled her whole lifetime for those rights Of man and that liberty which confer the great est glory on the American Republic\" Father IIecker. 1--WE on our part are sure that the canse of ignorance and superstition will never bs our own.'-Catholic World. IN EUROPE. II claim to he the Supreme Judge on eartl. Of the csnsciences Of mea, Of the peasant and the prince, Of the house hold and the legislature l am the last ,, Supreme Judge on earth of what is right and wrong Pope Plus, TX. '1f [ possessed the liberty I claim, I would close all Protestant schools and places Of worship in Rome. Pope Leo XIII. ''Let the public school system 60 whence it came-tO the Devil.' EDITOR ADvANCE:-The above is my excuse for sending you the enclosed verses. You may publish, them if you consider them worth it: 'Your Church has battled from the first Against these rights Of man. This liberty Of thought and speech Our fathers died to gain. These gland free SC hools, urclildren's hope ION may they flourish lOng) That gave us power to clearly see And know the right from wrong. Has ever striv'n by fraud or force, In every age and cline, To check the progress Of the world. Turn back the hands of Time. Your fair speech falls on guarded ears. We smooth tong,led Priests of Rome, We know the more We yield you now. The bolder you become Our lIist'ry's blood stained pages tell How once your Pope was God, And cruel priests in every land Ruled with an iron rod Hiding black hearts and viest crimes Beneath 4 robe Of snow; They forged the chains that bound our race -Four hundred years ago. And now that day has followed night, And peace succeeded pain, Shall we yield up these sacred rights And let you rule again: l.. Ills. 4 REPLY 10 THE JUDGE. EDITOR ADvANCE:-Your issue Of Feb. lSt has :. long article in defence of spiritualisn. There is IL long list of names in it who are represented AS being Spiritnalists. Although am not personally acquainted with any of item. will guarantee ii the question was asked them ARE you Il spiritualist, tne majority Of them v!nnld say, No. am naturally Of All inQuiring mind, and for several years; Was an investiga tor of s,'irll ual,sm in this aud other states. M.st OF the mediums with wii~m | have been acquainted have been. socially. very estilnable persons, but reliSi\"u>ly they were very unsou,d. | have known some who were Ullce good christians. that sri,ltualiSln has lnale .nural weeks OF, and are to-day break ! ing Tie laws uf Gud and man. ii;ivt you ever known anyone hurt by rea,ilns the bible aud obeying its l'r ctpts? H., We seen ard heard many spirit manifesto It ien~, us they ARE called, but lever anything which convinced nae that spirits had anything to do with it. | have heard things in the dark that we're very mysterious, which done in aylight were Ridiculous. | have seen 80 much tr!cktry; that were it not for The CONFIDENCE | place in Some med ium>, should say iC is AL trickery. F'r il person 1T Il distance to lead your paper he would think there was Il large body of Spirit ualists here. On the contrary. there are only Il few SpilituaIistS. while there are over 2CO church IN, ,nbers. The MethoMlsts, Pres!.yterians. Congregationalists. Episc@pals and LutheranS each have Il fine house Of worship. Your correspondent speaks of the great growth Of 81. iritua:ism in thirty years. When We take into consideration the diffusion of knowledge in cur day, they have made small progress compared to the growth Of fCI,rlstianity for the first thirty years after the death Of our Saviour. The bible has stood all the tests brought against it for nearly 1900 years, and the m1jority of people are more willing to trust their souls welfare to the doctrines of the bible than to the word of any medium. Where is the medium that would do for the world What Jesus did? Do the lives of Spirit ua}ists prove that their tlo' inc s bet ter than Christianity? Does their death prove it? Is your correspondent right sure 1.-THE main reason why the majority of civil lied mankind are not Spiritualists is because they decline to become acquainted with the subject\" beg leave to differ with him. The reason is, sensible people are not willing to give up Il bridge which has carried hum dreds of millions of souls to paradise, for one which, as far as anyone knows. has never landed any in 4 better land. The ''Judge\" thinks it about time men thought for themselves instead Of allowing priests and commentators ia think for them. think so too. In all my religious experience OF over 20 years | pletely led by the nose as Spiritualists are by their mediums. One of your correspondents speaks of minister coming to Worthington and getting in debt to the stores and going off in debt because those who promised to support him did not do it. If that is so. it is truly bad enough, but shall need some proof before 1 believe it. These few lines have not been written rs Il banter for an argument, bnt mere ! ly to let non-rPsidents know that all here do not think rs the Editor of the | ADVANCE thinks. W. Worth Remembering Now that good times are again upon us, it is worth rememberlng that 10 one can enjoy the ,pleasantest surroundings if in bad health. There are hundreds of miserable people going about to-day with dis | ordered stomach. liver or kidneys. when \n\n##### 3-041 | 1917-08-04 | Evening public ledger. [volume] (Philadelphia [Pa.]) 1914-1942 | terms ['ouija', 'spirit_phrase']\nHEADLINE: \n| \"eie Trying TO keep cool on the piazza. Dorothy told me you said one had been used by woman dead more than $50 summer\" The Ice was clinking in the lemonade pitcher as the glasses were filled. Dorothy was tanning herself. Ames was offering | to act as her punkah coolie. The Lady In white gown was sitting where a faint breath of air occasionally stirred the leaves on the vines. Doctor McFabre was lounging in the Gloucester hammock, as usual. sweltering among the pillows. Mrs. McFabre was urging him to take his ease in a cooler place. Where have you lived all these years, Owen, not to know anything about the ouija?\" asked Ames, turning for a moment from Dorothy. why, Dick\" said Dorothy. \"you sure | ly know all about It. We used to have | one at the house. Don't you remember\" You don't mean that fool contrivance | that had a heart.shaped board with a | pointer on one END that you made walk about another board on which the letters | OF the alphabet were printed?\" said Owen. Yes, that was it,\" said Dorothy. Sometimes there is pencil on the heartshaped board, and it writes sen. tences instead of spelling out words by pointing to the letters\" said I. when it writes 1t is sometimes called plan chette.\" What makes It mover\" Owen wanted to know. \"They tell us in st. Louis\" said I, \"where the long novel was written, that IL was moved by the spirit of the dead women working through the hands of two living women who had their hands on the board\" stuff and nonsense\" calculated Ames with a snort Of contempt. you may be right\" said I, but there are persons who have profound faith in that sort of thing. and they use the ouija board when they want to tell their own fortunes\" IT have had my fortune told by it,\" said Dorothy. what did 1t say?\" asked Ames. \"Why don't you tell her fortune by Napoleon's Oraculum?\" asked The Lady. Can you tell fortunes?\" Dorothy exclaimed eagerly. sitting up In her chair and forgetting the heat. 81 fhave a book of directions said 1, but you know I don't believe in that sort of thing\" Neither do l.\" Doctor McFabre aroused himself enough to remark. \"Napoleons Oraculum,\" said to Doro. thy, ignoring Doctor McFabre's interrupt ton, Consists of cabalistic table of six. teen columns of letters and numbers headed by sixteen astral keys There are sixteen questions to be asked, and six. teen different answers to each question. You select the question you want to ask and chance guides you to the answer. Do you want to try it?\" please let me-\" said Dorothy / took the hook from the table where it had been lying beside the lemonade pitcher and opened to the list of questions. When I handed it to the girl she studied the list while and then said she wanted to know whether she would obtain her wish. This was the first queston. gave her another book and told her to open it at random and read to me the first four words at the top of the left.hand page. She read, May l tell him.\" This was the guide to the astral KEY. As tho first word had an odd mum. ber of letters l made a single dot on piece of paper. The second word also had an odd number of letters, and l put another dot under the first one put two dots for the third word, as it had an even number of letters and single dot for | the last word. Then looked in column one and followed it horizontally till l came to a perpendicular column headed | by a combination of two single dots, a double dot and single dot. Where the | two columns crossed was the letter o. l looked at the table o, containing answers to ali the questions and there opposite | the same group of dots read this answer: Fortune shuns you at present. but will come in time\" \"Oh.h!\" sighed Dorothy Cheer up\" said Ames. That Is not | half bad\" Then in moment he added, | Let me try it,\" l gave the book to him. He studied the | questions and then, evidently with his | mind on the draft and the probability | that he would have to go TO france, ne | said, 81 choose the second question, Will | my undertakings be successful?'\" Following the same method as I EN-. | ployed in Dorothys case I found the an swer was: Remain here among your family and you will do WELL\" \"That means you won't have to leave the country\" said owen little contemptuously. Rubbish\" said Doctor McFabre. who had risen from the hammock and was now sitting on the edge. \"You don't dare try it, Owen,\" said Ames, with fine show Of indignation. LET me have the book\" and Owen reached out his hand He looked over the questions. II am going to be frank about 1t. Question number four is Shall I live In foreign partsT That is what I want TO know\"","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"4c3caebb-dde2-4ea8-95f6-1ccdf56200ae","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:01:18.783Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"##### 3-003 | 1883-02-15 | The Worthington advance. [volume] (Worthington, Minn.) 1874-1908 | terms ['spirit+medium', 'spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: \nWORTHINGTON, n THE CHURCH: IN AMERICA. ''The Catholic Church has battled her whole lifetime for those rights Of man and that liberty which confer the great est glory on the American Republic\" Father IIecker. 1--WE on our part are sure that the canse of ignorance and superstition will never bs our own.'-Catholic World. IN EUROPE. II claim to he the Supreme Judge on eartl. Of the csnsciences Of mea, Of the peasant and the prince, Of the house hold and the legislature l am the last ,, Supreme Judge on earth of what is right and wrong Pope Plus, TX. '1f [ possessed the liberty I claim, I would close all Protestant schools and places Of worship in Rome. Pope Leo XIII. ''Let the public school system 60 whence it came-tO the Devil.' EDITOR ADvANCE:-The above is my excuse for sending you the enclosed verses. You may publish, them if you consider them worth it: 'Your Church has battled from the first Against these rights Of man. This liberty Of thought and speech Our fathers died to gain. These gland free SC hools, urclildren's hope ION may they flourish lOng) That gave us power to clearly see And know the right from wrong. Has ever striv'n by fraud or force, In every a