mberlng that 10 one can enjoy the ,pleasantest surroundings if in bad health. There are hundreds of miserable people going about to-day with dis | ordered stomach. liver or kidneys. when \n\n##### 3-041 | 1917-08-04 | Evening public ledger. [volume] (Philadelphia [Pa.]) 1914-1942 | terms ['ouija', 'spirit_phrase']\nHEADLINE: \n| \"eie Trying TO keep cool on the piazza. Dorothy told me you said one had been used by woman dead more than $50 summer\" The Ice was clinking in the lemonade pitcher as the glasses were filled. Dorothy was tanning herself. Ames was offering | to act as her punkah coolie. The Lady In white gown was sitting where a faint breath of air occasionally stirred the leaves on the vines. Doctor McFabre was lounging in the Gloucester hammock, as usual. sweltering among the pillows. Mrs. McFabre was urging him to take his ease in a cooler place. Where have you lived all these years, Owen, not to know anything about the ouija?\" asked Ames, turning for a moment from Dorothy. why, Dick\" said Dorothy. \"you sure | ly know all about It. We used to have | one at the house. Don't you remember\" You don't mean that fool contrivance | that had a heart.shaped board with a | pointer on one END that you made walk about another board on which the letters | OF the alphabet were printed?\" said Owen. Yes, that was it,\" said Dorothy. Sometimes there is pencil on the heartshaped board, and it writes sen. tences instead of spelling out words by pointing to the letters\" said I. when it writes 1t is sometimes called plan chette.\" What makes It mover\" Owen wanted to know. \"They tell us in st. Louis\" said I, \"where the long novel was written, that IL was moved by the spirit of the dead women working through the hands of two living women who had their hands on the board\" stuff and nonsense\" calculated Ames with a snort Of contempt. you may be right\" said I, but there are persons who have profound faith in that sort of thing. and they use the ouija board when they want to tell their own fortunes\" IT have had my fortune told by it,\" said Dorothy. what did 1t say?\" asked Ames. \"Why don't you tell her fortune by Napoleon's Oraculum?\" asked The Lady. Can you tell fortunes?\" Dorothy exclaimed eagerly. sitting up In her chair and forgetting the heat. 81 fhave a book of directions said 1, but you know I don't believe in that sort of thing\" Neither do l.\" Doctor McFabre aroused himself enough to remark. \"Napoleons Oraculum,\" said to Doro. thy, ignoring Doctor McFabre's interrupt ton, Consists of cabalistic table of six. teen columns of letters and numbers headed by sixteen astral keys There are sixteen questions to be asked, and six. teen different answers to each question. You select the question you want to ask and chance guides you to the answer. Do you want to try it?\" please let me-\" said Dorothy / took the hook from the table where it had been lying beside the lemonade pitcher and opened to the list of questions. When I handed it to the girl she studied the list while and then said she wanted to know whether she would obtain her wish. This was the first queston. gave her another book and told her to open it at random and read to me the first four words at the top of the left.hand page. She read, May l tell him.\" This was the guide to the astral KEY. As tho first word had an odd mum. ber of letters l made a single dot on piece of paper. The second word also had an odd number of letters, and l put another dot under the first one put two dots for the third word, as it had an even number of letters and single dot for | the last word. Then looked in column one and followed it horizontally till l came to a perpendicular column headed | by a combination of two single dots, a double dot and single dot. Where the | two columns crossed was the letter o. l looked at the table o, containing answers to ali the questions and there opposite | the same group of dots read this answer: Fortune shuns you at present. but will come in time\" \"Oh.h!\" sighed Dorothy Cheer up\" said Ames. That Is not | half bad\" Then in moment he added, | Let me try it,\" l gave the book to him. He studied the | questions and then, evidently with his | mind on the draft and the probability | that he would have to go TO france, ne | said, 81 choose the second question, Will | my undertakings be successful?'\" Following the same method as I EN-. | ployed in Dorothys case I found the an swer was: Remain here among your family and you will do WELL\" \"That means you won't have to leave the country\" said owen little contemptuously. Rubbish\" said Doctor McFabre. who had risen from the hammock and was now sitting on the edge. \"You don't dare try it, Owen,\" said Ames, with fine show Of indignation. LET me have the book\" and Owen reached out his hand He looked over the questions. II am going to be frank about 1t. Question number four is Shall I live In foreign partsT That is what I want TO know\"","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"a5ff6e32-5885-42b8-8aec-6288db77d021","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","root":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","branch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":363,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":4},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","platform":"macos"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3de15aaf-6049-4e5c-9d2b-4fb31455b095","permission