"req_011CgYnKQmx2UCrv76HT8g2u","requestRef":"a26b9ef3-500a-42a9-9956-50a8c274125f","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"59a3a1f8-3664-47bd-a068-39bda3d95731","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:02:39.018Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"59a3a1f8-3664-47bd-a068-39bda3d95731","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4acd64b9-626e-4904-824b-5a9167cf4217","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HkdEaSTdEkWmeJgaTqfidP","type":"tool_result","content":"##### 1-024 | 1870-04-05 | Gold Hill daily news. [volume] (Gold Hill, N.T. [Nev.]) 1863-1882 | terms ['spirit_phrase']\nHEADLINE: \nThe FEAST OF THE DEAp.-The Chi nese Yesterday commenced their an nnal Feast of the Dead, which Is celebrated In the spring of the year and kept ND for several days. Throughout the day the laboring Chinese procured all sorts Of conveyances, which were loaded with roast pigs, fruits, pastry, ETC., and taken to the Cemetery. Here all the d~elicacies are spread over the graves Of departed friends, and around these they all kneel and bow a number of times. Friends about there are then Invited to partake with them of the refreshments, and a jovial time Is had, after which they return to town. Sum day is generally chosen by the laboring classes, and to-day the Weather repair to the Cemetery and 60 through the same ceremonies. Many Celestials from the interior come ON this occasion to the city to pay a friendly visit to deceased friends, their belief being that the spirit Of the dead fingers about the grave, particularly during this season Of the year. This Is also q reason given why their bones are returned to China, that relatives there may have an opportunity to pay this annual sac rifice over their remains.-S. F.. Ahd. \n\n##### 3-035 | 1884-04-22 | The Evening critic. | terms ['spirit_phrase']\nHEADLINE: A Charm That Failed.\nThe suit of prominent young railroad man In Savannah, Ga, against female NEGRO mancer for obtaining money under false pretenfes, Is exciting much interest In that city. The young man, who goes In tho best society, had long been paying fruitless attention to one of the beet known young ladies OF the city. TO produce love between discordant hearts, no consulted her. She agreed for Sl6 TO mako the young lady love him, the money to be paid signs OF success. He paid the first instalment, which was to set the spirits TO work. They wouldn't work at that price apparently, and to put the spirit world In motion again. The spirits then struck for the whole amount, and the young lady's heart was as hard as ever. The young man awoke to the folly OF his course and his threatened suit against the sorceress agitates society because IL would brlnG.llts name OF the young lady into unpleaspnsphb' licity. A. ~ \n\n##### 1-040 | 1902-04-17 | The Washburn times. [volume] (Washburn, Wis.) 1896-1976 | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: Hobbies Lead to Madness.\nThat well nigh every hobby can, If overridden, bear its rider toward insanity is proved by the lamentaBILE number of cases of people who have succumbed to the fatal infuence of a too keenly fixed leading idea. Enjoyed in reason, a hobby is the best safeguard imaginable against mental i'll health, but when followed up with undue persistence it only too often acts as a short route to the asylum. ] Witness, for instance, the case of the brilliant French novelist, Guy de Maupassant. This famous writer toward middle life took up merely as hobby the study Of occult science and spiritualistic phenomena. Grad ually the absorbing nature Of the weird research fascinated him so deeply that he could not speak, Think or dream Of aught else. Sleep deserted him, appetite failed him, and finally his actions became SO cratic that for the sake Of his own safety his friends were compelled to place the unfortunate author under mild restraint. He ended his days short \" afterward within the walls of the sanitarium to which he had been convened \n\n##### 3-040 | 1904-02-27 | Evening times-Republican. [volume] (Marshalltown, Iowa) 1890-1923 | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: odlsts, but as splritualists. They died / in the religion they had always pre-\nOdiSt5, but as Splrltuallssts. Ii'-y oio\" in the religion they had always prefer,ed.' The Christian Advocate. is seems in a former, issue has made similar statement, or, in effect that the FOX girls died spiritualists. Rev. Green says in his letter to the advocate; The late Mrs. Marie Fox Smith, was. al the time of her death, and for many years had been. a worthy member of the Methodist Episcopal church in East Palmyra, n. Y. into which she. with her honored husband, Mr.. Stephen Smith, now for some years in heaven was received by the writer In 800 She was q devout Christian and had nc part in or sympathy with spiritualism and never had. so far as the writer III intimate acquaintance with herself and Ifamily and with the community in whicf she spent her life could learn. She dlee in feebleness extreme but in the hope of the glorious gospel of our Lord Jesus | Christ\" \n\n##### 1-026 | 1906-07-28 | Imperial Valley press and the Imperial press. | terms ['spirit_phrase']\nHEADLINE: SCOTCH SUPERSTiTloNS. /  / Curious Methods That Were Adepted /  /  In Sickness and Death.\nA method much in vogue in Scotland at one time of ascertaining whether a sickness would prove fatal was to dig two holes in the ground, one called the quick grave, the other the dead hole. The sufferer was then placed between the two, and the hole toward which he turned indicated what would be the outcome of his malady. Sometimes a piece of rock was broken over the head of a person whose last agonies were painful alike to himself and to those who witnessed them It was believed that the heart of the sick man would thus be broken and his release hasten ed Windows and doors are always thrown wide open in order that the de from the house and escape from the evil ones that hover around eager to inthrall his soul. During the interval between death and burial hens and cats were kept carefully shut up. A person meeting these animals at such a juncture was doomed to blindness in the future. Moreover, unless a stream divided the two houses, farmers frequently refrain ed from yoking their oxen or horses be- fore the body was '1aid under the turf of truth Many women preserved, with the greatest reverence, their bridal attire to cover them in the cof- fin. Bread and water were placed in the chamber of death, for during the night prior to the burial the spirit of the departed one came to partake of them. Stillborn children and little ones who had not been blessed by the min ister were buried before sunrise In this way their admission to the land of promise was assured. Not to observe the practice was to destine the souls of these bairns to wander homeless and disconsolate. The fate of the suicide is lamentable. His body cannot rest in the kirkyard, for it would taint the souls of those who lie therein. Frequently he was buried in lone dike which separated two lairds' estates, and passersby were expected to cast pebble at the rude stone which marked the place. \n\n##### 3-049 | 1907-02-23 | The daily silver state. [volume] (Winnemucca, Nev.) 1903-1907 | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: \nOutside Of those who believe ID W. dence satisfa Glory to themselves thai they hold communion with the depart ed, there are many persons with no settled convictions on such subjects or altogether skeptical about them who are interested In the investiga' lions being made by the Society OF Psychical Research, which have been carried on for some years now BY scientists Of standing. The fact Of tclepathic communication seems to have been established beyond Ques ton. There are many cases OF per sons who at the time Of death have made friends at a distance aware OF their situation, and since wireless toBiography has come into use such oc' currences seem explainable On the same principle. The brain Is a dyna mo and may start q vibration, which another brain In close sympathy can receive and interpret. Some weeks since Professor Hodg. son. a prominent member Of the soct' ETY.. who did not believe In spiritual ism, during his last Illness promised some Of his fellow members that II b. found he survived physical death he would do his utmost to communicate with them. and they now claim that be has done 50. They say they SAL fol hours In a state Of expectancy, with their attention concentrated on him. Under such circumstances they might well believe impressions came from him which were, ~ q matter Of, fact subjective, and the opponents Of the spiritualistic theories say nc communication has ever been received which might not have been in the mind Of some living person. Such inquiries followed In a spirit Of candor along scientific lines can surely do no harm and are likely to widen the horizon OF human knowledge. \n\n##### 1-045 | 1922-03-14 | Evening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972 | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: CONCENTRATION, $500 / and ADVICE FAIL TO /  /  / PRODUCE A HUSBAND\nBY the Associated Press. NEW YORK. March 14.-Telepadvice purchased at a cost Of $500, failed to win a husband for Mrs. Lena Dunkley, head of the Brook Ign Truant School, she testified at the trial of Mrs. Anna War wick. leader of the Mayfiower Spiritualistic Church. of Jamaica. N. Y, who was in county court charged with grand larceny. For $500. said Mrs. Dunkley. she received. from Mrs. Warwick a photograph of a supposedly eligiBILE male and instructions to concentrate on the likeness. with the assurance that the subject would hand over his heart and hand. She received neither. she testified. Mrs. Warwick's defense was that the $500 was a mere loan and had nothing to do with love. A jury will decide the issue. \n\n##### 3-007 | 1924-03-06 | The Birmingham age-herald. | terms ['seance', 'spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: \nHoudini, the great magician and in- ternational character, comes to Bir- mingham Friday, where he will give three entertainments at the Masonic auditorium, under the auspices of the drill crops of Cyrene Commandery, Knights Templars, one on Friday and Saturday nights each, with matinee Saturday. Harry Houdini is internationally famous as q mystificr. He has baffled the public, the shrewdest police and the leading scientists. through his un- canny ability in realeasing himself from all the physical restraints that human ingenuity could devise. He has long been a puzzle to foremost investi- gators of physic phenomena. Now he is to appear in his great senational expose lecture: Fraud Mediums and Miracle Mongcrs.' He has always been intensely interested in spiritualistic and phychic phenomena. He has personally known most of the leading spiritualists of the last quarter of a century-and it is strange fact that they have been intensely interest. ed in Houdini. He was professional medium for several years and he has attended thousands of seances in this","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"2d9ba0f6-9c5a-4479-a74b-4b3f1a96401b","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:02:41.667Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"##### 1-024 | 1870-04-05 | Gold Hill daily news. [volume] (Gold Hill, N.T. [Nev.]) 1863-1882 | terms ['spirit_phrase']\nHEADLINE: \nThe FEAST OF THE DEAp.-The Chi nese Yesterday commenced their an nnal Feast of the Dead, which Is celebrated In the spring of the year and kept ND for several days. Throughout the day the laboring Chinese procured all sorts Of conveyances, which were loaded with roast pigs, fruits, pastry, ETC., and taken to the Cemetery. Here all the d~elicacies are spread over the graves Of departed friends, and around these they all kneel and bow a number of times. Friends about there are then Invited to partake with them of the refreshments, and a jovial time Is had, after which they return to town. Sum day is generally chosen by the laboring classes, and to-day the Weather repair to the Cemetery and 60 through the same ceremonies. Many Celestials from the interior come ON this occasion to the city to pay a friendly visit to deceased friends, their belief being that the spirit Of the dead fingers about the grave, particularly during this season Of the year. This Is also q reason given why their bones are returned to China, that relatives there may have an opportunity to pay this annual sac rifice over their remains.-S. F.. Ahd. \n\n##### 3-035 | 1884-04-22 | The Evening critic. | terms ['spirit_phrase']\nHEADLINE: A Charm That Failed.\nThe suit of prominent young railroad man In Savannah, Ga, against female NEGRO mancer for obtaining money under false pretenfes, Is exciting much interest In that city. The young man, who goes In tho best society, had long been paying fruitless attention to one of the beet known young ladies OF the city. TO produce love between discordant hearts, no consulted her. She agreed for Sl6 TO mako the young lady love him, the money to be paid signs OF success. He paid the first instalment, which was to set the spirits TO work. They wouldn't work at that price apparently, and to put the spirit world In motion again. The spirits then struck for the whole amount, and the young lady's heart was as hard as ever. The young man awoke to the folly OF his course and his threatened suit against the sorceress agitates society because IL would brlnG.llts name OF the young lady into unpleaspnsphb' licity. A. ~ \n\n##### 1-040 | 1902-04-17 | The Washburn times. [volume] (Washburn, Wis.) 1896-1976 | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: Hobbies Lead to Madness.\nThat well nigh every hobby can, If overridden, bear its rider toward insanity is proved by the lamentaBILE number of cases of people who have succumbed to the fatal infuence of a too keenly fixed leading idea. Enjoyed in reason, a hobby is the best safeguard imaginable against mental i'll health, but when followed up with undue persistence it only too often acts as a short route to the asylum. ] Witness, for instance, the case of the brilliant French novelist, Guy de Maupassant. This famous writer toward middle life took up merely as hobby the study Of occult science and spiritualistic phenomena. Grad ually the absorbing nature Of the weird research fascinated him so deeply that he could not spe