he head of a person whose last agonies were painful alike to himself and to those who witnessed them It was believed that the heart of the sick man would thus be broken and his release hasten ed Windows and doors are always thrown wide open in order that the de from the house and escape from the evil ones that hover around eager to inthrall his soul. During the interval between death and burial hens and cats were kept carefully shut up. A person meeting these animals at such a juncture was doomed to blindness in the future. Moreover, unless a stream divided the two houses, farmers frequently refrain ed from yoking their oxen or horses be- fore the body was '1aid under the turf of truth Many women preserved, with the greatest reverence, their bridal attire to cover them in the cof- fin. Bread and water were placed in the chamber of death, for during the night prior to the burial the spirit of the departed one came to partake of them. Stillborn children and little ones who had not been blessed by the min ister were buried before sunrise In this way their admission to the land of promise was assured. Not to observe the practice was to destine the souls of these bairns to wander homeless and disconsolate. The fate of the suicide is lamentable. His body cannot rest in the kirkyard, for it would taint the souls of those who lie therein. Frequently he was buried in lone dike which separated two lairds' estates, and passersby were expected to cast pebble at the rude stone which marked the place. \n\n##### 3-049 | 1907-02-23 | The daily silver state. [volume] (Winnemucca, Nev.) 1903-1907 | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: \nOutside Of those who believe ID W. dence satisfa Glory to themselves thai they hold communion with the depart ed, there are many persons with no settled convictions on such subjects or altogether skeptical about them who are interested In the investiga' lions being made by the Society OF Psychical Research, which have been carried on for some years now BY scientists Of standing. The fact Of tclepathic communication seems to have been established beyond Ques ton. There are many cases OF per sons who at the time Of death have made friends at a distance aware OF their situation, and since wireless toBiography has come into use such oc' currences seem explainable On the same principle. The brain Is a dyna mo and may start q vibration, which another brain In close sympathy can receive and interpret. Some weeks since Professor Hodg. son. a prominent member Of the soct' ETY.. who did not believe In spiritual ism, during his last Illness promised some Of his fellow members that II b. found he survived physical death he would do his utmost to communicate with them. and they now claim that be has done 50. They say they SAL fol hours In a state Of expectancy, with their attention concentrated on him. Under such circumstances they might well believe impressions came from him which were, ~ q matter Of, fact subjective, and the opponents Of the spiritualistic theories say nc communication has ever been received which might not have been in the mind Of some living person. Such inquiries followed In a spirit Of candor along scientific lines can surely do no harm and are likely to widen the horizon OF human knowledge. \n\n##### 1-045 | 1922-03-14 | Evening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972 | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: CONCENTRATION, $500 / and ADVICE FAIL TO /  /  / PRODUCE A HUSBAND\nBY the Associated Press. NEW YORK. March 14.-Telepadvice purchased at a cost Of $500, failed to win a husband for Mrs. Lena Dunkley, head of the Brook Ign Truant School, she testified at the trial of Mrs. Anna War wick. leader of the Mayfiower Spiritualistic Church. of Jamaica. N. Y, who was in county court charged with grand larceny. For $500. said Mrs. Dunkley. she received. from Mrs. Warwick a photograph of a supposedly eligiBILE male and instructions to concentrate on the likeness. with the assurance that the subject would hand over his heart and hand. She received neither. she testified. Mrs. Warwick's defense was that the $500 was a mere loan and had nothing to do with love. A jury will decide the issue. \n\n##### 3-007 | 1924-03-06 | The Birmingham age-herald. | terms ['seance', 'spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: \nHoudini, the great magician and in- ternational character, comes to Bir- mingham Friday, where he will give three entertainments at the Masonic auditorium, under the auspices of the drill crops of Cyrene Commandery, Knights Templars, one on Friday and Saturday nights each, with matinee Saturday. Harry Houdini is internationally famous as q mystificr. He has baffled the public, the shrewdest police and the leading scientists. through his un- canny ability in realeasing himself from all the physical restraints that human ingenuity could devise. He has long been a puzzle to foremost investi- gators of physic phenomena. Now he is to appear in his great senational expose lecture: Fraud Mediums and Miracle Mongcrs.' He has always been intensely interested in spiritualistic and phychic phenomena. He has personally known most of the leading spiritualists of the last quarter of a century-and it is strange fact that they have been intensely interest. ed in Houdini. He was professional medium for several years and he has attended thousands of seances in this","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"58e93cee-394b-4213-b0a8-0d76b5ecf49e","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","root":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","branch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":363,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":4},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":