1.036Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"a4a23922-8c99-4639-8efd-2cc6521404e4","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4acd64b9-626e-4904-824b-5a9167cf4217","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TgSeM2oTQNdHZXVX7u7uZ8","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.8.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"a1fd68ab-84c7-44fe-b7ca-481259a0bc97","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:13:51.941Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.8.md","oldString":"- Staged-diff scans of `ca57e25`, `0101271` and `7c928e8`: no `.env` value name is hit, and the machine name appears 0 times.","newString":"- Diff scans of all four commits (`72ef622`, `ca57e25`, `0101271` and `7c928e8`): no `.env` value name is hit, and the machine name appears 0 times.","originalFile":"# Step 2A.8: DONE\n\nOn `phase-2a-lexicon-v2`, from `main` at `5ac2e1b`, pushed:\n- `72ef622`: lexicon v2 and its tests\n- `ca57e25`: eval A on either lexicon, and `scripts/lexicon_v2.py`\n- `0101271`: the re-read's counts and manifests, v2's EDA, `lexicon_v2.json` and `added.json`\n- `7c928e8`: decision 31, the STATUS block, and the 2B and 2C plan items\n\n**PR #3** into main: https://github.com/Hotragn/Paperghosts/pull/3. When it opened, no CI checks had registered yet. `make test` gives 176 passed, and `make lint` exits 0.\n\n## Your conditions\n\n### 1. Keep v1\n\n- v1's candidates and samples were moved, not deleted, to `data/archive/lexicon-v1/`, with copies of its counts and manifests. `data/*` keeps them out of git.\n- `make eval-v1` runs `eval_a.py` on the archive. It reproduced `eval_a.json` and all eight charts byte for byte, apart from `generated_at`. I ran it twice: during the re-read, and again after the last change to `eval_a.py`.\n- `eval_a.py` now:\n  - takes the lexicon and code version from the data it is given\n  - lists terms from the data, so v1's order is kept\n  - masks only that read's own terms\n  - runs the judged stages (composition, recall and screen) only for v1\n\n### 2. Each candidate records its rule\n\nEach candidate's `terms` names the rules that matched it. `spirit+medium` is recorded when both words are found anywhere in the article. v1's terms come first in the pattern, so where a v1 term and a new one match at the same place, v1's term is the one recorded.\n\n### 3. The re-read\n\n- All 180 years ran at four processes, 14:49–16:50Z (120.6 min), for 340.4 GB.\n  - Every year finished at the first attempt.\n  - Peak memory was 551 MB (1919).\n- Since the read started, I touched nothing the worker imports.\n- **One code version:** all 180 manifests show `2d6ff0faaf81`, and it matches `code_version()` on disk.\n- **v1 ⊆ v2:** v1 had 1,050,459 candidates and v2 has 1,236,122.\n  - None of v1's is lost.\n  - 185,663 are added, and none of those holds a v1 term.\n- **1850 reproduces:** I moved its candidates file aside and ran `make ingest YEARS=1850`. All three outputs were byte-identical (`cmp` and sha256), with the sha256 the manifest records.\n  - Only the manifest's timestamps, seconds and peak memory differed.\n  - I put back the first run's manifest, so all 180 come from one run.\n- **Disk:** 4.1 GB in all, within the 10 GB budget, and 36 GiB are free.\n  - archive: 1.8 GiB\n  - v2's candidates: 1.5 GiB\n  - v2's samples: 563 MiB\n\n**Counts per rule.** \"Only it\" is what dropping that rule alone takes away.\n\n| Rule | Candidates | Added holding it | Added holding only it |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| spiritualism | 74,516 | 68,183 | 54,411 |\n| seance | 34,206 | 30,466 | 21,656 |\n| spirit_phrase | 42,186 | 35,695 | 24,537 |\n| spirit+medium | 79,374 | 72,856 | 57,676 |\n| ouija | 6,389 | 5,855 | 5,124 |\n\n### 4. The new rules, measured on fresh data\n\n- **The draw:** 200 added candidates at random, seed \"eval A v2 added\". It was split by the seed \"eval A v2 added split\" into 4 batches of 50 for reading agents, with the 2A.7 brief adapted to the new terms.\n- **First reading:** 169 no, 21 close calls and 10 reports.\n- **Blind check:** I re-judged a seeded 10% of their negatives (17, seed \"eval A v2 added check\") from the same text, without their notes. I agreed on all 17 (81.6–100%).\n- **Full reading:** I read every close call and report in full.\n  - All 10 reports stand.\n  - 3 close calls are reports and 18 are not.\n- **Beyond the 2A.7 protocol:** I also read in full the 8 negatives outside the blind check that the agents' notes called near. 1 of them is a report.\n  - In `added.json`, `rejudged` is \"sample\" for the blind check and \"full\" for an article read in full.\n  - Without this extra reading, the count is 13.\n- **14 of 200 are reports: 7.0% (4.2–11.4%), so v2 gains about 13,000 reports (7,830–21,200).** That is twice v1's 3.5%.\n- **Without the 2 borderline reports:** 12 of 200, 6.0% (3.5–10.2%), about 11,100 (6,430–18,900).\n\n| Rule | In the 200 | Reports | Rate (95%) | Reports among the added |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| spiritualism | 82 | 8 | 9.8% (5.0–18.1%) | ~6,650 (3,430–12,330) |\n| seance | 38 | 4 | 10.5% (4.2–24.1%) | ~3,210 (1,270–7,350) |\n| spirit_phrase | 37 | 4 | 10.8% (4.3–24.7%) | ~3,860 (1,530–8,820) |\n| spirit+medium | 67 | 3 | 4.5% (1.5–12.4%) | ~3,260 (1,120–9,000) |\n| ouija | 6 | **0** | 0% (0–39.0%) | at most 2,290 |\n\n- **ouija is flagged:** none of its 6 is a report. Dropping it is your call, and a filter would remove 5,124 candidates.\n- spirit+medium is the largest rule and has the lowest rate. Its 57 sampled articles that hold only it have 2 reports (3.5%), v1's rate.\n\n**The 14 reports:**\n- 1857, Middlebury: a spirit called Judd speaks through a trance medium.\n- **Borderline.** 1857: in the Picaud (\"real Monte Cristo\") narrative, printed as true, the dying Allut says he has \"seen and spoken with a soul separated from its body\". The reading agent called it a close call.\n- 1859, Plymouth: a spiritualist convention's president tells of the spirits of Washington and Lafayette in his presence.\n- 1869, Muskingum County: a family's spirit message about a $50,000 legacy.\n- 1873, Wilmington: a spiritualists' excursion whose circle materializes the dead.\n- 1875, Rutland: fourteen spirits materialize at the Eddys'.\n- 1895, Waterloo, Oregon: a speaker at a meeting is controlled by Sitting Bull's spirit, and a woman sees spirits in the audience. The reading agent called it a close call.\n- 1898, Sacramento: John Slater's test séance, with its ballot tests.\n- **Borderline.** 1907: an editorial on psychical research reports the claim that the dead Professor Hodgson has communicated, as he promised. The reading agent called it no, with a note saying it was near.\n- 1920, Trenton: a church officer says Lincoln's spirit predicts that Cox will be elected.\n- 1922: Conan Doyle says he has spoken with his dead son at séances.\n- 1923: a review of the records of Victor Hugo's table-turning séances.\n- 1923, Washington: Rev. G. Vale Owen lectures on messages from his dead parents and daughter. The reading agent called it a close call.\n- 1924, Chicago: a voice in a darkened spiritualist meeting.\n\n### 5. Recall, labelled in-sample\n\n- I read v1's screen of 38,900 again with v2. v2 makes 17 of the articles candidates, and they include 3 of its 4 reports: 1856, 1898 and 1919. The 1918 scout is still missed.\n- **Missed:** 1 in 38,883, so 0.0026% (0.00045–0.015%), about 9,500 (1,680–53,800) of 369,077,791.\n- **Recall:** v1's ~36,800 plus v2's ~13,000, over those and the missed, give **84%**.\n  - With the most misses the interval allows, it is **at least 48%**. Across the found reports' interval, that floor runs from 32% to 64%.\n  - Without the 2 borderline reports: 83%, and at least 47%.\n- **Caveat:** these figures are in-sample. 2C's eval B gives the independent figure. Decision 30's caveat stands too: the screen sees only reports that hold a flag word or a near-miss term.\n\n### 6. The EDA charts on v2\n\n- Six charts are in `eval/results/eval_a_v2/`: years, states, terms, lengths, OCR and reprints. `eval_a_v2.json` holds the numbers. I rendered each chart and checked it in light and dark.\n- The terms chart now lists the five rules by name.\n- v2's EDA:\n  - 3,338 candidates per million\n  - 6,695,792 near-miss articles\n  - 91,301 reprint clusters, holding 30.3% of candidates and leaving 952,591 distinct stories\n  - 32.0% of candidates with no batch path\n- The reel coverage is from scratch `reels.py`, as in decision 29. 93.0% of the no-batch-path candidates are on reels whose batch is known. That leaves 27,817 candidates on 1,258 reels for loc.gov, against v1's 25,595.\n\n### 7. Close-out\n\n- Decision 31, with a pointer at the end of decision 30.\n- The RESUME STATUS block, now \"Phase 2A, archive read, EDA and lexicon v2\". It has v2's numbers and the in-sample caveat. \"Next step\" points at 2B once PR #3 merges, with ouija as your call.\n- BUILD-PLAN:\n  - 2B: the reel map's counts are v2's (about 367,000 covered, 27,817 to look up).\n  - 2C `POLICY.md`: decision 31's borderline kinds.\n  - 2C eval B: it gives v2's independent recall.\n  - 2C full pass: 1,236,122 candidates (+17.7%), for the Tinker check.\n\n## Interpretation calls, yours to overrule\n\n- **First-person claims count.** A claim of communication with a named dead person counts, even in a lecture or an interview, like 2A.7's 1919 Curran report. This covers Vale Owen and Conan Doyle.\n- **Not counted:**\n  - jokes told as news: a 1873 \"shade\" questioned about a lawsuit, and 1872 automatic writing that names the Devil\n  - hypnotic \"second sight\" and a trance prophecy with no spirit\n  - Tennyson's mystical trance\n  - a medium's arrest, with nothing manifested\n- **The two borderline kinds**, a claim inside a narrative of doubtful truth and a particular claim inside a general essay, are now `POLICY.md` items.\n\n## Also\n\n- `make eval` takes 93 s and peaks at 4.7 GB. The judged part, `lexicon_v2.py` alone, takes 26 s.\n- Staged-diff scans of `ca57e25`, `0101271` and `7c928e8`: no `.env` value name is hit, and the machine name appears 0 times.\n- The Temporal dev server and worker for the read are stopped.\n- No loc.gov requests in this step. Nothing was spent.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":136,"oldLines":6,"newStart":136,"newLines":6,"lines":[" ## Also"," "," - `make eval` takes 93 s and peaks at 4.7 GB. The judged part, `lexicon_v2.py` alone, takes 26 s.","-- Staged-diff scans of `ca57e25`, `0101271` and `7c928e8`: no `.env` value name is hit, and the machine name appears 0 times.","+- Diff scans of all four commits (`72ef622`, `ca57e25`, `0101271` and `7c928e8`): no `.env` value name is hit, and the machine name appears 0 times."," - The Temporal dev server and worker for the read are stopped."," - No loc.gov requests in this step. Nothing was spent."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a4a23922-8c99-4639-8efd-2cc6521404e4","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
