{"parentUuid":"d7bd02e0-f1f5-4cc7-84cb-26138be2de0c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgYoRXP6jztQmUPkbbg3x","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Next, the hand-over note gets an addendum section with the marginal yields."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1955,"cache_read_input_tokens":336399,"output_tokens":1147,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":131},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1955,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1147,"cache_read_input_tokens":336399,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1955,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1955},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"cbfe02b6-32ce-4bda-97de-63e9acc1597c","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgYoRWVmVVFHsP7MWqAXR","requestRef":"98a20a7d-51b7-4236-babd-adba63a0495c","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"51efdac2-db27-49f4-a5ed-68b1e3392c96","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:17:07.120Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"51efdac2-db27-49f4-a5ed-68b1e3392c96","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgYoRXP6jztQmUPkbbg3x","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EDy1ZKHUtWFfZsbqH5gwhw","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.8.md","old_string":"## Your conditions\n\n### 1. Keep v1","new_string":"## Addendum: marginal yield\n\nYou asked for this after the PR opened. It counts the reports in the 200 that one rule catches and no other term catches, so it is what dropping that rule alone would lose.\n- It is in `lexicon_v2.json`, under `added.by_rule.<rule>.marginal` (over the 200) and `.only` (over the rule's sampled articles that hold no other term).\n- I wrote the test first and watched it fail. No rule was tuned or narrowed, and `added.json` and every other number in the report are unchanged.\n\n| Rule | Only it catches, of the 200 | Reports among the added that only it catches | Its sampled articles holding only it | Their rate (95%) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| spiritualism | 4: 2.0% (0.78–5.0%) | ~3,710 (1,450–9,340) | 64 | 6.3% (2.5–15.0%) of 54,411 |\n| seance | 2: 1.0% (0.27–3.6%) | ~1,860 (510–6,630) | 28 | 7.1% (2.0–22.6%) of 21,656 |\n| spirit_phrase | 2: 1.0% (0.27–3.6%) | ~1,860 (510–6,630) | 24 | 8.3% (2.3–25.9%) of 24,537 |\n| spirit+medium | 2: 1.0% (0.27–3.6%) | ~1,860 (510–6,630) | 57 | 3.5% (0.97–11.9%) of 57,676 |\n| ouija | 0: 0% (0–1.9%) | at most 3,500 | 3 | 0% (0–56.2%) of 5,124 |\n\n- 10 of the 14 reports hold a single rule. Each of the other 4 holds two or more: 1875 Eddys, 1922 Conan Doyle, 1923 Vale Owen and 1924 Chicago.\n- **spirit+medium:** unlike in 2A.7's screen, it alone catches 2 reports here, 1857 Middlebury and 1859 Plymouth. Its candidates holding only it are reports at 3.5%, v1's own rate, and they are the most numerous: 57,676.\n- The borderline reports are each caught by a single rule: Allut by spirit_phrase and Hodgson by spiritualism. Without them, spirit_phrase alone catches 1 and spiritualism alone catches 3.\n\n## Your conditions\n\n### 1. Keep v1"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1955,"cache_read_input_tokens":336399,"output_tokens":1147,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":131},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1955,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1147,"cache_read_input_tokens":336399,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1955,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1955},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01EDy1ZKHUtWFfZsbqH5gwhw":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.8.md","old_string":"## Your conditions\n\n### 1. Keep v1","new_string":"## Addendum: marginal yield\n\nYou asked for this after the PR opened. It counts the reports in the 200 that one rule catches and no other term catches, so it is what dropping that rule alone would lose.\n- It is in `lexicon_v2.json`, under `added.by_rule.<rule>.marginal` (over the 200) and `.only` (over the rule's sampled articles that hold no other term).\n- I wrote the test first and watched it fail. No rule was tuned or narrowed, and `added.json` and every other number in the report are unchanged.\n\n| Rule | Only it catches, of the 200 | Reports among the added that only it catches | Its sampled articles holding only it | Their rate (95%) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| spiritualism | 4: 2.0% (0.78–5.0%) | ~3,710 (1,450–9,340) | 64 | 6.3% (2.5–15.0%) of 54,411 |\n| seance | 2: 1.0% (0.27–3.6%) | ~1,860 (510–6,630) | 28 | 7.1% (2.0–22.6%) of 21,656 |\n| spirit_phrase | 2: 1.0% (0.27–3.6%) | ~1,860 (510–6,630) | 24 | 8.3% (2.3–25.9%) of 24,537 |\n| spirit+medium | 2: 1.0% (0.27–3.6%) | ~1,860 (510–6,630) | 57 | 3.5% (0.97–11.9%) of 57,676 |\n| ouija | 0: 0% (0–1.9%) | at most 3,500 | 3 | 0% (0–56.2%) of 5,124 |\n\n- 10 of the 14 reports hold a single rule. Each of the other 4 holds two or more: 1875 Eddys, 1922 Conan Doyle, 1923 Vale Owen and 1924 Chicago.\n- **spirit+medium:** unlike in 2A.7's screen, it alone catches 2 reports here, 1857 Middlebury and 1859 Plymouth. Its candidates holding only it are reports at 3.5%, v1's own rate, and they are the most numerous: 57,676.\n- The borderline reports are each caught by a single rule: Allut by spirit_phrase and Hodgson by spiritualism. Without them, spirit_phrase alone catches 1 and spiritualism alone catches 3.\n\n## Your conditions\n\n### 1. Keep v1"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"cbfe02b6-32ce-4bda-97de-63e9acc1597c","apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CgYoRWVmVVFHsP7MWqAXR","requestRef":"98a20a7d-51b7-4236-babd-adba63a0495c","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"b107b3e5-9d1c-4832-80e7-1d63f5d1f446","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:17:14.408Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"b107b3e5-9d1c-4832-80e7-1d63f5d1f446","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"53915d29-3b9f-4873-b1be-8f20126b960f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EDy1ZKHUtWFfZsbqH5gwhw","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.8.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"4ea2c824-bddb-4eec-9f3f-380d21c84d08","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:17:14.432Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.8.md","oldString":"## Your conditions\n\n### 1. Keep v1","newString":"## Addendum: marginal yield\n\nYou asked for this after the PR opened. It counts the reports in the 200 that one rule catches and no other term catches, so it is what dropping that rule alone would lose.\n- It is in `lexicon_v2.json`, under `added.by_rule.<rule>.marginal` (over the 200) and `.only` (over the rule's sampled articles that hold no other term).\n- I wrote the test first and watched it fail. No rule was tuned or narrowed, and `added.json` and every other number in the report are unchanged.\n\n| Rule | Only it catches, of the 200 | Reports among the added that only it catches | Its sampled articles holding only it | Their rate (95%) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| spiritualism | 4: 2.0% (0.78–5.0%) | ~3,710 (1,450–9,340) | 64 | 6.3% (2.5–15.0%) of 54,411 |\n| seance | 2: 1.0% (0.27–3.6%) | ~1,860 (510–6,630) | 28 | 7.1% (2.0–22.6%) of 21,656 |\n| spirit_phrase | 2: 1.0% (0.27–3.6%) | ~1,860 (510–6,630) | 24 | 8.3% (2.3–25.9%) of 24,537 |\n| spirit+medium | 2: 1.0% (0.27–3.6%) | ~1,860 (510–6,630) | 57 | 3.5% (0.97–11.9%) of 57,676 |\n| ouija | 0: 0% (0–1.9%) | at most 3,500 | 3 | 0% (0–56.2%) of 5,124 |\n\n- 10 of the 14 reports hold a single rule. Each of the other 4 holds two or more: 1875 Eddys, 1922 Conan Doyle, 1923 Vale Owen and 1924 Chicago.\n- **spirit+medium:** unlike in 2A.7's screen, it alone catches 2 reports here, 1857 Middlebury and 1859 Plymouth. Its candidates holding only it are reports at 3.5%, v1's own rate, and they are the most numerous: 57,676.\n- The borderline reports are each caught by a single rule: Allut by spirit_phrase and Hodgson by spiritualism. Without them, spirit_phrase alone catches 1 and spiritualism alone catches 3.\n\n## Your conditions\n\n### 1. Keep v1","originalFile":"# Step 2A.8: DONE\n\nOn `phase-2a-lexicon-v2`, from `main` at `5ac2e1b`, pushed:\n- `72ef622`: lexicon v2 and its tests\n- `ca57e25`: eval A on either lexicon, and `scripts/lexicon_v2.py`\n- `0101271`: the re-read's counts and manifests, v2's EDA, `lexicon_v2.json` and `added.json`\n- `7c928e8`: decision 31, the STATUS block, and the 2B and 2C plan items\n\n**PR #3** into main: https://github.com/Hotragn/Paperghosts/pull/3. When it opened, no CI checks had registered yet. `make test` gives 176 passed, and `make lint` exits 0.\n\n## Your conditions\n\n### 1. Keep v1\n\n- v1's candidates and samples were moved, not deleted, to `data/archive/lexicon-v1/`, with copies of its counts and manifests. `data/*` keeps them out of git.\n- `make eval-v1` runs `eval_a.py` on the archive. It reproduced `eval_a.json` and all eight charts byte for byte, apart from `generated_at`. I ran it twice: during the re-read, and again after the last change to `eval_a.py`.\n- `eval_a.py` now:\n  - takes the lexicon and code version from the data it is given\n  - lists terms from the data, so v1's order is kept\n  - masks only that read's own terms\n  - runs the judged stages (composition, recall and screen) only for v1\n\n### 2. Each candidate records its rule\n\nEach candidate's `terms` names the rules that matched it. `spirit+medium` is recorded when both words are found anywhere in the article. v1's terms come first in the pattern, so where a v1 term and a new one match at the same place, v1's term is the one recorded.\n\n### 3. The re-read\n\n- All 180 years ran at four processes, 14:49–16:50Z (120.6 min), for 340.4 GB.\n  - Every year finished at the first attempt.\n  - Peak memory was 551 MB (1919).\n- Since the read started, I touched nothing the worker imports.\n- **One code version:** all 180 manifests show `2d6ff0faaf81`, and it matches `code_version()` on disk.\n- **v1 ⊆ v2:** v1 had 1,050,459 candidates and v2 has 1,236,122.\n  - None of v1's is lost.\n  - 185,663 are added, and none of those holds a v1 term.\n- **1850 reproduces:** I moved its candidates file aside and ran `make ingest YEARS=1850`. All three outputs were byte-identical (`cmp` and sha256), with the sha256 the manifest records.\n  - Only the manifest's timestamps, seconds and peak memory differed.\n  - I put back the first run's manifest, so all 180 come from one run.\n- **Disk:** 4.1 GB in all, within the 10 GB budget, and 36 GiB are free.\n  - archive: 1.8 GiB\n  - v2's candidates: 1.5 GiB\n  - v2's samples: 563 MiB\n\n**Counts per rule.** \"Only it\" is what dropping that rule alone takes away.\n\n| Rule | Candidates | Added holding it | Added holding only it |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| spiritualism | 74,516 | 68,183 | 54,411 |\n| seance | 34,206 | 30,466 | 21,656 |\n| spirit_phrase | 42,186 | 35,695 | 24,537 |\n| spirit+medium | 79,374 | 72,856 | 57,676 |\n| ouija | 6,389 | 5,855 | 5,124 |\n\n### 4. The new rules, measured on fresh data\n\n- **The draw:** 200 added candidates at random, seed \"eval A v2 added\". It was split by the seed \"eval A v2 added split\" into 4 batches of 50 for reading agents, with the 2A.7 brief adapted to the new terms.\n- **First reading:** 169 no, 21 close calls and 10 reports.\n- **Blind check:** I re-judged a seeded 10% of their negatives (17, seed \"eval A v2 added check\") from the same text, without their notes. I agreed on all 17 (81.6–100%).\n- **Full reading:** I read every close call and report in full.\n  - All 10 reports stand.\n  - 3 close calls are reports and 18 are not.\n- **Beyond the 2A.7 protocol:** I also read in full the 8 negatives outside the blind check that the agents' notes called near. 1 of them is a report.\n  - In `added.json`, `rejudged` is \"sample\" for the blind check and \"full\" for an article read in full.\n  - Without this extra reading, the count is 13.\n- **14 of 200 are reports: 7.0% (4.2–11.4%), so v2 gains about 13,000 reports (7,830–21,200).** That is twice v1's 3.5%.\n- **Without the 2 borderline reports:** 12 of 200, 6.0% (3.5–10.2%), about 11,100 (6,430–18,900).\n\n| Rule | In the 200 | Reports | Rate (95%) | Reports among the added |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| spiritualism | 82 | 8 | 9.8% (5.0–18.1%) | ~6,650 (3,430–12,330) |\n| seance | 38 | 4 | 10.5% (4.2–24.1%) | ~3,210 (1,270–7,350) |\n| spirit_phrase | 37 | 4 | 10.8% (4.3–24.7%) | ~3,860 (1,530–8,820) |\n| spirit+medium | 67 | 3 | 4.5% (1.5–12.4%) | ~3,260 (1,120–9,000) |\n| ouija | 6 | **0** | 0% (0–39.0%) | at most 2,290 |\n\n- **ouija is flagged:** none of its 6 is a report. Dropping it is your call, and a filter would remove 5,124 candidates.\n- spirit+medium is the largest rule and has the lowest rate. Its 57 sampled articles that hold only it have 2 reports (3.5%), v1's rate.\n\n**The 14 reports:**\n- 1857, Middlebury: a spirit called Judd speaks through a trance medium.\n- **Borderline.** 1857: in the Picaud (\"real Monte Cristo\") narrative, printed as true, the dying Allut says he has \"seen and spoken with a soul separated from its body\". The reading agent called it a close call.\n- 1859, Plymouth: a spiritualist convention's president tells of the spirits of Washington and Lafayette in his presence.\n- 1869, Muskingum County: a family's spirit message about a $50,000 legacy.\n- 1873, Wilmington: a spiritualists' excursion whose circle materializes the dead.\n- 1875, Rutland: fourteen spirits materialize at the Eddys'.\n- 1895, Waterloo, Oregon: a speaker at a meeting is controlled by Sitting Bull's spirit, and a woman sees spirits in the audience. The reading agent called it a close call.\n- 1898, Sacramento: John Slater's test séance, with its ballot tests.\n- **Borderline.** 1907: an editorial on psychical research reports the claim that the dead Professor Hodgson has communicated, as he promised. The reading agent called it no, with a note saying it was near.\n- 1920, Trenton: a church officer says Lincoln's spirit predicts that Cox will be elected.\n- 1922: Conan Doyle says he has spoken with his dead son at séances.\n- 1923: a review of the records of Victor Hugo's table-turning séances.\n- 1923, Washington: Rev. G. Vale Owen lectures on messages from his dead parents and daughter. The reading agent called it a close call.\n- 1924, Chicago: a voice in a darkened spiritualist meeting.\n\n### 5. Recall, labelled in-sample\n\n- I read v1's screen of 38,900 again with v2. v2 makes 17 of the articles candidates, and they include 3 of its 4 reports: 1856, 1898 and 1919. The 1918 scout is still missed.\n- **Missed:** 1 in 38,883, so 0.0026% (0.00045–0.015%), about 9,500 (1,680–53,800) of 369,077,791.\n- **Recall:** v1's ~36,800 plus v2's ~13,000, over those and the missed, give **84%**.\n  - With the most misses the interval allows, it is **at least 48%**. Across the found reports' interval, that floor runs from 32% to 64%.\n  - Without the 2 borderline reports: 83%, and at least 47%.\n- **Caveat:** these figures are in-sample. 2C's eval B gives the independent figure. Decision 30's caveat stands too: the screen sees only reports that hold a flag word or a near-miss term.\n\n### 6. The EDA charts on v2\n\n- Six charts are in `eval/results/eval_a_v2/`: years, states, terms, lengths, OCR and reprints. `eval_a_v2.json` holds the numbers. I rendered each chart and checked it in light and dark.\n- The terms chart now lists the five rules by name.\n- v2's EDA:\n  - 3,338 candidates per million\n  - 6,695,792 near-miss articles\n  - 91,301 reprint clusters, holding 30.3% of candidates and leaving 952,591 distinct stories\n  - 32.0% of candidates with no batch path\n- The reel coverage is from scratch `reels.py`, as in decision 29. 93.0% of the no-batch-path candidates are on reels whose batch is known. That leaves 27,817 candidates on 1,258 reels for loc.gov, against v1's 25,595.\n\n### 7. Close-out\n\n- Decision 31, with a pointer at the end of decision 30.\n- The RESUME STATUS block, now \"Phase 2A, archive read, EDA and lexicon v2\". It has v2's numbers and the in-sample caveat. \"Next step\" points at 2B once PR #3 merges, with ouija as your call.\n- BUILD-PLAN:\n  - 2B: the reel map's counts are v2's (about 367,000 covered, 27,817 to look up).\n  - 2C `POLICY.md`: decision 31's borderline kinds.\n  - 2C eval B: it gives v2's independent recall.\n  - 2C full pass: 1,236,122 candidates (+17.7%), for the Tinker check.\n\n## Interpretation calls, yours to overrule\n\n- **First-person claims count.** A claim of communication with a named dead person counts, even in a lecture or an interview, like 2A.7's 1919 Curran report. This covers Vale Owen and Conan Doyle.\n- **Not counted:**\n  - jokes told as news: a 1873 \"shade\" questioned about a lawsuit, and 1872 automatic writing that names the Devil\n  - hypnotic \"second sight\" and a trance prophecy with no spirit\n  - Tennyson's mystical trance\n  - a medium's arrest, with nothing manifested\n- **The two borderline kinds**, a claim inside a narrative of doubtful truth and a particular claim inside a general essay, are now `POLICY.md` items.\n\n## Also\n\n- `make eval` takes 93 s and peaks at 4.7 GB. The judged part, `lexicon_v2.py` alone, takes 26 s.\n- Diff scans of all four commits (`72ef622`, `ca57e25`, `0101271` and `7c928e8`): no `.env` value name is hit, and the machine name appears 0 times.\n- The Temporal dev server and worker for the read are stopped.\n- No loc.gov requests in this step. Nothing was spent.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":8,"oldLines":6,"newStart":8,"newLines":24,"lines":[" "," **PR #3** into main: https://github.com/Hotragn/Paperghosts/pull/3. When it opened, no CI checks had registered yet. `make test` gives 176 passed, and `make lint` exits 0."," ","+## Addendum: marginal yield","+","+You asked for this after the PR opened. It counts the reports in the 200 that one rule catches and no other term catches, so it is what dropping that rule alone would lose.","+- It is in `lexicon_v2.json`, under `added.by_rule.<rule>.marginal` (over the 200) and `.only` (over the rule's sampled articles that hold no other term).","+- I wrote the test first and watched it fail. No rule was tuned or narrowed, and `added.json` and every other number in the report are unchanged.","+","+| Rule | Only it catches, of the 200 | Reports among the added that only it catches | Its sampled articles holding only it | Their rate (95%) |","+|---|---|---|---|---|","+| spiritualism | 4: 2.0% (0.78–5.0%) | ~3,710 (1,450–9,340) | 64 | 6.3% (2.5–15.0%) of 54,411 |","+| seance | 2: 1.0% (0.27–3.6%) | ~1,860 (510–6,630) | 28 | 7.1% (2.0–22.6%) of 21,656 |","+| spirit_phrase | 2: 1.0% (0.27–3.6%) | ~1,860 (510–6,630) | 24 | 8.3% (2.3–25.9%) of 24,537 |","+| spirit+medium | 2: 1.0% (0.27–3.6%) | ~1,860 (510–6,630) | 57 | 3.5% (0.97–11.9%) of 57,676 |","+| ouija | 0: 0% (0–1.9%) | at most 3,500 | 3 | 0% (0–56.2%) of 5,124 |","+","+- 10 of the 14 reports hold a single rule. Each of the other 4 holds two or more: 1875 Eddys, 1922 Conan Doyle, 1923 Vale Owen and 1924 Chicago.","+- **spirit+medium:** unlike in 2A.7's screen, it alone catches 2 reports here, 1857 Middlebury and 1859 Plymouth. Its candidates holding only it are reports at 3.5%, v1's own rate, and they are the most numerous: 57,676.","+- The borderline reports are each caught by a single rule: Allut by spirit_phrase and Hodgson by spiritualism. Without them, spirit_phrase alone catches 1 and spiritualism alone catches 3.","+"," ## Your conditions"," "," ### 1. Keep v1"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b107b3e5-9d1c-4832-80e7-1d63f5d1f446","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","hash":"776fe83e4185fadbf02bdd58f022305da0c4573645746c2b9e16f104ab7eb4a2","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Next, the hand-over note gets an addendum section with the marginal yields."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EDy1ZKHUtWFfZsbqH5gwhw","name":"Edit","input":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.8.md","old_string":"## Your conditions\n\n### 1. Keep v1","new_string":"## Addendum: marginal yield\n\nYou asked for this after the PR opened. It counts the reports in the 200 that one rule catches and no other term catches, so it is what dropping that rule alone would lose.\n- It is in `lexicon_v2.json`, under `added.by_rule.<rule>.marginal` (over the 200) and `.only` (over the rule's sampled articles that hold no other term).\n- I wrote the test first and watched it fail. No rule was tuned or narrowed, and `added.json` and every other number in the report are unchanged.\n\n| Rule | Only it catches, of the 200 | Reports among the added that only it catches | Its sampled articles holding only it | Their rate (95%) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| spiritualism | 4: 2.0% (0.78–5.0%) | ~3,710 (1,450–9,340) | 64 | 6.3% (2.5–15.0%) of 54,411 |\n| seance | 2: 1.0% (0.27–3.6%) | ~1,860 (510–6,630) | 28 | 7.1% (2.0–22.6%) of 21,656 |\n| spirit_phrase | 2: 1.0% (0.27–3.6%) | ~1,860 (510–6,630) | 24 | 8.3% (2.3–25.9%) of 24,537 |\n| spirit+medium | 2: 1.0% (0.27–3.6%) | ~1,860 (510–6,630) | 57 | 3.5% (0.97–11.9%) of 57,676 |\n| ouija | 0: 0% (0–1.9%) | at most 3,500 | 3 | 0% (0–56.2%) of 5,124 |\n\n- 10 of the 14 reports hold a single rule. Each of the other 4 holds two or more: 1875 Eddys, 1922 Conan Doyle, 1923 Vale Owen and 1924 Chicago.\n- **spirit+medium:** unlike in 2A.7's screen, it alone catches 2 reports here, 1857 Middlebury and 1859 Plymouth. Its candidates holding only it are reports at 3.5%, v1's own rate, and they are the most numerous: 57,676.\n- The borderline reports are each caught by a single rule: Allut by spirit_phrase and Hodgson by spiritualism. Without them, spirit_phrase alone catches 1 and spiritualism alone catches 3.\n\n## Your conditions\n\n### 1. Keep v1"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
